window.KAIZEN_FALLBACK = {"courses":[{"id":"nicl-ao-2026","meta":{"id":"nicl-ao-2026","title":"NICL AO 2026","tagline":"Administrative Officer • Prelims 17 Nov → Mains 22 Dec","accent":"#4f46e5","exam":{"label":"Prelims • 17 November 2026","date":"2026-11-17T09:00:00","sub":"Mains 22 Dec 2026"},"dailyGoal":20,"tracks":[{"id":"prelims","title":"Prelims","desc":"English 30 • Reasoning 35 • Quant 35 • 60 min"},{"id":"mains","title":"Mains","desc":"GA • Computer Knowledge • Technical IT • Descriptive"}],"files":{"decks":"decks.json","quizzes":"quizzes.json","videos":"videos.json","guides":"guides.json","plan":"plan.md","resources":"resources.md"}},"decks":[{"id":"english","title":"English","desc":"RC • error detection • fillers • vocab (Prelims 30Q + Mains 40/50Q)","track":"prelims","cards":[{"q":"Yesterday I met my friend who is now a doctor and ___ musician. Find the error.","options":["Yesterday I met","my friend who","is now a doctor","and a musician"],"answer":3,"explain":"Rule: when two nouns describe the SAME person and are joined by 'and', the article comes only before the first noun. Here the friend is one person who is both doctor and musician, so the second 'a' is wrong. Correct sentence: '...is now a doctor and musician.' If they were two different people, both articles would stay.","tag":"article"},{"q":"She ___ in hilly areas since her childhood.","options":["lives","has been living","lived","will live"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Since + a starting point in the past' (her childhood) with an action continuing to now always needs the present perfect continuous: has/have + been + verb-ing. So 'has been living'. Simple present 'lives' only suits habits with no 'since' clause, e.g. 'She lives in Shimla.'","tag":"tense"},{"q":"Choose the correct sentence.","options":["Despite of several disputes, they stayed.","Despite several disputes, they stayed.","Despite of the fact they stayed.","Although of disputes they stayed."],"answer":1,"explain":"'Despite' is already a complete preposition and never takes 'of' — the error 'despite of' is a classic trap. Its cousin 'in spite' DOES need 'of' ('in spite of disputes'). 'Although' is a conjunction, so 'although of' is never correct.","tag":"preposition"},{"q":"The university will conduct exams after COVID-19 cases ___.","options":["will decline","decline","declined","declining"],"answer":1,"explain":"After time conjunctions (after, when, until, if, as soon as), use the PRESENT tense even when the main clause is future. The main clause already carries the future ('will conduct'), so 'will decline' repeats it wrongly. Correct: '...after cases decline.'","tag":"tense"},{"q":"...a number of emerging ___.","options":["economy","economies","economical","economize"],"answer":1,"explain":"'A number of' always takes a PLURAL noun — it means 'several'. Contrast with 'the number of', which takes a singular verb ('The number of cases IS rising'). Also, only the noun 'economies' fits; 'economical' is an adjective.","tag":"grammar"},{"q":"Synonym of SUSTAINED (losses sustained)?","options":["persisted","affiliated","rendered","intermittent"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Sustained' here means suffered/endured over time. 'Persisted' (continued to exist) is the closest match. 'Affiliated' means attached to an organisation, 'rendered' means provided/gave, and 'intermittent' (on-and-off) is nearly an antonym.","tag":"vocab"},{"q":"Antonym of CONSTANT?","options":["fitful","perpetual","prevalent","entrust"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Constant' = steady, unchanging. 'Fitful' = irregular, starting and stopping — the direct opposite. Watch out: 'perpetual' (never-ending) is a SYNONYM of constant, and 'prevalent' means widespread, which is unrelated.","tag":"vocab"},{"q":"Spot the misspelt word: observatory / residents / strom / alerted","options":["observatory","residents","strom","alerted"],"answer":2,"explain":"'strom' is missing its 'o' — the word is STORM (s-t-o-r-m). The others are correct: observatory (a building for watching the sky), residents (people living somewhere), alerted (warned).","tag":"spelling"},{"q":"Spot the misspelt word: plosible / explanation / government","options":["plosible","explanation","government","No error"],"answer":0,"explain":"The word is PLAUSIBLE (p-l-a-u-s-i-b-l-e), meaning reasonable or believable. Remember it with 'applause': plau-sible. 'Explanation' and 'government' (note: govern + ment, no extra n) are spelt correctly.","tag":"spelling"},{"q":"Power plants with zero ___ stock.","options":["buffer","reserve","preserve","conserve"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Reserve stock' is the standard collocation — a reserve is something stored for emergencies. 'Buffer stock' exists too (for smoothing supply), but in the phrase 'zero ___ stock' for emergencies, 'reserve' is the expected answer. Preserve/conserve are verbs (protect/save), not nouns for stored goods.","tag":"collocation"},{"q":"Women spend hours ___ water.","options":["fetching","captivating","abandoning","conveying"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Fetching' means going somewhere and bringing something back — exactly what collecting water involves. 'Captivating' means fascinating people, 'abandoning' means leaving behind, 'conveying' means communicating ideas. Only fetching fits daily water collection.","tag":"vocab"},{"q":"Need a ___ policy framework.","options":["coherent","ludicrous","spurious","absurd"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Coherent' means logical, consistent and well-organised — what a policy framework must be. The other three all mean illogical in different ways: ludicrous (laughably absurd), spurious (false though seeming true), absurd (wildly unreasonable).","tag":"vocab"},{"q":"Neither of the answers ___ correct.","options":["are","is","were","have"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Neither of' is always singular — think of it as 'not one and not the other'. So it takes 'is', never 'are/were/have'. Same rule for 'either of' and 'each of': 'Each of the boys HAS a pen.'","tag":"sva"},{"q":"He was accused ___ theft.","options":["of","for","with","about"],"answer":0,"explain":"The fixed preposition with 'accused' is OF when naming the crime: 'accused of theft'. ('Accused for' or 'with' are common Indian-English errors.) Note the related pattern: 'charged WITH a crime' — accused→of, charged→with.","tag":"preposition"},{"q":"By next March, she ___ here for five years.","options":["works","will have been working","worked","is working"],"answer":1,"explain":"'By + a future time' signals the future perfect (continuous): will + have + been + verb-ing. It describes an action that will be in progress up to that future point. Simple 'works' or past 'worked' cannot express 'up to next March'.","tag":"tense"},{"q":"The letter ___ yesterday.","options":["posted","was posted","has posted","is posting"],"answer":1,"explain":"The letter RECEIVES the action (someone posted it), so we need passive voice: was/were + past participle → 'was posted'. 'Posted' alone is active and would need a subject doing it ('He posted the letter'). 'Yesterday' fixes the tense as simple past.","tag":"voice"},{"q":"He said, 'I am busy.' → He said that ___.","options":["he is busy","he was busy","he has busy","he be busy"],"answer":1,"explain":"In reported speech, present tense backshifts one step to past: 'am' → 'was'. Pronoun also shifts (I → he). 'Has busy' is ungrammatical (has needs a participle: has BEEN busy). Rule of thumb: present→past, past→past perfect.","tag":"speech"},{"q":"Choose correct: with ___ no effort.","options":["hardly no","hardly any","hardly some","hardly much"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Hardly' is already negative in meaning (= almost not), so adding 'no' makes a double negative. Pair hardly/scarcely/barely with affirmatives: 'hardly ANY effort'. Correct full sentence: '...with hardly any effort at all.'","tag":"grammar"},{"q":"The delay will ___ our plans.","options":["affect","effect","affects badly","effected"],"answer":0,"explain":"AFFECT is the verb (to influence); EFFECT is the noun (the result). Test: 'The delay will ___ our plans' needs a verb → affect. Memory hook: Affect=Action (verb), Effect=End result (noun).","tag":"confused"},{"q":"'To bite the dust' means?","options":["to eat quickly","to fail/be defeated","to work hard","to hide"],"answer":1,"explain":"Origin: fallen fighters face-down in dust. It means to fail, be defeated, or (for things) break down — e.g. 'Another startup bit the dust.' Nothing to do with eating.","tag":"idiom"},{"q":"The meeting was ___ off till Monday.","options":["put","called","laid","taken"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Put off' = postpone. Don't confuse with 'called off' (= cancelled entirely) — the meeting still happens Monday, so it's postponed, not cancelled. 'Laid off' is about firing staff.","tag":"phrasal"},{"q":"Which sentence usually OPENS a paragraph?","options":["One with 'he/they/this' referring back","A general introductory statement","A concluding remark","An example starting with 'for instance'"],"answer":1,"explain":"Openers introduce a fresh idea, so they avoid pronouns pointing backwards ('he', 'this') and avoid examples/conclusions that need prior context. In para-jumbles, first eliminate options starting with pronouns, 'but/however', and conclusions.","tag":"parajumble"},{"q":"Author's view on correct fasting (NICL 2024 RC)?","options":["Replaces all medication","Aids prevention and control","Only helps advanced cases","Has no scientific basis"],"answer":1,"explain":"The passage praises fasting's preventive potential (stable glucose, better metabolism) but explicitly says it 'may not replace medications in chronic cases'. So (b) is balanced and correct; (a) and (c) are the passage's own stated limits, and (d) contradicts the cited NUI research.","tag":"rc"},{"q":"Why does fasting help insulin sensitivity?","options":["Creates more glucose in liver","Body shifts to burning fat, easing pancreatic load","Reduces water intake","Increases meal frequency"],"answer":1,"explain":"The passage's mechanism: fasting shifts fuel from glucose to stored fat, which trims fat AND relieves the pancreas, while improving mitochondrial activity and cutting inflammation. The other options are never mentioned (liver glucose creation is actually what fasting reduces).","tag":"rc"},{"q":"Warning on extremely hard fasting?","options":["More is always better","May trigger stress, disrupting hormones","Only affects athletes","No warning given"],"answer":1,"explain":"Final paragraph warns overdoing fasting induces stress responses that disturb hormonal balance. The safe path per researchers: guided, personalized schedules (age, health, activity) — never extreme routines.","tag":"rc"},{"q":"Synonym of MITIGATE?","options":["alleviate","aggravate","imitate","migrate"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Mitigate' = make less severe. 'Alleviate' (relieve) matches exactly — both pair with pain/problems. 'Aggravate' (worsen) is the antonym; imitate/migrate are unrelated lookalikes.","tag":"vocab"},{"q":"Antonym of TRANSPARENT?","options":["clear","opaque","obvious","frank"],"answer":1,"explain":"Transparent = letting light/meaning through. 'Opaque' = blocking both light and understanding — the direct opposite. 'Clear', 'obvious', 'frank' are all synonyms of transparent.","tag":"vocab"},{"q":"Correct spelling?","options":["occurrence","ocurrence","occurrance","occurance"],"answer":0,"explain":"OC-CUR-RENCE: double C (from 'occur' + '-rence') and double R. The '-ance' ending (not '-ence') is the second trap. Trick: it 'occurred' twice — double letters twice.","tag":"spelling"},{"q":"___ honest man would admit it.","options":["A","An","The","No article"],"answer":1,"explain":"Articles follow SOUND, not spelling: 'honest' starts with a silent h, so it begins with the vowel sound 'o' → 'an honest man'. Compare: 'a horse' (h sounded), 'an hour' (silent h).","tag":"article"},{"q":"She is good ___ mathematics.","options":["in","at","for","with"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fixed preposition: 'good AT' a subject or skill ('good at cricket', 'bad at maths'), but 'good FOR' health/purposes and 'good WITH' people/tools. The blank is a subject → at.","tag":"preposition"},{"q":"Find error: One of the boys have left.","options":["One of","the boys","have","No error"],"answer":2,"explain":"'One of the + plural noun' always takes a SINGULAR verb, because the subject is 'one', not 'boys'. Correct: 'One of the boys HAS left.' Same pattern: 'Each of them IS ready.'","tag":"sva"},{"q":"Each of the students ___ submitted the form.","options":["have","has","have all","are"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Each of' is always singular, like 'either/neither of'. Correct: 'Each ... HAS'. Ignore the plural noun between — the subject is 'each'.","tag":"sva"},{"q":"The scissors ___ on the table.","options":["is","are","was lying","has"],"answer":1,"explain":"Scissors/trousers/spectacles are plural-only nouns: always 'are'. Contrast: 'a pair of scissors IS'. Same logic for news/mathematics (singular-only): 'The news IS good.'","tag":"sva"},{"q":"Neither the manager nor the clerks ___ present.","options":["was","were","is","has been"],"answer":1,"explain":"With neither/nor (and either/or), the verb agrees with the NEAREST subject — 'clerks' → were. Flip the order ('...nor the manager') and it becomes 'was'.","tag":"sva"},{"q":"Ten kilometres ___ a long walk.","options":["are","is","were","have"],"answer":1,"explain":"Distances, money, time as a single quantity take singular verbs: 'Ten km IS...', 'Rs.500 IS enough', 'Two hours IS plenty'. Think of it as one block.","tag":"sva"},{"q":"The committee ___ divided on the issue.","options":["is","are","was","has"],"answer":1,"explain":"Collective nouns take plural verbs when members act INDIVIDUALLY (divided opinions → are). If acting as one unit: 'The committee HAS decided.' Meaning decides number.","tag":"sva"},{"q":"No sooner had he arrived ___ it started raining.","options":["when","than","then","whenever"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fixed pair: 'No sooner ... THAN ...'. Its twin: 'Hardly/Scarcely ... WHEN ...'. Mixing them ('no sooner when') is the classic trap.","tag":"conjunction"},{"q":"He is taller ___ his brother.","options":["from","than","then","to"],"answer":1,"explain":"Comparatives (-er, more/less) pair with THAN. 'Then' is about time/order. Pattern: comparative adjective + than. Same: 'better than', 'more important than'.","tag":"grammar"},{"q":"___ European called at the office.","options":["A","An","The","No article"],"answer":0,"explain":"'European' starts with a consonant SOUND (yu-), so 'a'. Articles follow pronunciation: a university, a one-rupee note, but an MBA (em-), an hour, an FIR.","tag":"article"},{"q":"She has been working here ___ 2019.","options":["since","for","from","by"],"answer":0,"explain":"SINCE + point in time (2019, Monday, morning); FOR + duration (5 years, 2 hours). Test: can you put a calendar pin on it? 2019 → since.","tag":"preposition"},{"q":"He will finish the work ___ Monday.","options":["in","on","by","at"],"answer":2,"explain":"'By Monday' = no later than Monday (deadline). 'On Monday' = during that day. 'In' is for months/years. Deadline Qs almost always want 'by'.","tag":"preposition"},{"q":"Distribute the sweets ___ the five children.","options":["between","among","within","amid"],"answer":1,"explain":"Between = 2 parties; among = 3+. Five children → among. Same logic: 'among friends', 'between you and me'.","tag":"preposition"},{"q":"He insisted ___ paying the bill.","options":["to","for","on","about"],"answer":2,"explain":"Fixed preposition: insist ON + gerund ('insisted on paying'). Verbs carry their prepositions: good at, accused of, married to, afraid of, aware of — memorize as pairs.","tag":"preposition"},{"q":"She is married ___ a doctor.","options":["with","to","by","for"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Married TO' (person). Indian-English 'married with' is wrong. Cousin pair: 'engaged to', 'divorced from'.","tag":"preposition"},{"q":"I have no patience ___ slow workers.","options":["for","with","towards","about"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Patience WITH people', 'patience FOR delay/results'. Two different prepositions by object type — exams test exactly this split.","tag":"preposition"},{"q":"He is junior ___ me in service.","options":["than","to","from","over"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adjectives ending in -or (junior, senior, inferior, superior, prior) take TO, never than: 'junior to', 'superior to'. Than is only for -er comparatives.","tag":"grammar"},{"q":"Scarcely had we left ___ it began to pour.","options":["than","when","then","that"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hardly/Scarcely pair with WHEN; No sooner pairs with THAN. Two separate idiom families — never cross them.","tag":"conjunction"},{"q":"Unless you hurry, you ___ miss the train.","options":["would","will","shall","might had"],"answer":1,"explain":"Type-1 conditional (real possibility): If/Unless + present → will + base verb. 'Unless' already contains 'not', so never add another ('unless you don't hurry' is wrong).","tag":"conditional"},{"q":"If I were rich, I ___ travel the world.","options":["will","would","shall","can"],"answer":1,"explain":"Type-2 (imaginary): If + past (were for all persons) → would + base. 'If I WERE' (never 'was' in exams) signals the unreal branch.","tag":"conditional"},{"q":"Had he studied, he ___ passed.","options":["will have","would have","shall have","has"],"answer":1,"explain":"Type-3 (past impossible): Had + past participle → would have + participle. Inverted form of 'If he had studied'. All three conditionals hinge on the if-clause tense.","tag":"conditional"},{"q":"She suggested that he ___ early.","options":["leaves","leave","left","leaving"],"answer":1,"explain":"Subjunctive after suggest/insist/demand/require: base verb, no -s. '...that he LEAVE' (not leaves). American rule adopted in exams: that-clause stays bare.","tag":"subjunctive"},{"q":"It's time we ___.","options":["go","went","gone","going"],"answer":1,"explain":"'It's time / It's high time' + PAST tense for present meaning: 'It's time we WENT.' Odd but fixed — memorize the pattern, don't logic it.","tag":"subjunctive"},{"q":"You ___ smoke here; it's prohibited.","options":["must not","need not","don't need to","haven't to"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Must not' = prohibition (not allowed). 'Need not' = no necessity (allowed but unnecessary). Prohibition vs lack-of-necessity is the tested contrast.","tag":"modals"},{"q":"He ___ swim well as a child.","options":["could","can","will","shall"],"answer":0,"explain":"Past ability → could. 'Can' is present ability. Timeline decides: 'as a child' = past → could. Same: 'will be able to' for future.","tag":"modals"},{"q":"___ you please open the window?","options":["Could","Might","Should","Would always"],"answer":0,"explain":"Polite requests: Could/Would you... 'Might' expresses possibility, not requests. 'Could' is softer than 'can' — politeness scales with distance.","tag":"modals"},{"q":"Find error: He gave me an advice.","options":["He gave","me","an advice","No error"],"answer":2,"explain":"'Advice' is uncountable — no 'an', no plural 'advices'. Correct: 'a piece of advice' / 'some advice'. Same family: furniture, information, luggage, news.","tag":"articles"},{"q":"Find error: The furnitures are new.","options":["The furnitures","are","new","No error"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Furniture' is uncountable: 'The furniture IS new.' Uncountables (furniture, equipment, scenery, poetry) never take -s and take singular verbs.","tag":"grammar"},{"q":"Find error: She bought three dozens eggs.","options":["She bought","three dozens","eggs","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"After a number, dozen/score/hundred stay SINGULAR: 'three dozen eggs'. Without a number they pluralize: 'dozens of eggs'. Same: hundred, thousand, million.","tag":"grammar"},{"q":"Find error: He is my cousin brother.","options":["He is","my cousin brother","No error","—"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Cousin brother/sister' is Indian English — standard is just 'cousin'. Delete 'brother'. Same cleanup: 'return back' → return, 'repeat again' → repeat.","tag":"redundancy"},{"q":"Find error: Please return back my book.","options":["Please return","back","my book","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Return' already means 'give back' — 'back' is redundant. Redundancy Qs test doubled meanings: return back, repeat again, revert back, widow woman.","tag":"redundancy"},{"q":"Find error: The reason is because he was late.","options":["The reason","is because","he was late","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"'The reason is...' needs THAT, not because ('the reason is that...'). 'Because' already gives a reason — pairing doubles it. Correct: 'The reason is that he was late.'","tag":"grammar"},{"q":"Find error: Hardly he had slept when the bell rang.","options":["Hardly he had","slept","when the bell rang","No error"],"answer":0,"explain":"Inversion after negative openers: 'Hardly HAD he slept...' (auxiliary before subject). Same for Never/Rarely/Seldom/No sooner at sentence start.","tag":"inversion"},{"q":"Find error: No sooner did he come when it rained.","options":["No sooner did he come","when","it rained","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"No sooner pairs with THAN, not when. Correct: '...than it rained.' (Hardly/scarcely would take 'when'.) One word decides the whole Q.","tag":"conjunction"},{"q":"Find error: She asked me where was I going.","options":["She asked me","where was I","going","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"Indirect questions keep STATEMENT order: '...where I was going' (no inversion, no question mark). Direct: 'Where are you going?' → Indirect: '...where I was going.'","tag":"narration"},{"q":"Find error: He said that he will come.","options":["He said","that","he will","No error"],"answer":2,"explain":"Past reporting verb backshifts: 'said ... would come'. 'Said + will' mismatches tense. Only universal truths escape backshifting ('...that the sun rises').","tag":"narration"},{"q":"Find error: Being a rainy day, we stayed home.","options":["Being a rainy day","we stayed","home","No error"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dangling modifier: 'being a rainy day' must describe the subject, but 'we' aren't a day. Correct: 'IT being a rainy day, we stayed home.' Attach every opener to the noun it describes.","tag":"modifier"},{"q":"Find error: Walking down the road, a snake bit him.","options":["Walking down the road","a snake","bit him","No error"],"answer":0,"explain":"Who was walking? Him, not the snake. Correct: 'Walking down the road, HE was bitten...' or 'While he was walking...'. Classic dangling-participle trap.","tag":"modifier"},{"q":"Find error: She is more smarter than me.","options":["She is","more smarter","than me","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"Double comparative: use EITHER 'smarter' OR 'more smart' — never both. Same: 'more better', 'most bravest'. One degree marker per adjective.","tag":"comparative"},{"q":"Find error: This is the most unique case.","options":["This is","the most unique","case","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"Absolute adjectives (unique, perfect, complete, universal) admit NO degree — something is unique or it isn't. Delete 'most'. Same: 'more perfect', 'very unique'.","tag":"comparative"},{"q":"Find error: Prefer tea than coffee.","options":["Prefer","tea than","coffee","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Prefer' pairs with TO: 'prefer tea TO coffee'. Than belongs to comparatives (better than). Fixed pair, pure memory.","tag":"preposition"},{"q":"Find error: He denied to take the money.","options":["He denied","to take","the money","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Deny' takes a GERUND: 'denied TAKING'. Verb-pattern families: deny/enjoy/finish/mind + -ing; want/decide/promise + to-infinitive. Mixing them is the tested error.","tag":"gerund"},{"q":"Find error: I look forward to meet you.","options":["I look forward","to meet","you","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Look forward TO' — the 'to' is a PREPOSITION here, so a gerund follows: 'to MEETING you'. Same: 'addicted to smoking', 'used to driving'. If 'to' can take 'the' after it, it's a preposition.","tag":"gerund"},{"q":"Find error: She is used to get up early.","options":["She is used","to get","up early","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Be used TO' (accustomed) + gerund → 'used to GETTING up'. Contrast 'used TO get' (past habit, no 'is'). Two structures, one spelling — exams love the pair.","tag":"gerund"},{"q":"Find error: Let he go.","options":["Let","he","go","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"After 'let', use OBJECT pronouns: let HIM go. Same for make/help/bid in causatives: 'made him do it'. Subject pronouns (he/they) never follow let.","tag":"pronoun"},{"q":"Find error: Between you and I, he is wrong.","options":["Between","you and I","he is wrong","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"Prepositions take OBJECT pronouns: 'between you and ME'. Test by dropping one side: 'between I' sounds wrong → 'between me'. Same: 'for him and me'.","tag":"pronoun"},{"q":"Find error: Everyone must do their duty.","options":["Everyone","must do","their duty","No error"],"answer":2,"explain":"Strictly, indefinite pronouns (everyone, each, anybody) are singular → 'HIS duty'. Modern usage accepts 'their', but exams follow the traditional rule: singular pronoun. Choose 'his' in the test hall.","tag":"pronoun"},{"q":"Find error: The quality of apples were good.","options":["The quality","of apples","were","No error"],"answer":2,"explain":"Verb agrees with the HEAD noun ('quality', singular), not the nearer plural ('apples'). Correct: '...WAS good'. Always find the true subject before choosing the verb.","tag":"sva"},{"q":"Find error: A number of students was absent.","options":["A number of","students","was","No error"],"answer":2,"explain":"'A number of' = plural → WERE absent. Contrast 'THE number of students WAS large' (singular verb — the number itself is one quantity). A/the flips everything.","tag":"sva"},{"q":"Find error: She speaks English good.","options":["She speaks","English","good","No error"],"answer":2,"explain":"Verbs need ADVERBS: 'speaks English WELL'. 'Good' is an adjective (good boy). Quick test: well answers 'how?' — speaks how? Well.","tag":"adverb"},{"q":"Find error: He runs very fastly.","options":["He runs","very","fastly","No error"],"answer":2,"explain":"'Fast' is already an adverb (fast runner, runs fast) — 'fastly' doesn't exist. Same flat adverbs: hard ('work hard', not hardly=barely!), late, early, long.","tag":"adverb"},{"q":"Find error: I have seen him yesterday.","options":["I have seen","him","yesterday","No error"],"answer":0,"explain":"Finished-time markers (yesterday, last night, in 2020) forbid present perfect → simple past: 'I SAW him yesterday.' Present perfect pairs with unfinished time (today, this week, since 2020).","tag":"tense"},{"q":"Find error: I am knowing the answer.","options":["I am knowing","the answer","No error","—"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stative verbs (know, like, want, own, believe) don't take continuous forms. Correct: 'I KNOW the answer.' Test: can you 'be ___ing' it right now deliberately? If no, it's stative.","tag":"tense"},{"q":"Find error: By 2020, he works here ten years.","options":["By 2020","he works","here ten years","No error"],"answer":1,"explain":"'By + past time' needs past perfect: 'he HAD worked here ten years (by 2020)'. By-deadlines shift the tense one step back: future→future perfect, past→past perfect.","tag":"tense"},{"q":"Synonym of CANDID?","options":["Rude","Frank","Sly","Vague"],"answer":1,"explain":"Candid = frank, outspoken, honest. 'Rude' is blunt+unpleasant (wrong tone), 'sly' is cunning (opposite), 'vague' unclear. Camera LINK: a candid photo is unposed = honest.","tag":"vocab"},{"q":"Synonym of ABANDON?","options":["Keep","Forsake","Cherish","Pursue"],"answer":1,"explain":"Abandon = give up completely → forsake. Keep/cherish are antonyms; pursue (chase) is unrelated. Root 'ban' = proclaim against → to leave behind.","tag":"vocab"},{"q":"Antonym of BRAVE?","options":["Bold","Cowardly","Heroic","Daring"],"answer":1,"explain":"Brave↔cowardly. Bold/heroic/daring are all synonyms of brave — the Q surrounds the odd one with three friends. Strategy: group synonyms first, the leftover is usually the answer.","tag":"vocab"},{"q":"One word: one who can use both hands?","options":["Ambidextrous","Ambiguous","Versatile","Expert"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ambi- (both) + -dextrous (right-handed/skillful) = both-hands skilled. Ambiguous = unclear (different root sense). One-word Qs reward root knowledge: ambi-=both.","tag":"one-word"},{"q":"One word: killing of one's own race/people?","options":["Homicide","Genocide","Suicide","Fratricide"],"answer":1,"explain":"Genocide = -cide (killing) of a gens (race/people). Homicide=human, suicide=self, fratricide=brother, patricide=father, regicide=king. Learn the -cide family together.","tag":"one-word"},{"q":"Idiom 'to bite the dust'?","options":["To eat quickly","To fail/be defeated","To work hard","To hide"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fallen fighters face-down in dust → to fail/be defeated (or break down, for things). Nothing to do with eating.","tag":"idiom"},{"q":"Idiom 'a blessing in disguise'?","options":["A hidden curse","A good thing that seemed bad","A religious gift","A lucky charm"],"answer":1,"explain":"Something that looked unfortunate but turned out fortunate — e.g. missing a bus and avoiding an accident. Disguise = hidden form of a blessing.","tag":"idiom"},{"q":"Idiom 'once in a blue moon'?","options":["Every month","Very rarely","At night only","On festivals"],"answer":1,"explain":"Blue moons (second full moon in a month) are rare → very rarely. Frequency idioms: 'once in a blue moon' (rare) vs 'day in, day out' (routine).","tag":"idiom"},{"q":"Phrasal 'put off' vs 'put out'?","options":["Both mean postpone","Postpone vs extinguish/inconvenience","Both mean cancel","Same meaning"],"answer":1,"explain":"Put OFF = postpone (meeting put off). Put OUT = extinguish fire OR inconvenience ('sorry to put you out'). Preposition changes everything — learn phrasals as whole units.","tag":"phrasal"},{"q":"Phrasal 'look after' means?","options":["Admire","Take care of","Search for","Overlook"],"answer":1,"explain":"Look after = take care of. Family: look into (investigate), look up to (admire), look down on (despise), look forward to (anticipate). After=care, into=inquiry.","tag":"phrasal"},{"q":"RC: fasting's effect on the pancreas?","options":["Increases insulin output","Eases its burden via fat-burning shift","Shrinks it permanently","No effect mentioned"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passage: shifting fuel from glucose to fat stores 'eases the burden on pancreatic function'. It regulates load, not output volume — (a) misstates the mechanism.","tag":"rc"},{"q":"RC: why is fasting called sustainable?","options":["No discipline needed","Avoids nutrient deficiencies of crash diets","Works overnight","Needs no guidance"],"answer":1,"explain":"Unlike sharp restrictions that cause deficiencies, strategic fasting is sustainable — though it still demands discipline and guidance. (a), (c), (d) all contradict the passage's own qualifiers.","tag":"rc"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 'Exhort' means?","options":["Recommend","Coax","Pressure","Push"],"answer":2,"explain":"Exhort = strongly urge/press someone to act. 'Pressure' captures that force; 'coax' is gentle persuasion (flattery), 'push' is physical, 'recommend' is mild advice. When shades differ, pick the strongest match to 'strongly encourage'.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] David said to Anna, 'Mona will leave for her native place tomorrow.' Indirect?","options":["...that Mona will leave... tomorrow","...that Mona left... the next day","...that Mona would leave... tomorrow","David told Anna that Mona would leave for her native place the next day"],"answer":3,"explain":"Backshift will→would, and 'tomorrow' → 'the next day'. Only (d) does both. Partial conversions (keeping 'will' or 'tomorrow') are the designed traps.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 'Infamy' means?","options":["Notoriety","Glory","Integrity","Familiarity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Infamy = famous for BAD reasons = notoriety (paper spells it 'noteriety' — a typo, answer stands). Glory/integrity are positive; familiarity is neutral. Connotation first: negative word → negative answer.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 'A friendship founded on business is best than...' Improve?","options":["is good than","is better than","is best than","is better then"],"answer":1,"explain":"Comparative (two things compared) needs 'better THAN'. 'Best' is superlative (3+), and 'then' is time, not comparison. Two errors hiding in one option set — check degree AND spelling.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] If I had helped him, he ___.","options":["will not be drowned","would not be drowned","will not have drowned","would not have drowned"],"answer":3,"explain":"Type-3 conditional (past + had + participle) → would + HAVE + participle: 'would not have drowned'. 'Would not be drowned' misses the perfect aspect the past context demands.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] '...information provided to the staff was not adecuate...' Wrong word?","options":["information","provided","adecuate","retaliated"],"answer":2,"explain":"'adecuate' → ADEQUATE (a-d-e-q-u-a-t-e). Classic Q形: one planted misspelling among correct words. Sound it out: ad-e-kwut.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Opposite of CESSATION?","options":["Commencement","Renewal","Ongoing","Interruption"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cessation = stopping/ending → opposite is commencement (beginning). Renewal restarts (close but not opposite), ongoing = continuing state, interruption = temporary break.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Procrastinate is most opposite to?","options":["Experiment","Expedite","Exclude","Propagate"],"answer":1,"explain":"Procrastinate = delay; expedite = speed up/hasten. Root 'pro-' (forward) vs 'ex-pedite' (foot out the door, fast). Direct antonym pair.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 'He walked as faster he could...' Error part?","options":["as faster","he could so that","he would not","miss the train"],"answer":0,"explain":"Correct idiom: 'as FAST AS he could'. Comparative 'faster' can't sit inside as...as. Correct: 'He walked as fast as he could so that...'","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Kiran asked, 'Did you see the match last night?' Indirect?","options":["...whether I saw... the earlier night","...whether I has seen... earlier night","...did I see... last night","Kiran asked me whether I had seen the match the previous night"],"answer":3,"explain":"Yes/no Q → whether + statement order + backshift (did see → had seen) + last night → previous night. (d) gets tense+order right ('last night' retained loosely). (b) fails with 'has seen' (wrong person/tense).","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 'Relieved' is most similar to?","options":["Expand","Backed","Supported","Calmed"],"answer":3,"explain":"Relieved = eased of anxiety → calmed. 'Backed/supported' suit 'backed', not relieved. Feeling-words map to feeling-words.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 'Right action cannot came out of nothing...' Improve?","options":["come out of","came out off","come off","coming out of"],"answer":0,"explain":"Modal 'cannot' + BASE verb → 'cannot COME'. 'Off' (away from) ≠ 'of' (from) — preposition precision decides.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 'Repartee' means?","options":["Refuse","Celebrate","Response","Question"],"answer":2,"explain":"Repartee = witty, quick reply in conversation → response (clever one). Think dinner-party banter.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 'Assured' is most similar to?","options":["Insured","Definite","Doubted","Confident"],"answer":3,"explain":"Assured = confident/self-certain. 'Insured' is the insurance-context trap (different word entirely); 'definite' describes things, not feelings.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Opposite of FURTHEST?","options":["Longest","Closest","Greatest","Shortest"],"answer":1,"explain":"Furthest (most distant) ↔ closest (paper spells 'Closet' — read past the typo). Shortest is about length, not distance.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 'I assume ___ with me.' Fill?","options":["Every one agreeing","That every one agrees","Every one to agree","That every one to agree"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Assume' takes a full that-clause with finite verb: 'that everyone AGREES'. Infinitive 'to agree' can't follow 'that'.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] '...stayed up the whole knight and studied.' Wrong word?","options":["Worried","stayed","knight","studied"],"answer":2,"explain":"'knight' (armored rider) vs 'night' (evening) — homophone trap. He studied all NIGHT.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 'When will you hand ___ your assignment?'","options":["In","Back","Down","Into"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Hand IN' = submit. Hand back = return, hand down = pass through generations, hand over = surrender. Submit-context → in.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 'Barren' means?","options":["Good","Wholesome","Unproductive","Profitable"],"answer":2,"explain":"Barren land yields nothing → unproductive. Wholesome/profitable/good are positive — connotation eliminates three at once.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[NICL 2017] Chicago researchers concluded?","options":["Social-media addiction exceeds smoking/drinking cravings","Smoking addiction ranked highest","No difference found","They studied only students"],"answer":0,"explain":"Passage: people showed higher addiction to social media than the need to smoke or drink; media cravings ranked higher. Both findings point one way — addiction hierarchy topped by social media.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017] Why is oxytocin the 'cuddle chemical'?","options":["The brain releases pleasure chemicals when you kiss, hug — or tweet","It creates the urge to seek and search","It is simply dopamine","It prevents all addiction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Passage: released during kissing/hugging (also tweeting), building bonds like mother–baby. The seek/search urge is DOPAMINE's role — the passage contrasts the two chemicals; don't swap them.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017] Schools' actions on phones (passage)?","options":["Only punishments","Educational use + rewards AND banning devices","Ignored the issue","Only parent meetings"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passage gives BOTH: drastic bans (smartphones/iPads off premises) AND constructive use (education + rewards). 'Only' options die when the passage lists two approaches.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017] What is true of dopamine now?","options":["Simply a pleasure chemical","Creates 'wanting' drive; triggered by unpredictability + reward cues","Only affects smokers","Fully understood, no research left"],"answer":1,"explain":"Old belief = mere pleasure chemical; new finding = produces DESIRE to seek, fired by unpredictability, small info bits, reward cues — social media's exact ingredients. The passage explicitly updates the old view.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017] Why no medical term for social-networking addiction?","options":["No research exists","Cases aren't severe + habit is controllable","Doctors ignore it","It affects only kids"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passage: can't be called disease/disorder (not severe) and the habit can be maintained/prevented. Both halves needed — research DOES exist (Chicago study cited), killing option (a).","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017] Students' most common study-break activity?","options":["YouTube","Facebook","Twitter","WhatsApp"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passage: 'Facebook is the most common activity that university students switch to, when studying' — and heavy switchers show lower achievement. Direct line lift.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017] Best title for the passage?","options":["The Psychology of Social Addiction","Addiction in Students","Beliefs for Social Life","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"The passage spans chemicals (dopamine/oxytocin), research, schools — psychology of addiction covers all; student-only titles are too narrow. Title = widest accurate cover.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017] 'Social Networking Addiction' means?","options":["Stalking","Fake profiles","Finding friends online","Spending too much time on social sites"],"answer":3,"explain":"Passage definition: spending too much time on Facebook/Twitter/Instagram etc. Definition Qs quote the passage's own words.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017 cloze] '...holds its last ___ session before elections' (full attendance)","options":["contrived","plenary","conclusive","punitive"],"answer":1,"explain":"Plenary = session attended by ALL members. Contrived (forced), conclusive (decisive), punitive (punishing) miss the 'full attendance' hint in brackets — the bracket IS the definition.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017 cloze] '...in a ___ to clean up oceans'","options":["bud","strive","bid","venture"],"answer":2,"explain":"Idiom 'in a BID to' = in an attempt to. 'Strive to' needs different grammar (strive + to-verb); 'venture' needs 'a venture into'. Collocation decides.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017 cloze] '...agree to ___ such throwaway items'","options":["prohibit","protest","proclaim","promote"],"answer":0,"explain":"Context = banning plastics → prohibit (forbid by law). Protest (object), proclaim (announce), promote (encourage) all point the wrong way.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017 cloze] '...term that ___ a number of initiatives'","options":["featured","have","comprise","features"],"answer":3,"explain":"Singular subject 'term' needs singular verb → 'features'. 'Have/comprise' are plural; 'featured' is past (the term is current). Subject-verb agreement inside cloze.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017 cloze] '...part of an ___ to cut road fatalities'","options":["toil","endeavour","vantage","travail"],"answer":1,"explain":"'An endeavour to cut...' = an attempt/effort toward. Toil/travail = hard labour (wrong register), vantage = advantage point. Formal passages favour 'endeavour'.","tag":"nicl2017"},{"q":"[NICL 2017 cloze] 'Behind the ___ (commotion)...'","options":["rackets","gaggle","noise","chortle"],"answer":2,"explain":"Bracket defines commotion = noisy disturbance → 'noise'. Rackets (also noise, slang) is second-best but 'behind the noise' is the natural phrase; gaggle (geese/people), chortle (laugh) miss.","tag":"nicl2017"}]},{"id":"quant","title":"Quant","desc":"Simplification • series • quadratics • DI • arithmetic","track":"prelims","cards":[{"q":"Rs.4800 for 2y at 20% SI. Interest reinvested 2y at 10% CI. Interest from Y?","options":["Rs.384","Rs.403.2","Rs.472.4","Rs.503.8"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1 — SI from X: 4800 × 20% × 2 = Rs.1920. Step 2 — this 1920 becomes principal for Y: amount = 1920 × 1.1 × 1.1 = Rs.2323.2. Step 3 — CI interest = amount − principal = 2323.2 − 1920 = Rs.403.2. Trap: 384 is 20% of 1920 (one year's interest), not two years compounded.","tag":"si-ci"},{"q":"Still:stream=6:1, upstream 20 km/h. Time for 126 km downstream?","options":["4.2h","4.5h","5h","5.5h"],"answer":1,"explain":"Let still=6k, stream=k. Upstream = still − stream = 5k = 20 → k=4. So still=24, stream=4, downstream = 24+4 = 28 km/h. Time = 126/28 = 4.5 hours. Remember: upstream subtracts, downstream adds.","tag":"boat"},{"q":"Train A: pole 16s, 600m platform 36s. Speed?","options":["25 m/s","30 m/s","35 m/s","40 m/s"],"answer":1,"explain":"The extra 20 seconds (36−16) cover exactly the 600m platform, so speed = 600/20 = 30 m/s. Train length = 30×16 = 480m. Shortcut for these Qs: speed = platform length ÷ (platform time − pole time).","tag":"train"},{"q":"Series: 2, 96, 16, 768, 128, ?","options":["1536","3072","6144","12288"],"answer":2,"explain":"Alternate operations: ×48, then ÷6, repeating. 2×48=96, 96÷6=16, 16×48=768, 768÷6=128, so 128×48=6144. When a series jumps up-down-up, always test alternating patterns.","tag":"series"},{"q":"Series: 3, 29, 127, 345, ? (odd cubes +2)","options":["511","731","999","1333"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each term is an odd cube plus 2: 1³+2=3, 3³+2=29, 5³+2=127, 7³+2=345, so 9³+2=729+2=731. Clue: numbers just above perfect cubes (127, 345) scream 'cube ± constant'.","tag":"series"},{"q":"?×72/100 = 118 − 80% of 125. Find ?","options":["18","25","32","36"],"answer":1,"explain":"Simplify the right side first: 80% of 125 = 100, so RHS = 118−100 = 18. Then ? × 0.72 = 18 → ? = 18/0.72 = 25. Doing RHS first avoids messy decimals.","tag":"percent"},{"q":"m²−24m+135=0. Roots?","options":["15, 9","12, 12","15, −9","27, 5"],"answer":0,"explain":"Need two numbers multiplying to +135 with sum +24 (signs: −24m means both negative factors... actually both numbers are positive 15 and 9 since −15×−9=+135 and −15−9=−24). So m=15 or 9. Rule: for x²−Sx+P, find factor pairs of P summing to S.","tag":"quadratic"},{"q":"(m−16) = 32/(m−12). m?","options":["20, 8","16, 12","18, 10","24, 4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cross-multiply first: (m−16)(m−12)=32 → m²−28m+192−32=0 → m²−28m+160=0. Factor: need pair multiplying to 160, summing to 28 → 20 and 8. Always clear fractions before factoring.","tag":"quadratic"},{"q":"144 km/h train crosses a pole in 12.5s. Length?","options":["400m","450m","500m","550m"],"answer":2,"explain":"Convert km/h to m/s with ×5/18: 144×5/18 = 40 m/s. A pole means distance = train's own length = speed × time = 40×12.5 = 500m. Memorize ×5/18 (km/h→m/s) and ×18/5 (reverse).","tag":"train"},{"q":"Square perimeter 124m. Area?","options":["900","961","1024","1089"],"answer":1,"explain":"Side = perimeter/4 = 31. Area = 31² = 961. Memorize squares around 30: 29²=841, 30²=900, 31²=961, 32²=1024 — they recur constantly.","tag":"mensuration"},{"q":"10 people, average weight 50 kg. Total?","options":["450","500","550","600"],"answer":1,"explain":"Total = average × count = 50×10 = 500 kg. Nearly every average Q is solved by converting to totals first, operating, then converting back.","tag":"average"},{"q":"8% rent + 12% groceries, then 25% of the rest on transport. Total spent?","options":["32%","36%","40%","45%"],"answer":2,"explain":"After rent+groceries, 80% remains. Transport = 25% OF that remainder = 0.25×80 = 20% of salary (not 25%!). Total = 8+12+20 = 40%. Trap: applying the second % to salary instead of the remainder.","tag":"percent"},{"q":"48% of 650 + 22% of 350 = ?","options":["389","399","379","409"],"answer":0,"explain":"Split for mental math: 48% of 650 = (50%−2%) of 650 = 325−13 = 312. 22% of 350 = 10%×2 (=70) + 2% (=7) = 77. Total 312+77 = 389.","tag":"simplification"},{"q":"31% of 1001 ≈ ?","options":["300","310","330","341"],"answer":1,"explain":"Round to friendly numbers: ≈30% of 1000 = 300, plus ~1% of 1000 (=10) → 310. Approximation Qs reward rounding first, not exact computation.","tag":"approx"},{"q":"Series: 5, 11, 23, 47, ?","options":["71","83","95","119"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each term doubles and adds 1: 5×2+1=11, 11×2+1=23, 23×2+1=47, so 47×2+1=95. Pattern family: ×2±1, ×3±2 — test these before anything exotic.","tag":"series"},{"q":"Series: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30, ?","options":["36","40","42","44"],"answer":2,"explain":"First differences: +4, +6, +8, +10 — rising by 2 each time, so next difference is +12: 30+12=42. These are pronic numbers n(n+1): 1×2, 2×3, 3×4… 6×7=42.","tag":"series"},{"q":"x²−11x+30=0. Roots?","options":["5, 6","3, 10","−5, −6","15, 2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Need factors of 30 summing to 11: 5 and 6 (5×6=30, 5+6=11), both positive because the middle sign is negative... check: (x−5)(x−6)=x²−11x+30 ✓. So x=5 or 6. Option (b) multiplies to 30 but sums to 13 — always verify the sum.","tag":"quadratic"},{"q":"Rs.10000 at 10% CI for 2 years. Amount?","options":["11000","12000","12100","12150"],"answer":2,"explain":"Amount = P(1+r)ⁿ = 10000×1.1² = 10000×1.21 = 12100. (Interest alone would be 2100 — read whether the Q asks amount or interest; here it's amount.)","tag":"si-ci"},{"q":"A does work in 10 days, B in 15. Together?","options":["5 days","6 days","7.5 days","12.5 days"],"answer":1,"explain":"Add RATES, not days: 1/10+1/15 = 5/30 = 1/6 of work per day → 6 days. Formula: together = (a×b)/(a+b) = 150/25 = 6.","tag":"work"},{"q":"Inlet fills in 4h, outlet empties in 6h. Together?","options":["10h","12h","24h","2.4h"],"answer":1,"explain":"Net rate = fill − drain = 1/4 − 1/6 = 1/12 per hour → 12 hours to fill. Sign rule: inlet +, outlet −. If the result were negative, the tank would empty.","tag":"pipes"},{"q":"72 km/h in m/s?","options":["18","20","24","26"],"answer":1,"explain":"Multiply by 5/18: 72×5/18 = 4×5 = 20 m/s. (72/18=4 first, then ×5 — divide before multiplying to keep numbers small.)","tag":"tsd"},{"q":"Still water 15 km/h, stream 3 km/h. Downstream distance in 5h?","options":["60","75","90","105"],"answer":2,"explain":"Downstream speed = still + stream = 18 km/h. Distance = 18×5 = 90 km. Most errors come from subtracting instead of adding downstream.","tag":"boat"},{"q":"A:B=2:3, B:C=4:5. A:B:C?","options":["2:3:5","8:12:15","6:9:10","8:10:15"],"answer":1,"explain":"Make B common: B is 3 and 4, LCM 12. Scale first ratio by 4 (8:12), second by 3 (12:15) → 8:12:15. Never join ratios until the shared term matches.","tag":"ratio"},{"q":"5 numbers average 27. Excluding one, average 25. Excluded number?","options":["30","33","35","37"],"answer":2,"explain":"Total of 5 = 135; total of remaining 4 = 100. Excluded = 135−100 = 35. The removed value equals the drop in total — one subtraction, no algebra needed.","tag":"average"},{"q":"Father:son ages 3:1, sum 48. Father's age?","options":["24","30","36","40"],"answer":2,"explain":"4 ratio-parts = 48 → 1 part = 12 → father = 3×12 = 36. Ratio Qs: always convert the total into value-per-part first.","tag":"age"},{"q":"Profit Rs.25000 shared 2:3. Smaller share?","options":["8000","10000","12000","15000"],"answer":1,"explain":"5 parts = 25000 → 1 part = 5000 → smaller (2 parts) = 10000. Partnership profit follows investment×time ratios exactly like this.","tag":"partnership"},{"q":"CP Rs.800, profit 25%. SP?","options":["900","950","1000","1050"],"answer":2,"explain":"SP = CP × (1+profit%) = 800×1.25 = 1000. Profit% is always on CP unless stated otherwise — computing 25% of 1000 is the classic error.","tag":"profit"},{"q":"MP Rs.1200, discount 20%. SP?","options":["960","980","1000","1020"],"answer":0,"explain":"SP = MP × (1−discount) = 1200×0.8 = 960. Discount is on marked price; profit (if asked next) would be SP vs CP.","tag":"discount"},{"q":"40L mixture, 75% milk. Add 10L water. Milk %?","options":["56.25%","60%","62.5%","65%"],"answer":1,"explain":"Milk quantity never changes: 75% of 40 = 30L. New total = 50L. New % = 30/50 = 60%. Track the unchanged component — here, the milk.","tag":"mixture"},{"q":"A fair die thrown. Probability of even?","options":["1/3","1/2","2/3","1/6"],"answer":1,"explain":"Favourable outcomes {2,4,6} = 3 out of 6 equally likely → 3/6 = 1/2. Probability = favourable ÷ total, always count both carefully.","tag":"probability"},{"q":"Simplify: 45% of 300 + 25% of 200","options":["185","195","205","215"],"answer":0,"explain":"45% of 300 = 135; 25% of 200 = 50. Total 185. Split: 45% = 50%−5% (150−15).","tag":"simplification"},{"q":"2/5 of 375 + 3/8 of 400","options":["280","290","300","310"],"answer":2,"explain":"2/5×375 = 150; 3/8×400 = 150. Total 300. Fractions first, then add.","tag":"simplification"},{"q":"√144 + ∛27 + 5²","options":["30","34","40","44"],"answer":1,"explain":"12 + 3 + 25 = 40? No: 12+3+25=40. Recheck: √144=12, ∛27=3, 5²=25 → 40. Answer is 40.","tag":"simplification"},{"q":"Series: 7, 14, 28, 56, ?","options":["84","96","112","124"],"answer":2,"explain":"Constant ratio: each term doubles (56/28=2, 28/14=2...), so next = 56×2 = 112. Divide consecutive terms first — a constant quotient means geometric, then multiply once.","tag":"series"},{"q":"Series: 100, 90, 72, 46, ?","options":["10","12","14","18"],"answer":1,"explain":"Differences: −10, −18, −26 (down by 8 each) → next −34: 46−34=12.","tag":"series"},{"q":"Series: 1, 2, 6, 24, 120, ?","options":["600","720","840","960"],"answer":1,"explain":"Multiply by growing integers: ×2, ×3, ×4, ×5 — these are factorials (1!,2!,...,5!=120). Next multiplier is ×6: 120×6 = 720. Factorial fingerprints: 24, 120, 720 in a row.","tag":"series"},{"q":"Series: 11, 22, 33, 44, ?","options":["50","55","60","66"],"answer":1,"explain":"Constant difference +11 each step (22−11, 33−22...), so 44+11 = 55. Always test constant difference before exotic patterns — most series die at step one.","tag":"series"},{"q":"Wrong term: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 63","options":["32","63","16","8"],"answer":1,"explain":"Powers of 2: next should be 64, not 63. Wrong-term Qs: rebuild the rule, find the misfit.","tag":"series"},{"q":"x²−7x+12=0. Roots?","options":["3, 4","2, 6","−3, −4","1, 12"],"answer":0,"explain":"Need factors of 12 summing to 7: 3 and 4 (3×4=12, 3+4=7), both positive since the middle sign pattern (−7x, +12) gives (x−3)(x−4). Roots: 3, 4.","tag":"quadratic"},{"q":"x²+5x+6=0. Roots?","options":["2, 3","−2, −3","−1, −6","1, 6"],"answer":1,"explain":"Need factors of 6 summing to 5 with a +5x middle: both negative, −2 and −3 (−2×−3=6, −2−3=−5). So (x+2)(x+3)=0 → roots −2, −3. The +x term with +constant forces two negatives.","tag":"quadratic"},{"q":"2x²−7x+3=0. Larger root?","options":["3","0.5","1.5","2"],"answer":0,"explain":"2x²−7x+3=(2x−1)(x−3) → x=0.5 or 3. Larger = 3. For ax², split middle term: −6x−x.","tag":"quadratic"},{"q":"If x+y=10, xy=21. x,y?","options":["3, 7","4, 6","5, 5","2, 8"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pair summing to 10 with product 21: 3+7=10, 3×7=21. They are roots of t²−10t+21=0.","tag":"quadratic"},{"q":"SI on Rs.6000 at 8% for 3y?","options":["Rs.1200","Rs.1440","Rs.1600","Rs.1800"],"answer":1,"explain":"Simple interest is linear: P×r×t = 6000×0.08×3. 6000×0.08=480 per year, ×3 years = Rs.1440. No compounding in SI — just multiply through.","tag":"si"},{"q":"CI on Rs.5000 at 10% for 2y (interest)?","options":["Rs.1000","Rs.1050","Rs.1100","Rs.1150"],"answer":1,"explain":"Amount=5000×1.21=6050; interest=1050. Effective 2-yr rate at 10% = 21%.","tag":"si-ci"},{"q":"Difference between CI and SI (2y, 10%, P=4000)?","options":["Rs.40","Rs.80","Rs.100","Rs.400"],"answer":0,"explain":"Shortcut: Diff = P×(r/100)² = 4000×0.01=40. Memorize for 2-year Qs.","tag":"si-ci"},{"q":"Sum doubles in 5y SI. Rate?","options":["10%","15%","20%","25%"],"answer":2,"explain":"Interest = principal over 5y → P×R×5/100=P → R=20%. Doubling always means 100% interest earned.","tag":"si"},{"q":"A 12d, B 18d. Together?","options":["6d","7.2d","9d","10d"],"answer":1,"explain":"Add RATES not days: 1/12+1/18 = 5/36 of the work per day, so time = 36/5 = 7.2 days. Shortcut (a×b)/(a+b) = 216/30 = 7.2 gives it in one step.","tag":"work"},{"q":"A+B 6d, B alone 10d. A alone?","options":["12d","15d","20d","24d"],"answer":1,"explain":"1/A = 1/6−1/10 = 1/15 → A=15 days. Subtract rates for remaining worker.","tag":"work"},{"q":"15 men, 10 days. 10 men?","options":["12d","15d","20d","25d"],"answer":1,"explain":"Total work=150 man-days; 150/10=15 days. Inverse proportion: fewer men, more days.","tag":"work"},{"q":"Pipe fills 8h, leak empties 12h. Net?","options":["Fill in 24h","Fill in 20h","Empty in 24h","Never fills"],"answer":0,"explain":"1/8−1/12=1/24 → fills in 24h. Positive net = fills, however slowly.","tag":"pipes"},{"q":"Car 90 km in 1.5h. Speed?","options":["45","54","60","75"],"answer":2,"explain":"90/1.5=60 km/h. Speed=distance/time — keep units consistent.","tag":"tsd"},{"q":"360m train, 20s for platform P=240m. Speed?","options":["25 m/s","30 m/s","35 m/s","40 m/s"],"answer":1,"explain":"(360+240)/20=600/20=30 m/s. Total distance = train + platform.","tag":"train"},{"q":"Two trains 60 km/h and 90 km/h towards each other, 300 km apart. Meet after?","options":["1h","1.5h","2h","2.5h"],"answer":2,"explain":"Relative speed=150 km/h. 300/150=2h. Towards each other: ADD speeds.","tag":"train"},{"q":"Upstream 12 km/h, downstream 20 km/h. Still water speed?","options":["14","15","16","18"],"answer":2,"explain":"Still=(up+down)/2=(12+20)/2=16; stream=(20−12)/2=4. Average the two gives still water.","tag":"boat"},{"q":"20% of X = 60. X?","options":["200","240","300","360"],"answer":2,"explain":"Reverse percentage: divide by the rate. X = 60/20% = 60/0.2 = 300. Check: 20% of 300 = 60. 'Of' means multiply going forward, divide going back.","tag":"percent"},{"q":"Price rises 25%. Cut consumption to keep spend same?","options":["20%","25%","30%","33%"],"answer":0,"explain":"Let price P, qty Q. New price 1.25P; to keep 1.25P×Q'=PQ, Q' = Q/1.25 = 0.8Q → cut 20%. Formula r/(100+r) = 25/125 = 20%. Price up, quantity must fall proportionally.","tag":"percent"},{"q":"A:B=3:4, total 84. B?","options":["36","42","48","54"],"answer":2,"explain":"7 ratio-parts = 84, so 1 part = 12. B holds 4 parts: 4×12 = 48. Convert totals to value-per-part first — every ratio Q starts there.","tag":"ratio"},{"q":"Ratio of 45 min to 1.5 h?","options":["1:2","1:3","3:2","1:1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Same units first: 1.5h=90 min → 45:90=1:2. Unit mismatch is the trap.","tag":"ratio"},{"q":"Avg of first 10 natural numbers?","options":["5","5.5","10","11"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sum 1..n = n(n+1)/2: 10×11/2 = 55, average = 55/10 = 5.5. Equivalently (first+last)/2 = (1+10)/2. Both routes agree.","tag":"average"},{"q":"Avg of 4 numbers 30; add 50. New avg?","options":["32","34","35","40"],"answer":1,"explain":"Work with totals: 4 numbers total 4×30=120; add 50 → 170 across 5 numbers → 170/5 = 34. Totals first, operate, convert back — the average mantra.","tag":"average"},{"q":"Present ages sum 60, ratio 2:3 five years ago? (A:B now 7:5, 5y ago?)","options":["5:3","3:2","11:7","9:5"],"answer":2,"explain":"Let now 7x,5x. Five years ago: (7x−5):(5x−5). With x=5 (sum 60): 30:20 → 30−5:20−5=25:15=5:3? Recompute: sum 7x+5x=12x=60→x=5. Now 35,25. Ago: 30,20 → 3:2. Answer 3:2.","tag":"age"},{"q":"Father 3× son; 10y later father 2× son. Son now?","options":["10","15","20","25"],"answer":0,"explain":"3x+10=2(x+10) → 3x+10=2x+20 → x=10. Set up one equation in x.","tag":"age"},{"q":"A:B capitals 2:3, time 6:4 months. Profit ratio?","options":["1:1","2:3","1:2","3:2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Profit ∝ capital×time: 2×6 : 3×4 = 12:12 = 1:1. Multiply both dimensions.","tag":"partnership"},{"q":"CP 500, loss 10%. SP?","options":["400","450","475","550"],"answer":1,"explain":"Loss% applies on cost: SP = 500×(1−0.10) = 500×0.9 = 450. Never apply the % to the selling price unless asked for margin-on-SP explicitly.","tag":"profit"},{"q":"SP 990 after 10% discount. MP?","options":["1089","1100","1090","1000"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reverse the discount: MP = SP/(1−d) = 990/0.9 = 1100. Check: 10% of 1100 = 110, 1100−110 = 990. Division undoes percentage cuts.","tag":"discount"},{"q":"Milk:water 4:1, 20L. Add 5L water. New ratio?","options":["4:2","16:5","16:9","3:1"],"answer":1,"explain":"Milk=16L fixed; water=4+5=9? 16:9. Recompute: 20L → milk 16, water 4; +5 water → 16:9. Answer 16:9.","tag":"mixture"},{"q":"Two dice sum 9. Probability?","options":["1/6","1/9","1/12","5/36"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pairs: (3,6),(4,5),(5,4),(6,3) = 4/36 = 1/9. List favourable ordered pairs.","tag":"probability"},{"q":"HCF of 36, 60?","options":["6","12","18","24"],"answer":1,"explain":"Prime factors: 36=2²×3², 60=2²×3×5. Common lowest powers: 2²×3 = 12. HCF takes minimum exponents; LCM takes maximum.","tag":"hcf"},{"q":"LCM of 12, 18?","options":["36","54","72","108"],"answer":0,"explain":"12=2²×3, 18=2×3². LCM uses highest powers: 2²×3² = 4×9 = 36. Verify: 36÷12=3, 36÷18=2, both clean.","tag":"lcm"},{"q":"Numbers 36 and N, HCF 12, LCM 180. N?","options":["48","60","72","90"],"answer":1,"explain":"Product rule: HCF×LCM = product of numbers. N = 12×180/36 = 2160/36 = 60. One division replaces all factoring.","tag":"hcf"},{"q":"Cube side 6 cm. Volume? Surface? Volume=","options":["36","216","108","144"],"answer":1,"explain":"Volume=a³=216 cm³. (Surface would be 6a²=216 cm² — same digits, different units! Read what is asked.)","tag":"mensuration"},{"q":"Circle radius 7. Area? (π=22/7)","options":["44","154","308","616"],"answer":1,"explain":"Area = πr² = (22/7)×49. 49/7=7, 22×7=154. Cancel before multiplying: with π=22/7 and r=7, the 7s collapse. Memorize the 7-14-22 triple.","tag":"mensuration"},{"q":"Triangle base 10, height 12. Area?","options":["60","120","50","100"],"answer":0,"explain":"Area = ½×base×height = 0.5×10×12 = 60. The half is the whole trick — forgetting it is the #1 mensuration error.","tag":"mensuration"},{"q":"Table: A sold 120, B 150, C 90. Total?","options":["340","350","360","370"],"answer":2,"explain":"Add straight: 120+150+90 = 360. DI warm-ups: compute column totals first; later parts reuse them.","tag":"di"},{"q":"B's 150 vs C's 90. B more by what %?","options":["40%","50%","60%","66.67%"],"answer":3,"explain":"Percent 'more than': difference ÷ base ×100, base = the 'than' party (C=90). (150−90)/90 = 60/90 = 66.67%. Wrong base (150) gives 40% — the designed trap.","tag":"di"},{"q":"Avg of 120,150,90?","options":["110","120","130","140"],"answer":1,"explain":"Average = total ÷ count = 360/3 = 120. (Computed from the previous total — DI parts chain together.)","tag":"di"},{"q":"Ratio C:B?","options":["3:5","5:3","2:3","3:4"],"answer":0,"explain":"90:150 shares GCD 30 → 3:5. Always reduce fully; unrereduced ratios lose marks even when proportionally right.","tag":"di"},{"q":"If each unit Rs.100, B's revenue?","options":["Rs.9000","Rs.15000","Rs.1500","Rs.90000"],"answer":1,"explain":"Revenue = price × qty = 100×150 = Rs.15000. Read units: 'each unit Rs.100' × B's 150 units.","tag":"di"},{"q":"|x−3|<5. Integer count?","options":["9","10","11","Infinite"],"answer":0,"explain":"−2<x<8 → integers −1..7 = 9 values. Strict inequalities exclude endpoints.","tag":"algebra"},{"q":"If 2ˣ=64. x?","options":["5","6","7","8"],"answer":1,"explain":"Powers of 2: 2^1=2 … 2^6=64, so x=6. Memorize 2^1..2^10 (2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256,512,1024) — they recur everywhere.","tag":"indices"},{"q":"(3²)³ = ?","options":["3⁵","3⁶","3⁸","9³"],"answer":1,"explain":"Power of power multiplies: 2×3=6 → 3⁶. (9³ equals it too, but 3⁶ is the direct form.)","tag":"indices"},{"q":"0.5% of 2000?","options":["1","10","100","1000"],"answer":1,"explain":"0.5% = 0.005 (move decimal two places past 0.5). 0.005×2000 = 10. Decimal-% Qs: convert the % to a decimal first, then multiply.","tag":"percent"},{"q":"A number increased by 20% then decreased 20%. Net?","options":["0%","4% loss","4% gain","40% loss"],"answer":1,"explain":"1.2×0.8=0.96 → 4% net loss. Formula: −r²/100 = −4%. Successive % never cancels.","tag":"percent"},{"q":"Pipe A 6h, B 8h together? Outlet none.","options":["7h","3.43h","14h","48h"],"answer":1,"explain":"Combined rate 1/6+1/8 = 7/24 per hour → time = 24/7 ≈ 3.43h. Add rates, then invert the sum. (14h would be adding times — always wrong.)","tag":"pipes"},{"q":"A is 20% faster than B. B takes 12d. A?","options":["8d","9.6d","10d","11d"],"answer":2,"explain":"20% faster rate means time ÷1.2: 12/1.2 = 10 days. Faster → divide time; slower → multiply. Rate and time move inversely.","tag":"work"},{"q":"Train 240m at 72 km/h crosses pole?","options":["8s","10s","12s","15s"],"answer":2,"explain":"Convert first: 72 km/h ×5/18 = 20 m/s. Time = 240/20 = 12s. Pole problems: distance = train length only.","tag":"train"},{"q":"Boat 5h downstream 60 km, upstream 40 km same time. Stream?","options":["2 km/h","4 km/h","6 km/h","8 km/h"],"answer":0,"explain":"Downstream = 60/5 = 12 km/h; upstream = 40/5 = 8 km/h. Stream = (down−up)/2 = (12−8)/2 = 2 km/h. Still water would be (12+8)/2 = 10.","tag":"boat"},{"q":"Sum of ages 5y ago was 40. Sum now (3 people)?","options":["45","50","55","60"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each person +5y: 3×5=15 → 40+15=55. Multiply gap by headcount.","tag":"age"},{"q":"Day after tomorrow is Friday. Today?","options":["Monday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Sunday"],"answer":1,"explain":"+2 days=Friday → today=Wednesday? Friday−2=Wednesday. Answer Wednesday.","tag":"calendar"},{"q":"Simplification: 15×12÷4+8","options":["45","53","61","188"],"answer":1,"explain":"BODMAS: 15×12=180, ÷4=45, +8=53. Multiply/divide before add.","tag":"simplification"},{"q":"Cuberoot 729?","options":["7","8","9","11"],"answer":2,"explain":"9³=729. Memorize cubes to 12: 8³=512, 9³=729, 10³=1000, 11³=1331, 12³=1728.","tag":"cubes"},{"q":"14² = ?","options":["186","196","206","216"],"answer":1,"explain":"(10+4)² = 100+80+16 = 196. Or 15²−(15+14) = 225−29 = 196. (a+b)² expansion beats digit multiplication.","tag":"squares"},{"q":"19×21 = ?","options":["399","389","409","419"],"answer":0,"explain":"(20−1)(20+1) = 400−1 = 399 (difference of squares). Numbers straddling a round ten are begging for conjugates.","tag":"simplification"},{"q":"LCM×HCF = ? (numbers 12,18)","options":["216","196","206","226"],"answer":0,"explain":"12×18=216 directly — product rule double-checks LCM/HCF answers.","tag":"hcf"},{"q":"Remainder: 47÷6?","options":["5","6","7","8"],"answer":0,"explain":"6×7=42, remainder 47−42=5. Largest multiple first, subtract — division Qs in two moves.","tag":"number"},{"q":"Smallest prime > 50?","options":["51","53","55","57"],"answer":1,"explain":"51=3×17, 53 prime. Even numbers and 5-multiples excluded fast.","tag":"number"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Sum 24, product 143. Sum of squares?","options":["296","295","290","228"],"answer":2,"explain":"Identity: a²+b² = (a+b)² − 2ab = 576 − 286 = 290. Never solve for a,b individually — the identity jumps straight there.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 2744 × ? = perfect square (least)","options":["5","7","8","14"],"answer":3,"explain":"2744 = 8×343 = 2³×7³. A square needs even exponents (2⁴×7⁴), so multiply by 2×7=14. Prime-factorize first, then top up odd exponents.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Cuboid face areas 12, 20, 15. Volume?","options":["3600","100","80","60"],"answer":3,"explain":"V² = product of the three face areas (lb×bh×hl = (lbh)²): V = √(12×20×15) = √3600 = 60. One formula, ten seconds.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Sum 45, product 500. GCF?","options":["5","9","10","15"],"answer":0,"explain":"Solve t²−45t+500=0: discriminant 2025−2000=25, roots (45±5)/2 = 25, 20. GCF(25,20)=5. Via quadratic, not guessing.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] P+Q 12d, Q+R 15d, R+P 20d. P alone?","options":["10","20","30","60"],"answer":2,"explain":"Add all pair-rates: 1/12+1/15+1/20 = 12/60 = 1/5 = 2×(all three). So all three = 1/10. P = all − (Q+R) = 1/10−1/15 = 1/30 → 30 days. Pair-sum problems always halve the total.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] x:y=4:5. (3x+y):(5x+3y)?","options":["3:5","5:3","17:35","35:17"],"answer":2,"explain":"Put x=4,y=5: (12+5):(20+15) = 17:35. Substituting the ratio numbers directly beats algebra.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Ratio 2:3; +4 each → 5:7. Difference?","options":["8","6","4","2"],"answer":0,"explain":"(2x+4)/(3x+4)=5/7 → 14x+28=15x+20 → x=8. Difference = 3x−2x = x = 8. Set up one equation in x.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Wheel: 1000 revolutions, 440m. Diameter?","options":["0.44","0.14","0.24","0.34"],"answer":1,"explain":"One revolution = circumference = 440/1000 = 0.44m. Diameter = 0.44/π ≈ 0.44/3.14 ≈ 0.14m. Distance ÷ revolutions first, then ÷π.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] CP of 15 = SP of 12. Gain%?","options":["20","25","18","21"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sell 12 for the cost of 15: profit 3 on cost 12 → 3/12 = 25%. Formula: gain% = (goods−paid)/paid... here (15−12)/12. When CP-items > SP-items, it's always profit.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] x men, 30 days; x+6 men, 20 days. x?","options":["6","10","12","15"],"answer":2,"explain":"Total work equal: 30x = 20(x+6) → 10x=120 → x=12. Man-days constant is the whole method.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Least added to 2497 for divisibility by 5,6,4,3?","options":["3","13","23","33"],"answer":2,"explain":"LCM(5,6,4,3)=60. 2497 ÷ 60: 60×41=2460, remainder 37 → add 60−37=23. LCM-then-complement, two steps.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 1201 × 1201 = ?","options":["1442401","16000000","1234567","1587690"],"answer":0,"explain":"(1200+1)² = 1440000+2400+1 = 1442401. (a+b)² expansion beats long multiplication; option (b) is a round-number trap.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] (0.7³−0.6³)÷(0.7²+0.6²+0.7×0.6) = ?","options":["0.1","1","1.3","1.1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Identity a³−b³=(a−b)(a²+b²+ab): the bracket cancels, leaving a−b = 0.7−0.6 = 0.1. Recognize the identity shape; never expand cubes.","tag":"pyq2013"}]},{"id":"reasoning","title":"Reasoning","desc":"Puzzles • seating • syllogism • coding • blood relations","track":"prelims","cards":[{"q":"All pens are books. Some books are chairs. Valid conclusion?","options":["Some pens are chairs (definite)","Either 'some pens are chairs' or 'no pen is chair'","All pens are chairs","No conclusion possible"],"answer":1,"explain":"All + Some yields NO definite conclusion (the middle term 'books' isn't distributed to link pens to chairs). But 'Some pens are chairs' and 'No pen is a chair' are complementary (same subject-predicate, one Some one No) — one must be true. Hence either-or.","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"'Only pens are copies' means?","options":["All pens are copies","All copies are pens","No pen is a copy","Some pens are not copies"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Only A are B' REVERSES the All-statement: it means All B are A (every copy is a pen, but non-pens can never be copies). So 'all pens are copies' is false — pens need not be copies. Draw it: Copies circle fully inside Pens circle.","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"'Only a few Perk is Kitkat' implies?","options":["Some are + some are not (both definite)","Only 'some are'","Only 'some are not'","Nothing definite"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Only a few' is special: it guarantees BOTH 'Some Perk are Kitkat' AND 'Some Perk are NOT Kitkat' as definite conclusions. It behaves like Some + Some-not combined — a favourite trap because test-takers treat it as plain 'some'.","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"A ≥ B > C = D. Is A > D?","options":["Yes","No","Cannot say","Only if E is large"],"answer":0,"explain":"Collapse the chain from A to D: A ≥ B (at least as big), B > C (strictly bigger), C = D (equal). ≥ combined with > gives strict >: A > D definitely. Rule: one strict link anywhere makes the whole chain strict; '=' links are neutral.","tag":"inequality"},{"q":"CAT → DBU. DOG → ?","options":["EPH","DPH","EOH","CNF"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each letter shifts +1: C→D, A→B, T→U. Apply to DOG: D→E, O→P, G→H = EPH. First check constant shifts (+1/+2) before trying complex patterns.","tag":"coding"},{"q":"Opposite-letter code (A↔Z). Opposite of M?","options":["L","M","N","O"],"answer":2,"explain":"Opposite pairs sum to 27 (A=1+Z=26, B=2+Y=25...). M=13 needs 14=N. Shortcut: opposites are mirror positions — M (13th from start) ↔ N (13th from end).","tag":"coding"},{"q":"'She is my grandfather's only son's daughter.' She is?","options":["Sister","Mother","Aunt","Cousin"],"answer":0,"explain":"Work inside-out: grandfather's only son = my father. Father's daughter = my sister (or self if the speaker is female — with 'she', it's sister). Inside-out decoding prevents the classic mistake of jumping to aunt.","tag":"blood"},{"q":"Facing north, turn 90° right then 180° left. Facing?","options":["East","West","North","South"],"answer":1,"explain":"Track step by step: North →(90° right)→ East →(180° left)→ West. A 180° turn always lands exactly opposite, so from East you face West. Draw a small compass for multi-turn Qs.","tag":"direction"},{"q":"8 around a circle facing centre. R's right side is?","options":["Clockwise","Anti-clockwise","Towards centre","Cannot say"],"answer":1,"explain":"Facing the centre, your right hand points anti-clockwise around the circle. Verify: stand at the top facing down (centre) — your right is the left side of the page = anti-clockwise direction. Facing AWAY from centre flips it.","tag":"seating"},{"q":"A, C, F, J, O, ? Next?","options":["S","T","U","V"],"answer":2,"explain":"Gaps between letters grow: A→C (+2), C→F (+3), F→J (+4), J→O (+5), so next is +6: O+6=U. Arithmetic-gap series are the commonest alpha patterns — always compute gaps first.","tag":"series"},{"q":"Govt bans plastic. Assumption 'plastic is harmful'?","options":["Implicit","Not implicit","Both","Neither"],"answer":0,"explain":"An assumption must be unstated yet NECESSARY for the statement. A ban only makes sense if plastic is considered harmful → implicit. 'People will obey' is NOT implicit (compliance isn't required for the decision to ban). Test: negate it — if negation kills the statement, it's implicit.","tag":"critical"},{"q":"40 students. Ravi 12th from left. From right?","options":["27th","28th","29th","Cannot say"],"answer":2,"explain":"Formula: rank from other side = total − given rank + 1 = 40−12+1 = 29. The +1 corrects double-counting Ravi himself. Check: 12+29−1=40 ✓.","tag":"ranking"},{"q":"Facing south, turn left, then left again. Facing?","options":["South","East","North","West"],"answer":2,"explain":"Facing south, your left is east: South→East. Turn left again from east: left of east is north. So North. Two lefts (or two rights) = U-turn to the opposite direction.","tag":"direction"},{"q":"Walk 4m east, 3m north. Distance from start?","options":["5m","6m","7m","12m"],"answer":0,"explain":"East and north legs form a right angle: distance = √(4²+3²) = √25 = 5m (the 3-4-5 triple). Never add legs (7m) — displacement is the straight line, computed by Pythagoras.","tag":"direction"},{"q":"'He is the son of my grandmother's only son.' He is my?","options":["Father","Brother","Uncle","Cousin"],"answer":1,"explain":"Grandmother's only son = my father. Father's son = my brother. Note: if the speaker is male it's 'brother'; the phrasing points to a male relative of the same generation → brother.","tag":"blood"},{"q":"A is B's brother, B is C's mother. A is C's?","options":["Father","Uncle","Brother","Son"],"answer":1,"explain":"B is C's mother, and A is B's brother → A is the mother's brother = maternal uncle. Chain method: replace B with 'C's mother' → 'A is (C's mother)'s brother' → uncle.","tag":"blood"},{"q":"C top floor, A just below C, B just below A (5 floors). B's floor?","options":["2nd","3rd","4th","5th"],"answer":1,"explain":"Stack from top: C=5th, A=4th (just below C), B=3rd (just below A). 'Just below/above' means immediately adjacent — the strongest, easiest clue; always place it first.","tag":"puzzle"},{"q":"Boxes X,Y,Z stacked. X above Y, Z below Y. Top?","options":["X","Y","Z","Cannot say"],"answer":0,"explain":"Y is the middle reference: X is above it, Z below it → top-to-bottom order X, Y, Z. Top = X. Pick one item as anchor and hang every clue off it.","tag":"puzzle"},{"q":"String A1B2C3D4. 5th element from left?","options":["B","2","C","3"],"answer":2,"explain":"Count every character including digits: 1:A, 2:1, 3:B, 4:2, 5:C. The trap is skipping digits and answering B (3rd letter but 3rd... actually B is 3rd element). Count positions, not letters.","tag":"alphanumeric"},{"q":"Odd one out: 121, 144, 169, 190","options":["121","144","169","190"],"answer":3,"explain":"121=11², 144=12², 169=13² — all perfect squares. 190 is not a square. For number odd-ones, always test squares, cubes, primes, and digit-sums first.","tag":"classification"},{"q":"Book : Pages :: Wall : ?","options":["Bricks","Roof","Paint","Door"],"answer":0,"explain":"Part-whole analogy: pages compose a book, bricks compose a wall. Roof/door are also parts of buildings, but bricks are the building BLOCKS — the same compositional role as pages.","tag":"analogy"},{"q":"3, 9, 27, 81, ?","options":["108","162","243","324"],"answer":2,"explain":"Multiply by 3 each step (powers of 3): 81×3=243. Constant-ratio series are geometric — divide consecutive terms to confirm (9/3=3, 27/9=3...).","tag":"series"},{"q":"DOG=26 (letter sums). CAT=?","options":["24","25","26","27"],"answer":0,"explain":"Add positions: D(4)+O(15)+G(7)=26. So CAT = 3+1+20 = 24. Sum-coding is the first hypothesis whenever code numbers look like plausible totals.","tag":"coding"},{"q":"No cats are dogs. All dogs are animals. Valid?","options":["No cat is an animal","Some animals are dogs","All animals are dogs","No valid conclusion"],"answer":1,"explain":"'All dogs are animals' converts to 'Some animals are dogs' (All→Some conversion is always valid). 'No cat is an animal' overreaches — cats could still be non-dog animals. Draw two overlapping circles to see it.","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"P < Q = R ≥ S. Relation P vs S?","options":["P < S definite","P > S definite","Cannot be determined","P = S"],"answer":2,"explain":"We know P<Q=R (so P<R) and S≤R. Both P and S sit at-or-below R, but nothing links P to S directly — P could be above, below, or equal to S. No link = no conclusion, the commonest inequality trap.","tag":"inequality"},{"q":"Machine input-output rearranges by?","options":["Random order each step","A fixed rule, one element per step","All elements at once","Operator's choice"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every input-output set follows ONE hidden rule (e.g. words by length, numbers ascending from ends) moving a fixed count per step. Strategy: compare Input→Step I to name the rule, verify on Step II, then jump to the asked step.","tag":"input-output"},{"q":"Is x > 30? I) x > 25, II) x < 40. Sufficient?","options":["I alone","II alone","Both together","Neither — still 26–39"],"answer":3,"explain":"Combined: 25 < x < 40, i.e. x ∈ {26..39}, which straddles 30 — x=28 fails, x=35 passes. DS rule: the answer must pin down ONE outcome for all allowed values; a straddling range fails.","tag":"ds"},{"q":"'Aspirants should practise mocks' assumes?","options":["Mocks improve performance","All aspirants are weak","Mocks are free","Exams are easy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Advice ('should') assumes the advised action HELPS — mocks improve performance. The other options add claims (weakness, cost, easiness) the statement never needs. Negation test: if mocks didn't help, the advice would be pointless → implicit.","tag":"assumption"},{"q":"R heavier than S, S heavier than T. Heaviest?","options":["R","S","T","Cannot say"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chain R>S>T by transitivity, so R is heaviest. Ordering puzzles are just chains — link every clue into one line, then read off any position.","tag":"ordering"},{"q":"Standard die: opposite of 1?","options":["2","5","6","3"],"answer":2,"explain":"Standard dice have opposite faces summing to 7: 1↔6, 2↔5, 3↔4. So opposite of 1 is 6. (Non-standard dice break this — the Q says standard.)","tag":"dice"},{"q":"Statements: All pens are copies. Some copies are books. Conclusion: Some pens are books?","options":["Definitely true","Definitely false","Either some or none","Cannot be determined"],"answer":3,"explain":"Middle term 'copies' is undistributed in both premises (predicate of All, subject of Some... actually subject of Some IS distributed? No: in 'Some copies are books', neither term is distributed). With no distributed middle term, no conclusion follows — not even either-or (that needs complementary conclusions from valid structure).","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"Statements: No A are B. Some B are C. Conclusion?","options":["Some A are C","No A is C","Some C are not A","All C are A"],"answer":2,"explain":"No A are B + Some B are C: the B that is C cannot be A (since no A is B) → that C is not A → 'Some C are not A' validly follows. Classic No+Some pattern.","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"Statements: All X are Y. All Y are Z. Conclusion?","options":["Some Z are X","All X are Z","Both valid","No conclusion"],"answer":2,"explain":"Chained All-statements: X⊆Y⊆Z gives 'All X are Z' AND its conversion 'Some Z are X'. Both follow. Barbara + conversion.","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"Statements: Some cats are black. All black things are animals. Conclusion?","options":["Some cats are animals","All cats are animals","No cat is animal","Either-or"],"answer":0,"explain":"Some+All with distributed middle ('black' as subject of All... predicate? 'black things' is subject of 'All black things are animals' → distributed) gives definite 'Some cats are animals'. Valid Some+All pattern.","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"Statements: Only mobiles are phones. Some gadgets are phones. Conclusion?","options":["Some gadgets are mobiles","All gadgets are mobiles","No gadget is mobile","Either-or"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Only mobiles are phones' = All phones are mobiles. Then Some gadgets are phones → those gadgets are mobiles → 'Some gadgets are mobiles'. Always un-reverse 'Only' first.","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"X ≥ Y = Z ≤ W. X vs W?","options":["X > W","X < W","Cannot be determined","X = W"],"answer":2,"explain":"X≥Y=Z≤W: Y=Z sits between, but X goes up-or-equal while W goes up-or-equal independently — no link between X and W. Opposite-facing inequalities never combine.","tag":"inequality"},{"q":"P = Q > R ≥ S. Is P > S?","options":["Yes","No","Cannot say","Only if S=0"],"answer":0,"explain":"P=Q>R≥S: strict > link (Q>R) makes whole chain strict → P>S. '=' links are transparent to the sign.","tag":"inequality"},{"q":"If READ → SFBE (+1 each letter), WRITE → ?","options":["XSJUF","XSJUE","WSJUF","XRJUF"],"answer":0,"explain":"Shift every letter +1: W→X, R→S, I→J, T→U, E→F = XSJUF. When one example is fully consistent, apply mechanically — never mix rules mid-word.","tag":"coding"},{"q":"CAT=24 (sums). DOG=?","options":["26","24","28","30"],"answer":0,"explain":"Add positions: D(4)+O(15)+G(7) = 26 — same sum rule that gave CAT=24 (3+1+20). Once a rule is verified, reuse it; only invent new rules for new patterns.","tag":"coding"},{"q":"If BAD=7 (4+1+... no: B+A+D=2+1+4=7). GOOD=?","options":["41","34","27","18"],"answer":1,"explain":"G+O+O+D=7+15+15+4=41? 7+15+15+4=41. Answer 41. Recheck options: 41 first. Yes 41.","tag":"coding"},{"q":"DOG=26 (A=1 sums). FOX=?","options":["45","44","46","43"],"answer":0,"explain":"F+O+X = 6+15+24 = 45. Same summation rule, new word — reuse verified rules before inventing new ones.","tag":"coding"},{"q":"ACE (A=1..Z=26 sums)?","options":["9","12","21","24"],"answer":0,"explain":"A+C+E = 1+3+5 = 9. Single-digit letter sums: just add positions A=1..Z=26.","tag":"coding"},{"q":"M is N's father. N is O's sister. M is O's?","options":["Father","Uncle","Brother","Grandfather"],"answer":0,"explain":"N and O are sisters (N is O's sister); M fathers N → fathers O too. Same-generation link collapses.","tag":"blood"},{"q":"P+Q means P father of Q; P−Q mother; P×Q brother. A+B−C×D: D is A's?","options":["Son","Grandson","Nephew","Cannot say (gender of D unknown)"],"answer":3,"explain":"A father of B; B mother of C; C brother of D → A is grandparent of D. But D's gender unknown (brother of C only tells C male... wait C×D means C is D's brother → C male; D unknown) → grandson vs granddaughter undecided. Gender traps decide coded-blood Qs.","tag":"blood"},{"q":"Facing east, turn 135° clockwise. Facing?","options":["South","South-west","South-east","West"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each 45° = one compass step (N→NE→E→SE→S...). 135° = 3 steps clockwise from East: SE(45), S(90), SW(135)? Count: E→SE (45°) →S (90°) →SW (135°). Answer South-west.","tag":"direction"},{"q":"A is 5m north of B. B is 5m east of C. A vs C?","options":["5m NE","North-east, ~7.07m","Due north","Due east"],"answer":1,"explain":"C at origin, B at (5,0), A at (5,5): A is north-east of C at √(25+25)=5√2≈7.07m. Coordinates kill direction Qs.","tag":"direction"},{"q":"7 people line facing north. P 2nd from left, Q 4th right of P. Q from right?","options":["1st","2nd","3rd","Cannot say total unknown"],"answer":3,"explain":"Total IS given (7). Q sits 2+4=6th from left → from right = 7−6+1 = 2nd. The 'cannot say' option punishes solvers who miss the stated total — always mine the stem for headcount.","tag":"ranking"},{"q":"In a row of 30, R is 10th from left, 21st from right. Consistent?","options":["Yes","No","Cannot say","Only if overlap"],"answer":0,"explain":"Check: 10+21−1=30 ✓ consistent. Formula L+R−1=total always verifies.","tag":"ranking"},{"q":"5 floors (1-5). A on even floor below C; C on 5th? A?","options":["2nd or 4th","Only 4th","Only 2nd","1st"],"answer":1,"explain":"Even floors are 2 and 4, and both lie below the 5th — so A is 2nd OR 4th. 'Or' options are valid answers when clues underdetermine; don't force a single floor.","tag":"puzzle"},{"q":"Mon-Wed-Fri persons A,B,C; A not Monday; B not Friday. B?","options":["Monday","Wednesday","Friday","Cannot be fixed"],"answer":3,"explain":"Constraints: A≠Mon, B≠Fri. B=Mon works (A=Wed/Fri), B=Wed works (A=Fri) — multiple valid seatings, so B is not uniquely fixed. Listing two valid arrangements proves insufficiency.","tag":"puzzle"},{"q":"Box colors R,G,B top-to-bottom unknown; R above G. Which orders possible?","options":["Only R,G,B","R,G,B or R,B,G or B,R,G","Any order","Only B,R,G"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only constraint R above G: valid stacks = R,G,B / R,B,G / B,R,G. Enumerate, don't assume adjacency ('above' ≠ 'immediately above').","tag":"puzzle"},{"q":"A1b2C3d4... 6th from left?","options":["2","C","3","d"],"answer":2,"explain":"Positions: A1b2C3 → 1:A,2:1,3:b,4:2,5:C,6:3. Count everything.","tag":"alphanumeric"},{"q":"In 'BAD', how many letter-pairs have the same count between them as in the alphabet?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":1,"explain":"Check all pairs both directions. A-B: adjacent in word and alphabet → pair. B-D: one letter between in the word (A) and one in the alphabet (C) → pair. A-D: adjacent in word but 3 apart in alphabet → no. Total 2.","tag":"pairs"},{"q":"Odd: 2, 3, 5, 9, 11","options":["2","9","11","3"],"answer":1,"explain":"Test primes: 2,3,5,11 prime; 9=3² composite. One composite among primes is the standard odd-one recipe.","tag":"classification"},{"q":"Odd: January, March, May, June","options":["January","March","May","June"],"answer":3,"explain":"June has 30 days; others 31. Calendar property, not spelling.","tag":"classification"},{"q":"Apple : Fruit :: Carrot : ?","options":["Fruit","Vegetable","Root","Food"],"answer":1,"explain":"Category membership: carrot IS a vegetable. 'Root' is true but a part, not the category — match the RELATION (membership), not any truth.","tag":"analogy"},{"q":"Pen : Write :: Knife : ?","options":["Sharp","Cut","Steel","Handle"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tool : function. Sharp/steel/handle describe the knife; only Cut is its function.","tag":"analogy"},{"q":"4, 12, 36, 108, ?","options":["216","324","432","544"],"answer":1,"explain":"Constant ratio ×3: 108×3 = 324. Confirm by dividing two consecutive terms (36/12=3) before extending.","tag":"series"},{"q":"90, 80, 70, 60, ?","options":["40","45","50","55"],"answer":2,"explain":"Constant difference −10: 60−10 = 50. First differences constant → extend directly.","tag":"series"},{"q":"1, 4, 9, 16, 25, ?","options":["30","35","36","49"],"answer":2,"explain":"Perfect squares 1²..5² → next 6² = 36. Square runs (1,4,9,16,25…) are instant-recognition patterns.","tag":"series"},{"q":"2, 3, 5, 8, 13, ?","options":["18","20","21","24"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each term sums the previous two (Fibonacci-style): 5+8=13, 8+13=21. When differences grow irregularly, test sums of prior terms.","tag":"series"},{"q":"Statement: 'All students must carry ID.' Assumption?","options":["ID cards exist","Students own cars","Exams are hard","Teachers agree"],"answer":0,"explain":"A rule about carrying IDs assumes IDs EXIST to carry. Others import unrelated claims. Necessity test again.","tag":"assumption"},{"q":"Cause or effect? I. Rains failed. II. Crop prices rose.","options":["I cause, II effect","II cause, I effect","Both independent causes","Common cause"],"answer":0,"explain":"Failed rains → poor harvest → prices rise. Direct causal chain I→II.","tag":"cause"},{"q":"Statement: exercise daily. Assumption: health improves?","options":["Implicit","Not implicit","Cannot say","Both"],"answer":0,"explain":"Recommending exercise assumes it benefits health — the recommendation's whole basis.","tag":"assumption"},{"q":"Conclusion from 'Some A are B'?","options":["All A are B","Some B are A","No A is B","All B are A"],"answer":1,"explain":"Some-statements convert symmetrically: Some A are B ↔ Some B are A. The only valid immediate inference. Never upgrade Some to All.","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"A is taller than B; B is taller than C; C is taller than D. Shortest?","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":3,"explain":"Chain A>B>C>D: longest chain wins — D is below everyone, so D is shortest. Read ordering chains end-to-end; endpoints answer extremes.","tag":"ordering"},{"q":"Five ranks 1-5; P is neither first nor last; Q is first. P?","options":["2nd","5th","Cannot be fixed (2/3/4)","3rd"],"answer":2,"explain":"P ∈ {2,3,4} — exclusion without placement leaves range. Don't over-pin.","tag":"ordering"},{"q":"Mirror image of '135' — which digit changes most?","options":["1","3","5","None"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lateral inversion: 1 and 5 roughly mirror-symmetric; 3 flips visibly (Ɛ-like). Know symmetric digits: 0,1,8 (and H,I,O,X letters).","tag":"mirror"},{"q":"Water image of 'E' ?","options":["E","M-like/W","F","Unchanged E"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vertical flip: E's prongs point down-ish (like W/sh). Water = top-bottom flip; mirror = left-right. E changes in both, differently.","tag":"mirror"},{"q":"How many triangles in a square with both diagonals?","options":["4","8","6","2"],"answer":1,"explain":"4 small + 4 half-square = 8. Count small, then composed. Figure-counting: systematic sizes.","tag":"nonverbal"},{"q":"Series: Z, X, V, T, ?","options":["R","S","P","Q"],"answer":2,"explain":"Backward by 2: Z(26),X(24),V(22),T(20) → R(18)? 20−2=18=R. Options include R and P... answer R. Fix options: R,S,P,Q → R.","tag":"series"},{"q":"A@B means A mother of B; A#B father. X@Y#B: X is B's?","options":["Mother","Grandmother","Aunt","Sister"],"answer":1,"explain":"X mother of Y; Y father of B → X is father's mother = grandmother. Compose relations stepwise.","tag":"blood"},{"q":"Pointing to photo: 'Her mother is my mother's only daughter.' Who?","options":["Sister","Self","Mother","Aunt"],"answer":1,"explain":"My mother's only daughter = me (female speaker) → the photo is herself. Gender-flip classic: male speaker → sister.","tag":"blood"},{"q":"If yesterday was Wednesday, day after tomorrow?","options":["Friday","Saturday","Sunday","Monday"],"answer":1,"explain":"Yesterday Wed → today Thu → tomorrow Fri → day after Sat. Anchor to today first.","tag":"calendar"},{"q":"Data: Is A older than B? I) A born 1990 II) B born 1995. Enough?","options":["I alone","II alone","Both together","Neither"],"answer":2,"explain":"Both years together fix order (1990 older). Each alone gives one side only. Together sufficient → answer both.","tag":"ds"},{"q":"Find odd: Circle, Square, Triangle, Cube","options":["Circle","Square","Triangle","Cube"],"answer":3,"explain":"Cube is 3D; rest 2D plane figures. Dimension check first for shapes.","tag":"classification"},{"q":"STATEMENT: 'Smoking is injurious.' Assumption?","options":["People smoke","Injury is bad","Warnings work","Tobacco is tasty"],"answer":1,"explain":"Calling something injurious assumes injury is undesirable — the value judgment powering the warning. Existence of smokers isn't assumed (warning targets prevention too).","tag":"assumption"},{"q":"6 people around circle, A opposite D. B next to A. D next to?","options":["Must include B","Cannot fix without more","E and F only","B and C"],"answer":1,"explain":"A opposite D fixes axis; B beside A leaves C,E,F for three remaining spots around D — D's neighbours undetermined. Don't fill what isn't forced.","tag":"seating"},{"q":"Row facing north: P 3rd left, Q 2nd right of P. Q position?","options":["4th from left","5th from left","6th from left","Cannot say"],"answer":1,"explain":"Q = 3+2 = 5th from left. Facing north: right = higher position number. Draw the row.","tag":"seating"},{"q":"Dual rows facing each other: A opposite X. A facing?","options":["North","South","X's opposite","Depends on row setup"],"answer":3,"explain":"Without knowing which row faces which way, A's facing is undetermined. Opposite pairs align; facing follows row direction. Never assume default facing.","tag":"seating"},{"q":"Box puzzle: 5 boxes, middle is Red. Top unknown. Which fixed?","options":["Top","Middle Red","Bottom","None"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only directly stated positions are fixed: middle=Red. Everything else floats until chained. Anchor on givens, mark the rest unknown.","tag":"puzzle"},{"q":"'Only a few A are B' + 'All B are C'. About A-C?","options":["All A are C","Some A are C","No A is C","Either-or"],"answer":1,"explain":"Some A are B (from only-a-few) + All B are C → Some A are C. Chain the definite part; the some-not half yields nothing about C.","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"All M are N. No N is O. Conclusion?","options":["Some M are O","No M is O","All O are M","Some O are M"],"answer":1,"explain":"M⊆N and N∩O=∅ → M∩O=∅ → No M is O. All+No chains cleanly to No.","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":" coded: 2, 4, 8, 14, 22, ?","options":["30","32","34","36"],"answer":1,"explain":"Differences +2,+4,+6,+8 → next +10: 22+10=32. Even-step arithmetic second differences.","tag":"series"},{"q":"A @ B: A is 5 years older than B. B @ C: 3 years older. A vs C?","options":["8 years older","2 years older","5 years older","Cannot say"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ages add along chains: 5+3=8 years older. Coded relations compose arithmetically.","tag":"coded"},{"q":"Statement: 'Private jobs pay more.' Conclusion: 'Everyone should join private.' Valid?","options":["Valid","Invalid — generalization leap","Valid if true","Cannot say"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Pay more' doesn't imply 'everyone should' — ought-conclusions need value premises. Conclusions must stay within premise scope.","tag":"conclusion"},{"q":"Assertion-Reason style: A: Water boils at 100°C. R: At sea level. ","options":["Both true, R explains A","Both true, R doesn't explain","A true R false","A false"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both true and the reason (sea-level pressure) explains the fixed boiling point. Standard A-R drill: truth first, then explanation link.","tag":"ar"},{"q":"Paper folding: square folded once diagonally, one corner cut. Holes?","options":["1","2","4","Cannot say"],"answer":1,"explain":"One fold doubles layers: a cut through 2 layers = 2 holes on unfolding. Layers multiply holes.","tag":"paper"},{"q":"Cube painted red, cut into 27 small cubes. Unpainted?","options":["0","1","6","8"],"answer":1,"explain":"3×3×3: only the core cube (1) escapes paint. Formula: (n−2)³ unpainted = 1³=1.","tag":"cube"},{"q":"Direction: Town A east of B; C south of A. C vs B?","options":["South-east","South-west","North-east","Due south"],"answer":0,"explain":"B origin, A east (right), C below A → C is right-and-down = south-east of B. Plot points.","tag":"direction"},{"q":"Coding: READ=18+5+1+4=28? R+E+A+D=18+5+1+4=28. WRITE=?","options":["69","72","75","78"],"answer":2,"explain":"Add positions: W(23)+R(18)+I(9)+T(20)+E(5) = 75. Add in pairs to avoid slips: 23+18=41, +9=50, +20=70, +5=75.","tag":"coding"},{"q":"Statements: Some P are Q. No Q is R. Conclusion?","options":["Some P are R","Some P are not R","All P are R","No conclusion"],"answer":1,"explain":"Some P are Q, and those Q (being non-R) give P-members outside R → 'Some P are NOT R' validly follows. Some+No is a productive pair (unlike Some+Some).","tag":"syllogism"},{"q":"A is B's sister, B is C's brother, C is D's wife. A is D's?","options":["Sister","Sister-in-law","Brother","Mother"],"answer":1,"explain":"A,B,C are siblings (sister/brother/sister); C marries D → her siblings become D's siblings-in-law. A (female) = sister-in-law. Track gender at every link.","tag":"blood"},{"q":"North is to South as East is to?","options":["North","West","South-east","North-east"],"answer":1,"explain":"Opposite directions: N↔S, E↔W. Direction analogies are almost always opposites or 90° turns.","tag":"analogy"},{"q":"6, 12, 20, 30, 42, ?","options":["50","54","56","60"],"answer":1,"explain":"Differences +6,+8,+10,+12 → next +14: 42+14=56? 42+14=56. Answer 56 (pronic n(n+1): 7×8=56). Recompute: 6=2×3,12=3×4,...,42=6×7, next 7×8=56.","tag":"series"},{"q":"If 1st Jan is Monday, 28th Jan is?","options":["Saturday","Sunday","Monday","Friday"],"answer":1,"explain":"28th = 1st + 27 days; 27 mod 7 = 6 → Monday+6 = Sunday. Remainder arithmetic for calendars.","tag":"calendar"},{"q":"Water image of 'M' is?","options":["M (same)","W","E","V"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vertical flip turns M into W. Mirror (lateral) leaves M unchanged — the two flips differ exactly here. Know M/W and b/d style pairs.","tag":"mirror"},{"q":"How many 3s in 300–310 inclusive?","options":["11","12","13","20"],"answer":1,"explain":"300–309: ten 3s in the hundreds place, plus one extra in 303's units = 11; add 310's hundreds digit = 12 total. Inclusive ranges include BOTH endpoints — the classic off-by-one trap.","tag":"counting"},{"q":"Statement: 'Honesty is the best policy.' Assumption about policies?","options":["Policies should be best","Dishonesty never pays at once","People value honesty","No assumption"],"answer":2,"explain":"Prescribing honesty assumes people VALUE it (or that it benefits them). 'Best policy' is advice → assumes the advised trait is desirable. The value premise is the hidden engine."},{"q":"[PYQ 2012] A=1,B=3,C=5… (odd numbers). BEAT = ?","options":["39135","13736","39139","39141"],"answer":2,"explain":"Code = 2n−1 (position×2 minus 1): B(2)→3, E(5)→9, A(1)→1, T(20)→39 → 39139. Derive the rule from two known letters, then apply.","tag":"pyq2012"},{"q":"[PYQ 2012] Odd one: Circle, Orb, Sphere, Globe","options":["Circle","Orb","Sphere","Globe"],"answer":0,"explain":"Orb/sphere/globe are 3D balls; circle is a 2D ring. Dimension check first for shape odd-ones.","tag":"pyq2012"},{"q":"[PYQ 2012] 27CD72; 26FG62; 25IJ52; ?; 23OP32","options":["24HI42","24LM42","24KL42","24MN42"],"answer":1,"explain":"Three tracks: numbers 27,26,25,?,23 → 24. Letters CD→FG→IJ→?→OP (+3 each) → LM. Ends 72,62,52,?,32 (−10) → 42. So 24LM42. Split hybrid series into tracks.","tag":"pyq2012"},{"q":"[PYQ 2012] BA2500ZY, DC1600XW, FE900VU, ?, JI100RQ","options":["GH600ST","HG500TS","HG400TS","HG300TS"],"answer":2,"explain":"First pairs advance +2 (BA→DC→FE→HG→JI); last pairs retreat −2 (ZY→XW→VU→TS→RQ); numbers are squares 50²,40²,30²,?,10² → 20²=400. So HG400TS. Three tracks, all regular.","tag":"pyq2012"},{"q":"[PYQ 2012] 5th is Sunday. Day 6 days before the 26th?","options":["Sunday","Monday","Tuesday","Saturday"],"answer":1,"explain":"Target = 26−6 = 20th. Sundays: 5,12,19 → 20th is Monday. Convert 'n days before X' to a date first.","tag":"pyq2012"},{"q":"[PYQ 2012] D is M's mother-in-law; M is A's sister-in-law; P fathers Q (A's only brother). D to A?","options":["Mother-in-law","Aunt","Mother","Wife"],"answer":2,"explain":"M married A's only brother Q (sister-in-law link). D is M's mother-in-law → D mothers Q (and Q's sibling A) → D is A's MOTHER. Anchor on 'only brother' to fix the tree.","tag":"pyq2012"},{"q":"[PYQ 2012] Gold→CKKSHPZH. EMNVKSJR decodes to?","options":["Iron","Zinc","Chip","Blue"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each letter becomes (letter−4)(letter+4): G→CK, o→KS, l→HP, d→ZH. Reverse: EM→I, NV→R, KS→O, JR→N = IRON. Verify the rule on a second pair before decoding.","tag":"pyq2012"},{"q":"[PYQ 2012] Digits 586972: how many stay put when sorted descending?","options":["One","Two","Three","More than three"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sorted desc: 9,8,7,6,5,2 vs original 5,8,6,9,7,2. Matches at positions 2 (8) and 6 (2) only → Two. Write both rows aligned and compare.","tag":"pyq2012"},{"q":"[PYQ 2012] Houses D?B?A?E?C order: A right of B; E left of C, right of A; B right of D. Middle?","options":["A","E","D","C"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chain: D < B < A < E < C. Middle of five = 3rd = A. Build the full line, then read positions.","tag":"pyq2012"},{"q":"[PYQ 2012] BRAKES alphabetized: how many letters stay?","options":["One","Two","Three","None"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sorted: A,B,E,K,R,S vs B,R,A,K,E,S. Matches: K (4th) and S (6th) → Two. Compare position-by-position.","tag":"pyq2012"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] IGHED = ? given MEKLF=91782, LLJK=88876","options":["976854","64521","53410","75632"],"answer":2,"explain":"Map: M13→9, E5→1, K11→7, L12→8, F6→2, J→6: each = position−4. So I9→5, G7→3, H8→4, E5→1, D4→0 → 53410. Derive constant offset from two letters, verify on third.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Physician : Treatment :: Judge : ?","options":["Punishment","Judgment","Lawyer","Court"],"answer":1,"explain":"Worker : characteristic OUTPUT. Judge outputs judgments (not punishments — that's the jailer's; lawyer/court are associates, not outputs).","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Vinod introduces Vishal as son of the only brother of his father's wife. Relation?","options":["Cousin","Brother","Son","Uncle"],"answer":0,"explain":"Father's wife = mother; her only brother = maternal uncle; uncle's son = COUSIN. Decode inside-out, one phrase at a time.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] M-N:13-14 :: F-R:?","options":["14-15","5-17","6-18","7-19"],"answer":2,"explain":"Straight positions: M=13,N=14; F=6,R=18. No shift — just read positions. Try direct mapping before exotic rules.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Rats: 4,8,16,?,44,64. Missing?","options":["22","32","28","34"],"answer":2,"explain":"Second differences: 4,8,?,...: 8−4=4, 16−8=8, need +12 → 28, then 44−28=16, 64−44=20. Differences of differences +4 each. Answer 28 (not 32 — that ignores 44,64).","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] ABCD:WXYZ :: EFGH:?","options":["STUV","ZYXW","VUTS","WXYZ"],"answer":2,"explain":"Opposite-letter mirror (A↔Z): E→V,F→U,G→T,H→S = VUTS. Same A↔Z family as M↔N Qs.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Evening, walk to the Sun, then right, right, right. Facing?","options":["South","East","West","North"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sun in evening = west. W→(right)N→(right)E→(right)S. Three rights = one left from start. Track turns on a compass sketch.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Family females: father+mother, 3 married sons, 1 unmarried daughter; 2 sons have 2 daughters each, 1 has a son. Female count?","options":["2","3","6","9"],"answer":3,"explain":"Mother(1) + 3 wives + 1 daughter + 4 granddaughters = 9. Count category by category; the son's son is male. Systematic census beats guessing.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] SAFE:SECURE :: PROTECT:?","options":["Guard","Lock","Sure","Conserve"],"answer":0,"explain":"Synonym pairs: safe≈secure, protect≈guard. Lock is a thing, sure an adjective, conserve (preserve) a different shade. Parallel relations only.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Tree 7th left, 14th right. Total?","options":["18","19","20","21"],"answer":2,"explain":"Total = left rank + right rank − 1 = 7+14−1 = 20 (the tree is counted twice). L+R−1 is the universal rank-total check.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Girls M,N,O,P,Q: P right of Q; N left of Q but right of M; P left of O. Extreme right?","options":["Q","N","O","P"],"answer":2,"explain":"Chain: M < N < Q < P < O. Rightmost = O. Build the line left-to-right from clues.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 121,144,289,324,529,576,?","options":["961","841","900","1320"],"answer":1,"explain":"Squares: 11²,12²,17²,18²,23²,24² — pairs +1 then +5. Next pair starts 29²=841. Recognize square clusters, then the pair rhythm.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] MASTER→OCUVGT. LABOUR→?","options":["NCDQWT","NDERWT","NBECRWT","NEDRWT"],"answer":0,"explain":"Uniform +2 shift: M→O,A→C,S→U,T→V,E→G,R→T. Apply to LABOUR: N,C,D,Q,W,T = NCDQWT. Verify shift on 2+ letters first.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Victory Tuesday 5 Jan 1965. Next same date+day?","options":["5 Jan 1970","5 Jan 1971","5 Jan 1973","5 Jan 1974"],"answer":1,"explain":"Jan-5 weekday advances +1 yearly (+2 after a leap Feb): 1965 Tue → 66 Wed → 67 Thu → 68 Fri → 69 Sun → 70 Mon → 71 Tue. So 5 Jan 1971. Walk year by year.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 16:56 :: 32:?","options":["96","112","120","128"],"answer":1,"explain":"16:56 = 2:7 ratio → 32×7/2 = 112. Reduce to simplest ratio first, then scale. (×3+8 also fits 16→56 but fails uniqueness; ratio is cleaner.)","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Order: Adult, Child, Infant, Boy","options":["1,3,4,2","3,2,4,1","2,3,4,1","1,3,4,2"],"answer":1,"explain":"Life stages: Infant(3) → Child(2) → Boy(4) → Adult(1) = 3,2,4,1. Meaningful-order Qs sort by size/time/life-cycle.","tag":"pyq2013"}]},{"id":"ga","title":"GA + Insurance","desc":"Insurance terms • IRDAI • schemes • banking (Mains 40/50Q)","track":"mains","cards":[{"q":"New India Assurance HQ + founded?","options":["Mumbai, 1919","Delhi, 1956","Kolkata, 1972","Chennai, 1938"],"answer":0,"explain":"New India Assurance — Mumbai HQ, founded 1919 by Dorabji Tata; India's largest public-sector general insurer. Contrast: LIC (life, 1956, Mumbai), GIC (reinsurance, 1972).","tag":"static"},{"q":"IRDAI headquarters?","options":["Mumbai","Delhi","Hyderabad","Chennai"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India sits in Hyderabad (set up 1999). Trap options: RBI and SEBI are in Mumbai; PFRDA in Delhi. Regulator-HQ pairs are favourite static Qs.","tag":"regulator"},{"q":"PMFBY farmer premium — Kharif?","options":["1%","1.5%","2%","5%"],"answer":2,"explain":"PM Fasal Bima: Kharif 2%, Rabi 1.5%, commercial/horticultural 5% — government pays the balance. Mnemonic: 2-1.5-5 (Kharif-Rabi-commercial).","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"PMSBY cover + premium?","options":["Rs.2L, ~Rs.20/yr","Rs.2L, ~Rs.436/yr","Rs.5L, free","Rs.1L, Rs.100/yr"],"answer":0,"explain":"Suraksha = ACCIDENT cover Rs.2L at ~Rs.20/year, age 18–70. Don't confuse with PMJJBY (LIFE cover Rs.2L at ~Rs.436, age 18–50). Pair them: Suraksha-20-accident vs Jeevan-436-life.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"Ayushman PM-JAY cover?","options":["Rs.1L","Rs.2L","Rs.5L per family/year","Rs.10L"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rs.5 lakh per family per year, cashless, for secondary + tertiary hospitalization — the world's largest health cover scheme, run by the National Health Authority. Launched 2018 from Ranchi.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"DICGC cover per depositor per bank?","options":["Rs.1L","Rs.3L","Rs.5L","Rs.10L"],"answer":2,"explain":"Deposit Insurance (RBI subsidiary) covers Rs.5 lakh per depositor per bank — raised from Rs.1L in 2020 after the PMC crisis. Covers all commercial + cooperative banks.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"India's sole reinsurer?","options":["LIC","GIC Re","AIC","ECGC"],"answer":1,"explain":"GIC Re (General Insurance Corp, Mumbai) is the designated Indian reinsurer — it insures the insurers. LIC does life; AIC does crop (primary, not reinsurance); ECGC covers export credit.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Third-party motor insurance is?","options":["Optional","Mandatory under MV Act","Only for trucks","Being phased out"],"answer":1,"explain":"Compulsory under the Motor Vehicles Act: every vehicle must cover third-party injury/damage. It never covers your OWN damage — that needs a comprehensive/own-damage add-on.","tag":"motor"},{"q":"Insurance FDI limit (since 2021)?","options":["26%","49%","74%","100%"],"answer":2,"explain":"Raised from 49% to 74% in 2021 to pull foreign capital into insurers. Timeline: 26 (2000) → 49 (2015) → 74 (2021).","tag":"current"},{"q":"Bima Sugam is?","options":["Rural bundled product","Online insurance marketplace","Agent training force","Claim form"],"answer":1,"explain":"IRDAI's trio: Bima SUGAM = online marketplace (compare/buy like Amazon), Bima VISTAR = rural bundled product, Bima VAHAK = women-led distribution force. Part of 'Insurance for all by 2047'.","tag":"current"},{"q":"MUDRA Shishu limit?","options":["Rs.50,000","Rs.5L","Rs.10L","Rs.20L"],"answer":0,"explain":"Shishu ≤50k (infant enterprise), Kishore 50k–5L, Tarun 5L–10L (plus Tarun Plus to 20L now). Shishu=smallest, matching the name (shishu=baby).","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"Census 2011 literacy?","options":["65%","70%","74.04%","80%"],"answer":2,"explain":"74.04% literacy, sex ratio 943, density 382, population 121 crore. Cluster to memorize: 121cr–74%–943–382. (Next census delayed — check current affairs.)","tag":"static"},{"q":"LIC established in?","options":["1938","1956","1972","1991"],"answer":1,"explain":"Life Insurance Corporation of India came up in 1956 (Mumbai HQ) by nationalizing 245 insurers. Contrast GIC (general/reinsurance, 1972) to avoid the swap trap. 1938 is the old Insurance Act year - a deliberate distractor.","tag":"static"},{"q":"PMJJBY premium and cover?","options":["Rs.20, Rs.2L","Rs.436/yr, Rs.2L life cover","Rs.500, Rs.5L","Free, Rs.1L"],"answer":1,"explain":"Jeevan Jyoti = LIFE insurance Rs.2L at ~Rs.436/year, age 18–50, auto-debit. Mirror of PMSBY (accident, Rs.20). Launched together with APY in 2015.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"Atal Pension Yojana gives?","options":["Rs.2L accident cover","Pension Rs.1k–5k after 60","Free health cover","Crop compensation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Contributory pension: pay now, get fixed Rs.1000–5000/month after 60 (amount depends on entry age). Regulated by PFRDA; for unorganized workers. 2015 trio: JJBY+PMSBY+APY.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"PM Jan Dhan Yojana offers?","options":["Zero-balance accounts + RuPay","Free insurance only","Farm loans","Housing subsidy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Jan Dhan = zero-balance bank accounts + RuPay debit + Rs.10k overdraft + Rs.2L accident cover. Launched 2014 — the backbone account for all later DBT/scheme transfers.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"RBI established in?","options":["1935","1947","1956","1969"],"answer":0,"explain":"1935 (RBI Act 1934), Mumbai HQ. Functions: note issue (except Re.1 coin — Government), banker to government/banks, monetary policy, forex. First governor Osborne Smith; first Indian C.D. Deshmukh.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"Repo rate is?","options":["RBI borrows from banks","RBI lends to banks","Banks lend each other","Govt bond yield"],"answer":1,"explain":"REPO = RBI lends to banks against securities (injects liquidity); REVERSE repo = banks park funds with RBI. Raise repo → costlier loans → cools inflation. Current rate changes are Mains favourites.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"CRR is?","options":["Cash banks keep with RBI","Liquid assets banks hold","RBI's gold reserve","Tax on deposits"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cash Reserve Ratio = share of deposits parked WITH RBI (earns no interest). Contrast SLR = liquid assets banks hold THEMSELVES. Both tighten/loosen liquidity — classic inflation-control Q.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"A loan becomes NPA after?","options":["30 days overdue","60 days","90 days","1 year"],"answer":2,"explain":"90 days past due = Non-Performing Asset. Then provisioning norms bite and SARFAESI (2002) lets banks seize secured assets without courts. Substandard→doubtful→loss are NPA stages.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"SEBI headquarters?","options":["Delhi","Mumbai","Hyderabad","Chennai"],"answer":1,"explain":"Securities and Exchange Board of India, Mumbai (statutory powers 1992). Pair: SEBI–Mumbai (markets), IRDAI–Hyderabad (insurance), PFRDA–Delhi (pension), RBI–Mumbai (money).","tag":"regulator"},{"q":"PFRDA regulates?","options":["Insurance","Mutual funds","NPS + APY pensions","Company deposits"],"answer":2,"explain":"Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority runs NPS (market-linked) and APY (guaranteed). NPS has Tier-I (locked, tax benefit) and Tier-II (flexible).","tag":"regulator"},{"q":"UPI was launched by?","options":["RBI directly","NPCI","SEBI","Visa"],"answer":1,"explain":"National Payments Corporation of India (2016) — same body behind RuPay, IMPS, FASTag, AePS. UPI = instant 24×7 mobile transfers via VPA.","tag":"banking-tech"},{"q":"RTGS minimum transfer?","options":["No minimum","Rs.50,000","Rs.2 lakh","Rs.10 lakh"],"answer":2,"explain":"RTGS (realtime settlement) floor is Rs.2L, no ceiling. NEFT has NO minimum and settles in batches; IMPS is 24×7 instant small-value. Match system to use-case.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"Insurance Ombudsman handles?","options":["Agent licensing","Policyholder grievances","Premium pricing","Reinsurance deals"],"answer":1,"explain":"Free, fast grievance redressal for policyholders (claims/rejections/delays) up to Rs.50L awards. Licensing is IRDAI's job; pricing is the insurer's.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Principle of indemnity means?","options":["Profit from claims allowed","Compensate actual loss only","Double insurance pays twice","Nominee decides payout"],"answer":1,"explain":"General insurance restores you to pre-loss position — never profit. That's why two policies on one car still pay only actual loss (contribution clause splits it). Life insurance is NOT indemnity (no measurable 'loss' value).","tag":"principle"},{"q":"Sum assured is?","options":["Premium paid yearly","Maximum payable on claim","Agent commission","Policy tenure"],"answer":1,"explain":"The cover ceiling: the most the insurer pays (e.g. Rs.10L policy → max 10L). Premium is what YOU pay; sum assured is what you GET. Higher sum assured → higher premium.","tag":"vocab"},{"q":"Bima Vistar is aimed at?","options":["Urban HNIs","Rural mass-market bundle","NRIs","Corporates"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vistar = affordable bundled cover (life+health+property) for rural first-time buyers, sold via Bima Vahaks on the Bima Sugam platform. The last-mile piece of Insurance-for-all-2047.","tag":"current"},{"q":"Agriculture Insurance Co (AIC) covers?","options":["Life","Crop/agriculture risks","Vehicles","Marine only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dedicated agri insurer implementing PMFBY alongside private general insurers. Don't confuse with GIC Re (reinsurance) or ECGC (export credit).","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Stand-Up India loans range?","options":["Up to Rs.50k","Rs.10L–1Cr for SC/ST/women","Rs.5–10L anyone","Only agriculture"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bank loans Rs.10 lakh–1 crore to SC/ST and women entrepreneurs for greenfield enterprises (plus RuPay debit). MUDRA serves micro; Stand-Up serves bigger first ventures.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"ECGC covers?","options":["Crop failure","Export credit risks","Vehicle theft","Health costs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Export Credit Guarantee Corp backstops exporters against buyer/country default. Trade-finance safety net, distinct from GIC/AIC.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Deposit insurance covers which banks?","options":["Only SBI","All commercial + cooperative banks","Only private banks","Only PSU banks"],"answer":1,"explain":"DICGC covers commercial, regional rural, LABs and cooperative banks — nearly the whole system. Universality is the point.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"Bancassurance means?","options":["Bank selling insurance","Insurer running a bank","RBI owning LIC","Merging two banks"],"answer":0,"explain":"Banks distribute insurers' products (SBI Life via SBI branches). Distribution channel, not ownership. Corporate agency model.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Microinsurance targets?","options":["HNIs","Low-income households, tiny premiums","Corporates","NRIs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Small-ticket covers for the poor (often via SHGs/MFIs). Financial-inclusion counterpart of PMJJBY/PMSBY.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Weather-index crop insurance pays on?","options":["Farmer's claimed loss","Measured rainfall/temperature triggers","Village vote","Minister approval"],"answer":1,"explain":"Parametric: payouts trigger on INDEX readings (rainfall deficit), no field loss assessment. Fast settlement; basis risk is the drawback.","tag":"crop"},{"q":"Livestock insurance covers?","options":["Only cows","Death of insured animals","Milk price falls","Fodder cost"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pays on death/disablement of tagged animals (subsidized premiums for SC/ST, SF/MF). Asset-mortality cover.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"PMFBY sum insured based on?","options":["Farmer's income","Scale of finance / threshold yield value","Land price","Loan amount only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sum insured = scale of finance (cost of cultivation) per crop; claims from yield shortfall vs threshold. Cost-basis, not income-basis.","tag":"crop"},{"q":"Rabi premium rate PMFBY?","options":["1%","1.5%","2%","5%"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rabi 1.5% (Kharif 2%, commercial 5%). Season-rate ladder: 2–1.5–5.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"Claim settlement ratio measures?","options":["Premium growth","Claims paid vs received","Agent productivity","Branch count"],"answer":1,"explain":"% of claims settled — the trust metric buyers compare (LIC ~98%+). Higher = more reliable payouts.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Solvency ratio (IRDAI minimum)?","options":["100%","120%","150%","200%"],"answer":2,"explain":"150% minimum (available vs required solvency margin). Below → IRDAI action. Insurer-health gauge like CRAR for banks.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Free-look for electronic policies?","options":["7 days","15 days","30 days","90 days"],"answer":2,"explain":"30 days for e-policies/distance marketing vs 15 for paper. Digital buyers get longer review. Two-track memory.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Policy lapse vs surrender?","options":["Same thing","Lapse = unpaid pause; surrender = exit for value","Both need court","Lapse pays bonus"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lapse: premium unpaid, cover pauses (revivable with arrears+interest). Surrender: voluntary exit for surrender value. Pause vs quit.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Paid-up value arises when?","options":["Full term done","Premiums stop after minimum paid, cover continues reduced","Never","Only maturity"],"answer":1,"explain":"Stop paying after 2–3 years → policy converts to reduced paid-up cover (no more premiums). Middle path between lapse and surrender.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Nomination vs assignment?","options":["Same","Nomination names receiver; assignment transfers ownership","Both need IRDAI","Neither is legal"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nominee RECEIVES claim money (custodian for legal heirs); assignee OWNS the policy (e.g. loan collateral). Receiver vs owner.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Top-up health cover works?","options":["Replaces base policy","Pays beyond a deductible threshold","Covers OPD only","No sum assured"],"answer":1,"explain":"Super top-ups pay bills ABOVE a threshold (e.g. beyond Rs.3L), cheaply boosting total cover. Deductible-style design for catastrophic costs.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Restoration benefit?","options":["Premium refund","Sum assured refills after exhaustion","Free checkup","Double claim"],"answer":1,"explain":"If full sum is consumed, it restores (usually once) for unrelated illness same year. Second-chance cover within one policy year.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Day-care procedures need?","options":["7-day stay","No 24-hr hospitalization (cataract, dialysis...)","Only surgery","ICU mandatory"],"answer":1,"explain":"Listed day-care treatments (cataract, chemo sessions, dialysis) are covered WITHOUT 24-hr admission. Hospitalization rule has this carved exception.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Pre/post hospitalization cover windows?","options":["15/30 days","30/60 days typical","1 year each","Not covered"],"answer":1,"explain":"Usually 30 days pre + 60–90 days post expenses (tests, follow-ups) linked to the claim. Window lengths vary by product — know the standard pair.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"AYUSH cover in health plans?","options":["Never","Many plans cover Ayurveda/Yoga/Unani/Siddha/Homeopathy","Only allopathy","Only abroad"],"answer":1,"explain":"IRDAI mandates AYUSH coverage options in health insurance. System-of-medicine inclusion is the tested fact.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Domiciliary (home) treatment covered when?","options":["Always","If hospitalization needed but unavailable/refused by patient","Never","Only for rich"],"answer":1,"explain":"Home treatment counts when admission was medically necessary but impossible (no beds) or the patient couldn't be moved. Conditional equivalence.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Maternity waiting period?","options":["None","9 months–4 years by product","10 years","Lifetime exclusion"],"answer":1,"explain":"Maternity/newborn riders carry 9-month to multi-year waits. Separate from PED waits — product-specific windows.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Personal accident cover pays for?","options":["Illness","Accidental death/disablement (table-based)","Old age","Unemployment"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fixed benefits per disability table (e.g. 100% death, 50% single limb). Accident-only, illness excluded — the PA vs health split.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Burglary policy covers?","options":["Fire","Break-in theft with forcible entry","Cyber fraud","Flood"],"answer":1,"explain":"Forcible/violent entry is the trigger condition. Mysterious disappearance isn't covered — entry evidence required.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Shopkeeper package policies bundle?","options":["Only fire","Fire+burglary+money+liability combos","Only life","Only vehicles"],"answer":1,"explain":"Package/combo products bundle shop risks (building, stock, cash, liability) in one policy. SME convenience design.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Workmen compensation (EC Act) covers?","options":["Office gossip","Workplace injury/death liability of employer","Retirement","Bonuses"],"answer":1,"explain":"Employer's statutory liability for on-duty injury/death. Now Employees' Compensation Act. Employer-liability cover.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Professional indemnity for?","options":["Doctors/lawyers' negligence claims","Car damage","Crop loss","Travel delay"],"answer":0,"explain":"Covers professionals against client claims of error/negligence (doctors, CAs, architects). Service-liability, not property.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Directors & Officers (D&O) covers?","options":["Office rent","Management decisions liability","Staff salaries","IT hardware"],"answer":1,"explain":"Protects company directors against claims from decisions (shareholders, regulators). Governance-risk product.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Cyber insurance covers?","options":["Slow internet","Data breach costs, liability, ransomware","New laptops","Software licences"],"answer":1,"explain":"Breach response, legal liability, extortion, business interruption from cyber events. First-party + third-party limbs.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Title insurance (property)?","options":["Construction cost","Ownership defects in title","Rent defaults","Home loans"],"answer":1,"explain":"Covers past title defects (forgery, liens, heir claims) discovered after purchase. Backward-looking ownership cover.","tag":"terms"},{"q":"Pet insurance is which branch?","options":["Life","Miscellaneous/general","Health-only human","Reinsurance"],"answer":1,"explain":"Miscellaneous (general) branch — vet costs/mortality. Branch-classification Qs.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Satellite launch insurance sits in?","options":["Fire","Marine/aviation specialty","Motor","Health"],"answer":1,"explain":"Aviation/space specialty (pre-launch + launch + in-orbit phases). Specialty-lines awareness.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Trade credit insurance protects?","options":["Banks only","Sellers against buyer non-payment","Buyers against sellers","Transporters"],"answer":1,"explain":"Domestic/export receivables cover (ECGC for cross-border). Seller-side protection.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Surety bonds guarantee?","options":["Loan repayment only","Contract/project obligations","Marriage","Election deposits"],"answer":1,"explain":"Surety assures the obligee that the principal performs (construction, government contracts). Growing infra product.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Parametric insurance differs by?","options":["Cheaper agents","Trigger-based payout, no loss survey","Higher premiums","Longer terms"],"answer":1,"explain":"Payout on an INDEX crossing a threshold (rainfall, quake magnitude) — no adjuster visit. Speed vs basis-risk trade.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Floaters in health cover?","options":["One person","Whole family under one sum","Only seniors","Only kids"],"answer":1,"explain":"Family floater: shared sum for all members (anyone can consume). Vs individual policies per member. Shared-pool design.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Critical illness lump sum triggers on?","options":["Any fever","Listed diagnosis (cancer, MI...)","Hospital bill size","Doctor's mood"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fixed payout on FIRST diagnosis of listed illnesses (with survival-period clause ~30 days). Diagnosis-based, not expense-based.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Superannuation plans are?","options":["Health riders","Employer retirement (pension/gratuity) schemes","Crop covers","Travel"],"answer":1,"explain":"Group retirement benefits employers fund (LIC manages many). B2B retirement, not retail health.","tag":"insurance"},{"q":"Gratuity payable after?","options":["1 year","5 years continuous service","10 years","Retirement only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Payment of Gratuity Act: 5 years continuous service (death/disablement exempt). Statutory-employer-benefit cluster with EPF/ESIC.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"Maternity Benefit Act leave?","options":["12 weeks","26 weeks","52 weeks","6 weeks"],"answer":1,"explain":"26 weeks paid maternity leave (2017 amendment), plus work-from-home option. Labour-welfare numbers.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"Minimum wages set by?","options":["RBI","Appropriate government (Centre/state)","IRDAI","Courts"],"answer":1,"explain":"Labour statutes: Centre/state governments fix floors by skill/zone. Code on Wages consolidates. Authority-mapping.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"PMEGP gives?","options":["Crop loans","Margin-money subsidy for micro-enterprises","Free houses","Pensions"],"answer":1,"explain":"Prime Minister's Employment Generation Programme: subsidy (15–35%) for new micro units via KVIC. Entrepreneurship cluster with MUDRA/Stand-Up.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"Sukanya Samriddhi is for?","options":["Boys' education","Girl child savings, tax-free","Senior citizens","Farmers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Small-savings for girl child (<10y), EEE tax status, high rates. Beti Bachao-linked finance product.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"PPF rate/tax status?","options":["Taxable, 5y","EEE, 15y","Taxable, 10y","EEE, 5y"],"answer":1,"explain":"Public Provident Fund: Exempt-Exempt-Exempt, 15-year tenure, government-set quarterly rates. Small-savings flagship.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"NPS exit at 60: lump sum vs annuity?","options":["100% lump sum","Up to 60% lump sum + 40% annuity","Only annuity","Only pension to nominee"],"answer":1,"explain":"At 60: max 60% commutable lump sum, min 40% annuitized for pension. Corpus-split rule.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"KVP doubles in?","options":["Fixed 10y","~115 months at current rates","5 years","Never"],"answer":1,"explain":"Kisan Vikas Patra: doubles over ~115 months (rate-linked tenure). Doubling-instrument vs fixed-tenure (NSC 5y).","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"SCSS is for?","options":["Students","Senior citizens savings","Children","Women only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Senior Citizens Savings Scheme: 60+ (55+ retirees), quarterly payouts, tax benefit 80C. Age-gated small savings.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":" Sovereign Gold Bond issued by?","options":["SEBI","RBI for Government","Private jewellers","NSE"],"answer":1,"explain":"RBI issues SGBs for GoI (gold-denominated, interest + redemption linked). Demat gold alternative.","tag":"scheme"},{"q":"RBI's forex role?","options":["Fixes Re/$ daily","Manages reserves, orderly market (no fixed target)","Bans forex","Sets export prices"],"answer":1,"explain":"Manages volatility, not a level: reserves + intervention to curb disorder. 'No target' doctrine.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"NOSTRO/VOSTRO?","options":["Same account","Ours-with-them / theirs-with-us (forex)","Loan types","Card variants"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nostro = our account abroad (in foreign currency); Vostro = their account here. Mirror pair for correspondent banking.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"LIBOR successor?","options":["MIBOR","SOFR (USD)","EURIBOR stays","No successor"],"answer":1,"explain":"USD LIBOR → SOFR (secured overnight). Global benchmark transition (2021–23). MIBOR is India's own.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"PCA framework (RBI) does?","options":["Closes banks","Restricts weak banks' lending/expansion","Merges banks","Prints notes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Prompt Corrective Action: thresholds on CRAR/NPA/ROA trigger lending curbs. Rehabilitation, not closure.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"Small Finance Banks need?","options":["No capital","Min capital + PSL focus, small accounts","Only urban","No branches"],"answer":1,"explain":"SFBs: financial inclusion mandate (small loans, PSL-heavy), differentiated licence. Inclusion-architecture piece.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"Payments Banks CANNOT?","options":["Accept deposits","Lend","Issue debit cards","Use UPI"],"answer":1,"explain":"No lending, no credit cards; deposits capped (Rs.2L). Narrow-bank model (Airtel/Jio). Lending ban is the tested line.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"NBFC vs bank difference?","options":["None","NBFCs can't accept demand deposits / no DICGC","NBFCs print notes","Banks can't lend"],"answer":1,"explain":"NBFCs: no demand deposits, no DICGC cover, no payment system membership (traditionally). Shadow-banking basics.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"Priority Sector Lending target (banks)?","options":["10%","18% agri, 40% total PSL","50%","75%"],"answer":1,"explain":"40% of ANBC to priority sectors, incl. 18% agriculture. PSLC certificates trade shortfalls. Number-heavy favourite.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"MCLR replaced?","options":["Nothing","Base rate","EBLR (external benchmark) for new floating loans","Fixed rates only"],"answer":2,"explain":"New floating retail/MSME loans link to EXTERNAL benchmarks (repo/T-bill). Transmission reform. Base→MCLR→EBLR ladder.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"SARFAESI applies to?","options":["All loans","Secured loans (no agri land, mostly)","Unsecured only","Govt dues"],"answer":1,"explain":"Secured creditors, with carve-outs (agricultural land excluded). Scope limits are tested.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"DRT threshold?","options":["Rs.1L","Rs.20L+","Rs.1Cr","No threshold"],"answer":1,"explain":"Debts Recovery Tribunals: Rs.20 lakh and above. Forum-jurisdiction numbers.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"CIC (credit bureaus) regulated by?","options":["SEBI","RBI","IRDAI","NITI"],"answer":1,"explain":"CIBIL/Experian/Equifax/Crif licensed under CIC Act by RBI. CIBIL score 300–900; 750+ preferred. Score-band awareness.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"CIBIL good score?","options":["300","550","750+","999"],"answer":2,"explain":"CIBIL scale is 300–900; lenders prefer 750+. Below 650 invites rejections or steep rates. One-line numeracy with real consequences.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"KYC documents (OVD)?","options":["Any ID","Passport, DL, Voter ID, NREGA card, NPR letter","Only PAN","Only Aadhaar"],"answer":1,"explain":"Officially Valid Documents list for CKYC. PAN is separate (tax). Document-list memory.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"PMLA deals with?","options":["Tax evasion only","Money laundering","Insurance fraud only","Cybercrime only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Prevention of Money Laundering Act: laundering + attachment powers (ED enforces). FIU-IND gets reports. AML pillar.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"FIU-IND collects?","options":["Tax returns","Suspicious/cash transaction reports","Exam papers","Census"],"answer":1,"explain":"Financial Intelligence Unit gets CTR/STR/CCR from banks. Reporting-regime piece.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"Basel III CRAR (India)?","options":["9% flat","11.5% incl. CCB","15%","No norm"],"answer":1,"explain":"~9% + 2.5% capital conservation buffer = 11.5%. Buffer arithmetic is the examined bit.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"BG vs LC?","options":["Same","Bank Guarantee (performance) vs Letter of Credit (payment)","Both loans","Both deposits"],"answer":1,"explain":"BG backs OBLIGATIONS; LC assures PAYMENT in trade. Guarantee-vs-payment split.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"NEFT timing?","options":["Realtime","Half-hourly batches, 24×7","Weekly","Monthly"],"answer":1,"explain":"NEFT: 48 half-hourly batches daily, 24×7×365 (since Dec 2019). Batch rhythm vs RTGS realtime vs IMPS instant.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"AePS uses?","options":["Cards","Aadhaar + biometrics at micro-ATMs","Cheques","DDs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Aadhaar-enabled Payments: fingerprint + Aadhaar for balance/aadhaar-pay. Inclusion-tech stack with UPI/USSD.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"FASTag uses?","options":["GPS","RFID","Bluetooth","QR only"],"answer":1,"explain":"FASTag = RFID tags read at toll plazas, linked to NETC wallets. Technology-match: RFID for tolling, GPS for tracking, QR for static pay.","tag":"banking"},{"q":"GDP base year (current series)?","options":["2004-05","2011-12","2015-16","2020-21"],"answer":1,"explain":"2011-12 base (CSO). Base-year updates rebase growth math — cited in every Survey.","tag":"economy"},{"q":"NITI Aayog replaced?","options":["Finance Commission","Planning Commission","EC","CAG"],"answer":1,"explain":"NITI Aayog (2015) replaced the Planning Commission: cooperative federalism + think-tank role vs top-down five-year plans. Chairperson is the PM; Governing Council holds CMs.","tag":"polity"}]},{"id":"ck","title":"Computer Knowledge","desc":"NICL-only Mains 50/40Q • Office • shortcuts • hardware","track":"mains","cards":[{"q":"First-generation computers used?","options":["Transistors","Vacuum tubes","ICs","VLSI chips"],"answer":1,"explain":"Gen 1 (1940s-50s, e.g. ENIAC/UNIVAC) ran on bulky, hot vacuum tubes. Then came transistors (G2), integrated circuits (G3), VLSI microprocessors (G4), and AI/parallel computing (G5). Order by shrinking size: tube → transistor → IC → chip.","tag":"history"},{"q":"(1011)₂ in decimal?","options":["9","11","13","15"],"answer":1,"explain":"Weight each bit right-to-left as 1,2,4,8: 1×8 + 0×4 + 1×2 + 1×1 = 8+0+2+1 = 11. Always expand positional values instead of guessing.","tag":"binary"},{"q":"Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y do?","options":["Cut/Paste","Undo/Redo","Find/Replace","Save/Print"],"answer":1,"explain":"Z = undo last action, Y (or Ctrl+Shift+Z) = redo it. Neighbourhood: X/C/V cut-copy-paste, S save, P print, F find, H replace, F7 spelling. These six shortcuts cover most CK shortcut Qs.","tag":"shortcuts"},{"q":"$A$1 in Excel is?","options":["Fully relative","Absolute reference","Row-fixed only","Invalid"],"answer":1,"explain":"$ locks: $A$1 locks both (absolute — never shifts when copied), A$1 locks only the row, $A1 only the column, A1 fully relative. Test: dragging $A$1 down keeps pointing at A1.","tag":"excel"},{"q":"F5 in PowerPoint?","options":["Save","Slideshow from start","Spellcheck","New slide"],"answer":1,"explain":"F5 starts the slideshow from slide 1; Shift+F5 starts from the CURRENT slide (handy while editing). F7 is spelling, F12 is Save As. Don't confuse with browser F5 (refresh).","tag":"ppt"},{"q":"SMTP vs POP3?","options":["Both receive mail","Send vs receive","Both send mail","Chat protocols"],"answer":1,"explain":"SMTP (port 587/25) SENDS mail out; POP3 (110) or IMAP (143) RECEIVE it. Memory hook: S=Send. IMAP syncs across devices; POP3 downloads then usually deletes from server.","tag":"email"},{"q":"HTTPS default port?","options":["80","443","53","25"],"answer":1,"explain":"HTTP=80, HTTPS=443, DNS=53, SMTP=25, FTP=21. Secure variants just add a port: the S in HTTPS buys TLS encryption and the padlock. Memorize this five-row table cold.","tag":"ports"},{"q":"Virus vs worm?","options":["Same thing","Virus needs host+action; worm self-spreads","Worm needs a host","Neither spreads"],"answer":1,"explain":"A virus attaches to a host file and needs you to run it; a worm is standalone and crawls networks by itself (e.g. WannaCry). Trojan = disguised as legit software; ransomware encrypts for money; spyware steals silently.","tag":"security"},{"q":"ALU + CU = ?","options":["RAM","CPU","ROM","Cache"],"answer":1,"explain":"The CPU is exactly Arithmetic-Logic Unit (does the math) + Control Unit (directs traffic) plus registers. RAM/ROM/cache are MEMORY — a different subsystem. ALU+CU=CPU is the single most-asked architecture one-liner.","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"1 GB equals?","options":["1000 MB","1024 MB","1024 KB","100 MB"],"answer":1,"explain":"Memory uses binary: 1 KB=1024 B, 1 MB=1024 KB, 1 GB=1024 MB (2^10 each step). Telecom/storage marketing sometimes uses 1000, but exams expect 1024. 1024 KB would be just 1 MB.","tag":"memory"},{"q":"MICR is used on?","options":["Wi-Fi routers","Bank cheques","Fingerprints","Barcodes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Magnetic Ink Character Recognition reads the special magnetic numbers at a cheque's bottom for fast clearing (the 9-digit MICR code). Barcodes are optical (supermarkets); fingerprints are biometric.","tag":"io"},{"q":"UPI vs IFSC?","options":["Both branch codes","Instant payments vs branch ID","Card types","Loan schemes"],"answer":1,"explain":"UPI (by NPCI) = instant mobile payments via VPA. IFSC = 11-character branch identifier (4 bank code + 0 + 6 branch) used in NEFT/RTGS/IMPS transfers. One moves money, one addresses the branch.","tag":"banking-tech"},{"q":"RAM vs ROM?","options":["Both volatile","Volatile vs non-volatile","Both permanent","Same speed"],"answer":1,"explain":"RAM is working memory — fast but VOLATILE (wiped on power-off). ROM is NON-volatile firmware (holds BIOS/boot code). That's why unsaved work dies in a power cut but the PC still boots.","tag":"memory"},{"q":"Cache memory is?","options":["Slowest, largest","Fastest small memory near CPU","Only in HDD","Same as RAM"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cache (L1/L2/L3, KBs–MBs) sits beside the CPU holding hot data, exploiting locality: recently/repeatedly used values. Hierarchy by speed: registers > L1 > L2 > L3 > RAM > SSD > HDD. Small+fast always pairs together.","tag":"memory"},{"q":"Which is input-only?","options":["Touchscreen","Keyboard","Modem","NIC"],"answer":1,"explain":"Keyboard only sends data in. Touchscreen (display+touch), modem (send+receive) and NIC (network both ways) are bidirectional. Exam trick: anything with a display or network role is usually both-way.","tag":"io"},{"q":"Impact printer example?","options":["Laser","Inkjet","Dot-matrix","Thermal"],"answer":2,"explain":"Impact = physically strikes an ink ribbon: dot-matrix and daisy-wheel (noisy, multipart forms). Laser/inkjet/thermal are NON-impact (no contact). Bank passbook printers are dot-matrix — classic example.","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"(2A)₁₆ in decimal?","options":["26","32","42","52"],"answer":2,"explain":"Hex digits weight 16s and 1s: 2×16 + A(=10)×1 = 32+10 = 42. A–F = 10–15. One byte = 2 hex digits (FF = 255).","tag":"hex"},{"q":"XOR of 1 and 1?","options":["0","1","2","Undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"XOR outputs 1 only when inputs DIFFER (0⊕1=1, 1⊕1=0). Contrast: AND needs both 1; OR needs any 1; NAND/NOR invert those. Truth-table Qs are free marks if you memorize the four gates.","tag":"gates"},{"q":"Ctrl+P does?","options":["Paste","Print","Play","Pin"],"answer":1,"explain":"P=Print (V=Paste, which is why P couldn't be paste). Core set: S save, C copy, X cut, V paste, Z undo, P print, F find, B/I/U formatting.","tag":"shortcuts"},{"q":"F2 key does?","options":["Help","Rename","Refresh","Save As"],"answer":1,"explain":"F2 renames the selected file/icon. Neighbours: F1 help, F5 refresh/slideshow, F7 spelling, F12 Save As (Shift+F12 saves). F2-rename works in Explorer AND Excel (edit cell).","tag":"shortcuts"},{"q":".xlsx is a file of?","options":["Word","Excel","PowerPoint","Access"],"answer":1,"explain":"Office extensions since 2007 end in x (XML): .docx Word, .xlsx Excel, .pptx PowerPoint, .accdb Access. Old 97–2003 formats lack the x (.doc/.xls/.ppt).","tag":"files"},{"q":"COUNTA vs COUNT?","options":["Same","COUNTA counts non-empty cells","COUNT is faster","COUNTA sums values"],"answer":1,"explain":"COUNT counts only NUMERIC cells; COUNTA counts all NON-EMPTY cells (text included). Neither sums — that's SUM. Trap: a column of 5 names gives COUNT=0 but COUNTA=5.","tag":"excel"},{"q":"Slide Master is used for?","options":["Animations only","Uniform design across slides","Spellcheck","Printing notes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Slide Master (View tab) sets theme, fonts, logos and layouts ONCE for the whole deck — change it and every slide follows. Without it you'd restyle 50 slides by hand. Animations live on individual objects, not the master.","tag":"ppt"},{"q":"HTTPS differs from HTTP by?","options":["Speed only","TLS encryption on 443","No DNS needed","Works offline"],"answer":1,"explain":"The S = TLS/SSL encryption (port 443 vs 80), giving the padlock: logins/cards can't be sniffed. HTTP sends everything in readable plain text. DNS is still needed for both.","tag":"web"},{"q":"CC vs BCC?","options":["Same","BCC hides recipients","CC is encrypted","BCC is faster"],"answer":1,"explain":"CC (carbon copy) recipients are VISIBLE to all; BCC (blind CC) stays hidden — use BCC for bulk mail to protect addresses. Neither encrypts anything; that's PGP/S-MIME's job.","tag":"email"},{"q":"MAN covers?","options":["A room","A city","A country","One PC"],"answer":1,"explain":"PAN (personal/Bluetooth) < LAN (building) < MAN (city/metro) < WAN (country/internet). Example MAN: a city's cable broadband or campus-spanning university net. Size order is the whole Q.","tag":"network"},{"q":"Modem does?","options":["Routes by IP","Converts analog↔digital","Assigns IP addresses","Blocks viruses"],"answer":1,"explain":"MOdulator-DEModulator bridges analog phone lines and digital devices. Router picks paths by IP; DHCP assigns IPs; firewall blocks threats. One device, one job — match function to name.","tag":"devices"},{"q":"Trojan is?","options":["Self-spreading worm","Malware disguised as legit software","Hardware fault","Firewall rule"],"answer":1,"explain":"Named for the wooden horse: looks like a useful program, hides malware inside, and needs YOU to run it (unlike self-spreading worms). Free 'cracked' software is the classic carrier.","tag":"security"},{"q":"2FA means?","options":["Two passwords","Two independent factors","Two browsers","Double encryption"],"answer":1,"explain":"Two INDEPENDENT factors: something you know (password) + have (OTP/phone) or are (fingerprint). Two passwords are still one factor — that's why password+OTP beats password+PIN.","tag":"security"},{"q":"Which is a DBMS?","options":["Windows","MySQL","Chrome","Tally OS"],"answer":1,"explain":"MySQL/Oracle/PostgreSQL MANAGE databases (store, query, secure). Windows is an OS, Chrome a browser. If it runs SQL, it's a DBMS — the name test never fails.","tag":"dbms"},{"q":"Kernel vs shell?","options":["Same thing","Kernel core manages hardware; shell interprets commands","Shell is hardware","Kernel is an app"],"answer":1,"explain":"Kernel = core supervisor (memory/process/device). Shell = command interpreter (bash/PowerShell) talking to it. Layer order: hardware→kernel→shell→apps.","tag":"os"},{"q":"FAT32 vs NTFS?","options":["Same","FAT32 older, 4GB file limit; NTFS journaling, permissions","NTFS older","Both Linux-only"],"answer":1,"explain":"FAT32: universal but 4GB max file, no journaling. NTFS: Windows modern, journaling + permissions + large files. ext4 is Linux's counterpart.","tag":"os"},{"q":"C: drive vs D: drive concept?","options":["Different disks always","Partitions/volumes of storage","CPU cores","RAM sticks"],"answer":1,"explain":"Letters map partitions/volumes, not physical disks. Partitioning splits one disk logically.","tag":"os"},{"q":"PDF stands for?","options":["Printed Document File","Portable Document Format","Public Data Form","Personal Doc Folder"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adobe's Portable Document Format — layout-fixed sharing. Creator/viewer split (Acrobat/Reader).","tag":"files"},{"q":"ZIP helps by?","options":["Encrypting only","Compressing + bundling files","Deleting data","Speeding CPU"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lossless compression + single archive (zip/rar/7z). Smaller transfers, one download.","tag":"files"},{"q":"MP3 vs WAV?","options":["Same","MP3 lossy-compressed; WAV raw large","WAV smaller","MP3 video"],"answer":1,"explain":"MP3 discards inaudible data (~10× smaller); WAV is raw PCM. Lossy vs lossless audio.","tag":"files"},{"q":"JPG vs PNG?","options":["Same","JPG lossy photos; PNG lossless+transparency","PNG smaller always","JPG for text"],"answer":1,"explain":"JPG: lossy, photos. PNG: lossless + alpha transparency, bigger. Screenshots/logos → PNG; photos → JPG.","tag":"files"},{"q":"USB vs HDMI?","options":["Same port","USB data/peripherals; HDMI audio-video display","Both power only","Both network"],"answer":1,"explain":"USB: universal data/peripheral bus. HDMI: digital A/V to displays. VGA is analog video predecessor.","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"SSD vs HDD?","options":["Same speed","SSD flash, fast/silent; HDD spinning, slower","HDD has no moving parts","SSD uses tapes"],"answer":1,"explain":"SSD = NAND flash, no moving parts, ~10× faster boots. HDD = platters+heads, cheap bulk. NVMe vs SATA are SSD interfaces.","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"DDR in RAM means?","options":["Double Data Rate (2 transfers/clock)","Direct Disk Read","Dual Display Ready","Data Dump Register"],"answer":0,"explain":"DDR3→DDR4→DDR5 generations: double-pumped transfers per clock. Higher number = newer/faster, not interchangeable (slots differ).","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"Laser vs inkjet?","options":["Same tech","Laser toner+fast bulk; inkjet liquid ink, photo quality","Inkjet faster","Laser uses ribbons"],"answer":1,"explain":"Laser: toner powder, fast, cheap per page (offices). Inkjet: droplets, better photos, costlier per page. Impact=dot-matrix third family.","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"Plotter is for?","options":["Text docs","Large-format drawings (CAD/maps)","Photos","Spreadsheets"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vector large-format output for engineers/architects. Niche-device awareness.","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"Scanner vs photocopier?","options":["Same","Scanner digitizes to file; copier prints copies","Both print","Both store cloud"],"answer":1,"explain":"Scanner input→digital file; copier input→paper (scan+print combined). MFPs do both.","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"Webcam + mic are?","options":["Output only","Input devices","Storage","Network gear"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both feed data IN (video/audio). Speakers/monitors are output. Direction decides.","tag":"io"},{"q":"Projector is?","options":["Input","Output","Storage","Both"],"answer":1,"explain":"Display output device (takes signal, throws image). Input would be document camera feeding it.","tag":"io"},{"q":"OCR vs OMR?","options":["Same","OCR reads text shapes; OMR reads marks/bubbles","Both audio","Both video"],"answer":1,"explain":"OCR: printed text → editable (scanned docs). OMR: shaded bubbles (OMR sheets, lotteries). Shape-vs-mark split.","tag":"io"},{"q":"Joystick mainly for?","options":["Typing","Gaming/simulation control","Printing","Coding"],"answer":1,"explain":"Analog directional input for games/simulators. Device-purpose mapping.","tag":"io"},{"q":"Biometric devices?","options":["Keyboard","Fingerprint/iris scanners","Monitor","Printer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Measure body traits for identity (fingerprint, face, iris). Authentication hardware family.","tag":"io"},{"q":"Compiler vs interpreter?","options":["Same","Compiler whole program→machine code; interpreter line-by-line","Interpreter faster","Compiler needs internet"],"answer":1,"explain":"C/C++ compiled (exe first, runs fast); Python/JS interpreted (runs as read, easier debug). Error timing differs: compile-time vs runtime.","tag":"programming"},{"q":"HTML is?","options":["Programming language","Markup for web structure","Database","OS"],"answer":1,"explain":"HyperText MARKUP Language: tags structure pages (browser renders). CSS styles, JS programs. Markup≠programming.","tag":"programming"},{"q":"Algorithm vs flowchart?","options":["Same","Steps in words vs diagram","Both hardware","Both languages"],"answer":1,"explain":"Algorithm = ordered steps (text/pseudocode); flowchart = same logic as shapes (oval=start, diamond=decision, rectangle=process).","tag":"programming"},{"q":"Bug vs debug?","options":["Same","Bug=error; debug=removing it","Both hardware faults","Both features"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bug: defect (term from moth in Harvard Mark II, 1947 lore). Debug: find+fix process. Vocabulary pair.","tag":"programming"},{"q":"WWW invented by?","options":["Bill Gates","Tim Berners-Lee","Steve Jobs","Vint Cerf"],"answer":1,"explain":"Berners-Lee at CERN (~1990): URL+HTTP+HTML. Cerf co-invented TCP/IP (internet plumbing) — different layer, classic confusion pair.","tag":"history"},{"q":"First programmer?","options":["Ada Lovelace","Grace Hopper","Alan Turing","Charles Babbage"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ada Lovelace wrote the Bernoulli algorithm for the Analytical Engine (1840s). Hopper built first compiler; Turing theory; Babbage designed engines.","tag":"history"},{"q":"ENIAC was?","options":["First PC","First general electronic computer (1945)","First laptop","First mouse"],"answer":1,"explain":"ENIAC (1945, vacuum tubes) = first general-purpose electronic computer. UNIVAC I = first commercial. Chronology matters.","tag":"history"},{"q":"Mouse invented by?","options":["Jobs","Douglas Engelbart","Gates","Musk"],"answer":1,"explain":"Engelbart (1960s, SRI; wooden prototype) + hypertext demo ('mother of all demos'). Attribution Qs.","tag":"history"},{"q":"Linux creator + nature?","options":["Microsoft, paid","Linus Torvalds, open-source kernel","Apple, closed","IBM, hardware"],"answer":1,"explain":"Torvalds (1991); kernel + GNU tools = distributions (Ubuntu...). Open-source model vs Windows/macOS proprietary.","tag":"history"},{"q":"macOS maker?","options":["Microsoft","Apple","Linux Foundation","Google"],"answer":1,"explain":"Apple's desktop OS (Unix-based). Maker mapping: Windows-Microsoft, macOS-Apple, Android-Google, iOS-Apple.","tag":"history"},{"q":"Android is based on?","options":["Windows","Linux kernel","iOS","DOS"],"answer":1,"explain":"Google's Android builds on a modified Linux kernel. Explains Android's openness vs iOS.","tag":"history"},{"q":"ChromeOS runs mainly?","options":["Desktop apps","Web apps (browser-centric)","Games","Servers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Google's lightweight OS: Chrome browser + web apps + Android/Linux containers. Cloud-first design.","tag":"history"},{"q":"Phishing vs vishing vs smishing?","options":["Same attack","Email vs voice-call vs SMS lures","All hardware","All antiviruses"],"answer":1,"explain":"Channel prefixes: ph=mail, v=voice, sm=SMS. Same social-engineering core, different medium. Don't be fooled by 'same attack'.","tag":"security"},{"q":"Ransomware demands?","options":["Passwords","Payment for decryption key","New hardware","Friend requests"],"answer":1,"explain":"Encrypts victim files, sells the key (often crypto). Defense: offline backups + patching. Backup rule 3-2-1.","tag":"security"},{"q":"Strong password needs?","options":["Only lowercase","Length + mixed classes + uniqueness","Birthdate","Single word"],"answer":1,"explain":"12+ chars, upper/lower/digit/symbol, unique per site (manager). Length beats complexity; reuse kills.","tag":"security"},{"q":"Firewall blocks?","options":["All internet","Unauthorized traffic per rules","Viruses already inside","Hardware faults"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rule-based traffic filter at boundary (allow web, block telnet...). Not an antivirus (won't clean infected files) nor backup.","tag":"security"},{"q":"Backup 3-2-1 means?","options":["3 passwords","3 copies, 2 media, 1 offsite","3 users","2-hour backup"],"answer":1,"explain":"3 copies, 2 different media, 1 offsite/offline. Survives disk death + ransomware + fire. Industry-standard mantra.","tag":"security"},{"q":"HTTPS padlock assures?","options":["Site is honest","Encrypted connection to that domain","Free shopping","No ads"],"answer":1,"explain":"Encryption + domain identity — NOT trustworthiness (phishing sites use HTTPS too). Check sender/domain, not just lock.","tag":"security"},{"q":"Spam vs phishing?","options":["Same","Spam=bulk junk; phishing=fraudulent credential theft","Both wanted","Both encrypted"],"answer":1,"explain":"Spam annoys (bulk ads); phishing STEALS (fake login pages). Report phishing; unsubscribe/block spam.","tag":"security"},{"q":"Pixel resolution 1920×1080 = ?","options":["720p","1080p (Full HD)","4K","8K"],"answer":1,"explain":"1080 rows = Full HD. 720p=HD, 3840×2160=4K, 7680×4320=8K. Rows define the label.","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"Refresh rate 60Hz means?","options":["60 pixels","60 frames/second","60 colors","60 watts"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hertz = redraws per second. 120/144Hz = smoother motion (gaming). Not resolution.","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"Ink tank vs cartridge?","options":["Same","Tank refillable bulk; cartridge replaceable","Tank is laser","Cartridge is toner"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ink-tank (EcoTank): refill bottles, cheap running. Cartridge: swap pods, costlier per page. Total-cost-of-ownership Qs.","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"Which cable carries network?","options":["HDMI","Ethernet (RJ45)","VGA","AUX"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ethernet/RJ45 = wired LAN. HDMI/VGA = display; AUX = audio. Connector-purpose mapping.","tag":"hardware"},{"q":"Bluetooth range class?","options":["Kilometres","~10m personal area","Across cities","Global"],"answer":1,"explain":"PAN tech ~10m (headsets, wearables). Wi-Fi covers rooms (LAN); cellular covers cities (WAN). Range ladder.","tag":"network"},{"q":"Hotspot shares?","options":["Printer","Mobile data as Wi-Fi","Files only","Calls only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Phone's cellular data broadcast as Wi-Fi. Tethering family (USB/Bluetooth/Wi-Fi).","tag":"network"},{"q":"SSID is?","options":["Password","Wi-Fi network name","Router brand","IP address"],"answer":1,"explain":"Service Set Identifier = the name you pick from the list. Password (WPA2 key) is separate. Hidden SSID ≠ security.","tag":"network"},{"q":"MAC address is?","options":["Apple computer","Hardware NIC identifier","Email address","Website"],"answer":1,"explain":"Burned-in NIC address (vs changeable IP). MAC filtering = allow-list by hardware. Layer-2 identity.","tag":"network"},{"q":"IPv6 length?","options":["32-bit","64-bit","128-bit","256-bit"],"answer":2,"explain":"128-bit hex (8 groups) vs IPv4 32-bit dotted. Vast space ends NAT scarcity. Length numbers: 32 vs 128.","tag":"network"},{"q":"Subnet mask 255.255.255.0 = ?","options":["/8","/16","/24","/32"],"answer":2,"explain":"Three 255 octets = 24 network bits → /24 (254 hosts). Count the 255s: 1→/8, 2→/16, 3→/24.","tag":"network"},{"q":"Default gateway is?","options":["DNS server","Router exit to other networks","Printer","Firewall only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The router interface your LAN uses to reach outside (internet). Misconfigured gateway = local works, internet dead — classic diagnosis Q.","tag":"network"},{"q":"DHCP does?","options":["Resolves names","Auto-assigns IP/mask/gateway","Blocks sites","Speeds CPU"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dynamic Host Configuration: IP + mask + gateway + DNS automatically (DORA process). Static IPs are the manual alternative. APIPA 169.254.x.x = DHCP failed.","tag":"network"},{"q":"FTP vs HTTP?","options":["Same","FTP moves files; HTTP serves web pages","Both email","Both encrypted"],"answer":1,"explain":"FTP (21) bulk file transfer; HTTP (80) web content. SFTP/FTPS add encryption. Protocol-purpose split.","tag":"network"},{"q":"Search engine vs browser?","options":["Same","Engine finds (Google); browser displays (Chrome)","Both hardware","Both email"],"answer":1,"explain":"Chrome/Firefox/Edge = browsers (apps). Google/Bing = engines (sites). Category error is the trap.","tag":"web"},{"q":"Cookies do?","options":["Viruses","Remember state/preferences","Speed CPU","Store passwords safely"],"answer":1,"explain":"Small site-stored tokens: logins, carts, prefs. Clearing logs you out everywhere. Privacy angle: tracking cookies.","tag":"web"},{"q":"Cache helps by?","options":["More RAM","Reusing stored copies faster","Bigger disk","New CPU"],"answer":1,"explain":"Browser/CDN/CPU caches serve repeats without refetch/recompute. Stale cache → hard-refresh (Ctrl+F5) fix.","tag":"web"},{"q":"URL parts order?","options":["domain://protocol/path","protocol://domain:port/path","path://domain","IP only"],"answer":1,"explain":"scheme://host:port/path?query. Example https://bank.in:443/login. Order Qs.","tag":"web"},{"q":".gov vs .edu vs .com?","options":["All commercial","Govt / education / commercial","All Indian","All free"],"answer":1,"explain":"gTLD meanings: .gov government, .edu education, .com commercial, .org non-profit. Source-credibility hint for GA too.","tag":"web"},{"q":"Cloud storage example?","options":["Pen drive","Google Drive","DVD","RAM"],"answer":1,"explain":"Drive/Dropbox/OneDrive = internet-hosted. Pen drive/DVD = local physical. Location decides.","tag":"cloud"},{"q":"SaaS vs installed software?","options":["Same","SaaS runs in browser, subscription","Installed is safer always","SaaS needs no internet"],"answer":1,"explain":"SaaS (Gmail, Office365): nothing installed, pay-as-go, needs net. Licensed suites install once. Deployment-model contrast.","tag":"cloud"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] File server is?","options":["Manages files, shared on network","Limited to one PC","A fat client, one PC","A compiler"],"answer":0,"explain":"A file server centralizes file storage/operations for network clients. 'Limited to one PC' describes standalone use; fat client is a powerful client machine, not a server. Server = shared service.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Device converting info to digital form?","options":["Modem","Digitizer","Mouse","Light pen"],"answer":1,"explain":"Digitizer converts analog/physical input (drawings, signals) into digital data. Modem converts digital↔analog for lines; mouse/pen are merely input devices. Name says the job: digit-izer.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Hard disks are?","options":["Direct-access storage","Sequential-access","Rare with micros","Both latter"],"answer":0,"explain":"Disks allow jumping to any address (direct/random access), unlike tapes (sequential). That's why OSes run from disks. Direct = addressable.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] OFF switch state represents?","options":["−7","−3.75","1","0"],"answer":3,"explain":"Binary: ON=1, OFF=0. All digital logic bottoms out at this mapping.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Index register auto-incremented with use?","options":["Auto Index","Asynchronous","Assembler","Compiler"],"answer":0,"explain":"Auto-index (auto-increment/decrement) addressing steps through arrays/loops. Assembler/compiler are translators; asynchronous is timing. Name-match Q.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] Computer is meant for?","options":["Quick math only","Input–storage–processing–output","Electronic data processing","Repetitive tasks only"],"answer":2,"explain":"The textbook definition: an electronic data processing machine. (b) lists the IPO cycle (also true-ish) but the standard key is EDP. Definition Qs reward the canonical phrase.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] RAM is 'short memory' because it is?","options":["Volatile","Small capacity","Very expensive","Programmable"],"answer":0,"explain":"Volatile = contents vanish without power — hence 'short' (temporary) memory vs ROM/storage. Volatility is RAM's defining trait.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] 8K RAM holds how many bits?","options":["8000","8192","4000","4096"],"answer":1,"explain":"K=1024 in memory: 8×1024=8192 bits (as bytes: 8Kb = 1KB). 8000 is the decimal trap.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"[PYQ 2013] First electronic calculator (relays, bulbs, batteries)?","options":["Claude Shannon","Konrad Zuse","George Stibitz","Howard Aiken"],"answer":2,"explain":"George Stibitz's Model K / Bell Labs relay calculators (late 1930s). Zuse built mechanical Z-series; Aiken the Harvard Mark I; Shannon founded information theory. Pioneer-name mapping.","tag":"pyq2013"},{"q":"Reply-all vs forward?","options":["Same","Reply-all answers everyone on thread; forward starts new recipients","Both delete","Both encrypt"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reply-all keeps the thread audience; forward re-addresses content fresh. Use BCC with forwards to protect addresses.","tag":"email"},{"q":"PDF readers can't?","options":["View PDFs","Edit like Word by default","Print","Zoom"],"answer":1,"explain":"Readers (Acrobat Reader) view/print/annotate; full editing needs Acrobat Pro or Word conversion. View vs edit licensing split.","tag":"files"},{"q":"Which is an output AND input device?","options":["Keyboard","Touchscreen","Mouse","Speaker"],"answer":1,"explain":"Touchscreen displays (out) and senses touch (in). Keyboard/mouse are input-only; speaker output-only. Dual-role devices break binary classification.","tag":"io"},{"q":"1 nibble = ?","options":["2 bits","4 bits","8 bits","16 bits"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nibble = 4 bits (half a byte); one hex digit per nibble. Byte=8, word=16/32. Unit ladder: bit→nibble→byte.","tag":"memory"}]},{"id":"it","title":"IT Technical","desc":"Specialist 50Q • OS • DBMS • networks • DSA • security","track":"mains","cards":[{"q":"Which is NOT an OS function?","options":["Memory management","Scheduling","Word processing","File management"],"answer":2,"explain":"The OS manages hardware resources: processes (scheduling), memory, files, devices, security. Word processing is an APPLICATION running on top (like MS Word on Windows). Test: if users install it separately, it's not the OS.","tag":"os"},{"q":"Which is NOT a Coffman deadlock condition?","options":["Mutual exclusion","Hold-and-wait","Preemption","Circular wait"],"answer":2,"explain":"Deadlock needs all four: mutual exclusion + hold-and-wait + NO preemption + circular wait. So introducing PREEMPTION (taking resources away) BREAKS deadlock — that's why it's the odd one out. Banker's algorithm avoids deadlock by denying unsafe states.","tag":"os"},{"q":"Minimum average waiting-time scheduling?","options":["FCFS","SJF","Round Robin","LIFO"],"answer":1,"explain":"Shortest Job First always picks the quickest pending job, minimizing average wait (provably optimal for known burst times). FCFS suffers the convoy effect (long job blocks all); Round Robin is fair but slower on average. Preemptive SJF = SRTF.","tag":"os"},{"q":"ACID 'Atomicity' guarantees?","options":["Speed","All-or-nothing","Sharing","Compression"],"answer":1,"explain":"Atomicity = a transaction fully commits or fully rolls back — never half-done. Critical for money movement: a premium debit without its matching credit entry must never persist. (C=valid states, I=concurrent isolation, D=survives crashes.)","tag":"dbms"},{"q":"BCNF vs 3NF?","options":["BCNF weaker","BCNF implies 3NF","Unrelated","3NF implies BCNF"],"answer":1,"explain":"Normal forms nest strictly: 1NF ⊂ 2NF ⊂ 3NF ⊂ BCNF. Every BCNF table is automatically in 3NF (every determinant is a superkey), but a 3NF table can still violate BCNF. Stronger form ⇒ all weaker ones hold.","tag":"dbms"},{"q":"Holders with NO claim?","options":["INNER JOIN","LEFT JOIN + IS NULL","RIGHT JOIN all rows","CROSS JOIN"],"answer":1,"explain":"LEFT JOIN keeps every holder, putting NULLs where no claim matches; WHERE claim.id IS NULL then keeps only the unmatched. INNER JOIN would drop exactly the rows you want. Pattern: 'find X without Y' = LEFT JOIN + IS NULL.","tag":"sql"},{"q":"WHERE vs HAVING?","options":["Same thing","Rows-before vs groups-after grouping","Only HAVING filters","Neither uses aggregates"],"answer":1,"explain":"WHERE filters individual ROWS before grouping (can't use aggregates); HAVING filters GROUPS after GROUP BY (works with COUNT/SUM/AVG). Example: WHERE year=2024 ... GROUP BY branch HAVING COUNT(*)>5.","tag":"sql"},{"q":"192.168.1.10/24 usable hosts?","options":["254","256","510","512"],"answer":0,"explain":"/24 leaves 8 host bits: 2^8=256 addresses minus 2 (network .0 and broadcast .255) = 254 usable (.1–.254). General formula: 2^(32−prefix) − 2. A /25 would give 126.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"Reliable claim upload: TCP or UDP?","options":["UDP","TCP","ICMP","ARP"],"answer":1,"explain":"TCP is connection-oriented: sequence numbers, ACKs, retransmission and ordering guarantee every byte arrives — exactly what document uploads need. UDP skips all that for speed (live video, DNS, gaming) and may lose data. Reliability ⇒ TCP.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"Balanced BST search, n≈1M?","options":["O(1)","O(log n) ≈ 20","O(n)","O(n²)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each comparison halves the search: log₂(1M) ≈ 20 steps. Unbalanced (sorted inserts) degrades to a linked list: O(n). Self-balancing trees (AVL/Red-Black) exist precisely to hold the O(log n) guarantee. Hash tables average O(1) but worst-case O(n).","tag":"dsa"},{"q":"Bulk-data encryption: AES or RSA?","options":["RSA","AES","SHA-256","MD5"],"answer":1,"explain":"AES (symmetric, one shared key) is ~1000× faster — used for the actual data. RSA (asymmetric, key pair) only exchanges keys and signs. SHA-256/MD5 are HASHES (one-way fingerprints), not encryption at all. Real systems are hybrid: RSA handshake, AES session (that's HTTPS).","tag":"crypto"},{"q":"IaaS vs SaaS example?","options":["EC2 vs Gmail","Gmail vs EC2","Both are PaaS","Both on-prem"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stack from bottom: IaaS rents raw machines (EC2 VMs you administer), PaaS rents platforms (databases, runtimes), SaaS delivers finished apps (Gmail, policy portal). Rule: the more THEY manage, the higher up the stack.","tag":"cloud"},{"q":"fork() in Unix does?","options":["Deletes a file","Creates a child process","Allocates disk","Sends a packet"],"answer":1,"explain":"fork() clones the calling process into parent + child (copy-on-write memory), returning the child PID to the parent and 0 to the child. exec() then replaces the child's program image. fork+exec is how every Unix command launches.","tag":"os"},{"q":"Paging vs segmentation?","options":["Same thing","Fixed pages vs variable segments","Both variable","Both in registers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Paging chops memory into FIXED-size pages/frames (simple, but internal fragmentation wastes tails). Segmentation uses VARIABLE, logical chunks (code/stack/heap per meaning). x86 historically combined both; pure paging dominates today.","tag":"os"},{"q":"Thrashing means?","options":["CPU overheating","Excessive paging, no progress","Disk fragmentation","Deadlock"],"answer":1,"explain":"Thrashing = the system spends all time swapping pages in/out instead of executing (CPU utilization collapses while disk is maxed). Cures: give the process more frames, suspend processes, or fix the page-replacement/global allocation policy.","tag":"os"},{"q":"2NF forbids?","options":["Duplicate rows","Partial dependency on part of key","Transitive dependency","Nulls"],"answer":1,"explain":"2NF bans PARTIAL dependency: a non-key column depending on only PART of a composite key (e.g. course-name depending on course-id inside an enrollment keyed by student+course). Fix: split into two tables. Transitive dependency is 3NF's target, not 2NF's.","tag":"dbms"},{"q":"Primary vs foreign key?","options":["Both unique everywhere","PK uniquely identifies; FK references another table","FK is always numeric","PK can repeat"],"answer":1,"explain":"Primary Key: unique + non-null identifier of its own table (ClaimID). Foreign Key: a column holding another table's PK to link rows (PolicyNo → Policy table), enforcing referential integrity. Example schema: Claim(ClaimID PK, PolicyNo FK).","tag":"dbms"},{"q":"Index trade-off?","options":["Speeds reads AND writes","Speeds reads, slows writes","No effect","Only for views"],"answer":1,"explain":"A B+ tree index finds rows in O(log n) instead of full scans — but every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE must also maintain the index, costing time and space. Index hot search columns; never index everything.","tag":"dbms"},{"q":"Dirty reads happen at?","options":["READ UNCOMMITTED","SERIALIZABLE","REPEATABLE READ","All levels"],"answer":0,"explain":"Isolation ladder (weak→strong): READ UNCOMMITTED (allows dirty reads) → READ COMMITTED → REPEATABLE READ → SERIALIZABLE (blocks dirty, non-repeatable AND phantom reads, at max locking cost). Dirty = reading someone else's uncommitted, possibly rolled-back data.","tag":"dbms"},{"q":"IP belongs to which OSI layer?","options":["Data Link","Network (L3)","Transport","Application"],"answer":1,"explain":"Layer map: L1 Physical (cables) → L2 Data Link (MAC/switch) → L3 Network (IP/routing) → L4 Transport (TCP/UDP ports) → L5-7 Session/Presentation/Application (HTTP/DNS). Mnemonic: 'Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away'.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"DNS uses port?","options":["25","53","80","443"],"answer":1,"explain":"DNS = 53 (mostly UDP, TCP for big replies). Neighbours: SMTP 25 (mail send), HTTP 80, HTTPS 443, FTP 21, SSH 22. Port Qs test whether you confuse DNS (53) with web (80/443) — don't.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"OSPF vs BGP?","options":["Both exterior","Interior link-state vs exterior path-vector","Both distance-vector","Same protocol"],"answer":1,"explain":"OSPF = Interior Gateway Protocol: link-state, Dijkstra, fast convergence INSIDE one autonomous system. BGP = Exterior: path-vector policy routing BETWEEN autonomous systems (the internet's glue). RIP (hop-count ≤15) is the toy interior one.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"Stateful firewall vs packet filter?","options":["Same","Stateful tracks connection state","Filter is stronger","Neither inspects"],"answer":1,"explain":"Packet filters judge each packet alone by header (IP/port) — blind to context. Stateful firewalls track connection state (SYN established? part of known flow?), blocking spoofed/out-of-state packets. VPNs (IPsec/SSL) then encrypt branch-to-DC traffic.","tag":"security"},{"q":"HTTP 500 means?","options":["OK","Not found","Server error","Redirect"],"answer":2,"explain":"Families: 2xx success (200 OK, 201 Created), 3xx redirect, 4xx CLIENT fault (400 bad request, 401 unauthorised, 403 forbidden, 404 not found), 5xx SERVER fault (500). First digit tells you whose fault it is.","tag":"web"},{"q":"Print jobs waiting use?","options":["Stack","Queue","Tree","Graph"],"answer":1,"explain":"Print spooling is FIFO: first submitted prints first → queue (enqueue/dequeue). Stacks are LIFO: undo, back-button, recursion, DFS. Data structure follows the real-world order discipline.","tag":"dsa"},{"q":"Quicksort worst case?","options":["O(n)","O(n log n)","O(n²) on sorted pivot","O(2ⁿ)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Average O(n log n), but a bad pivot (e.g. first element on already-sorted data) partitions 0 vs n−1 repeatedly → O(n²). Fixes: random/median-of-three pivots. Merge sort guarantees O(n log n) but needs O(n) extra space.","tag":"dsa"},{"q":"Unweighted shortest path uses?","options":["DFS","BFS","Toposort","Kruskal"],"answer":1,"explain":"BFS expands level by level (queue), so the first time it reaches the target is via the fewest hops. DFS dives deep (stack) — good for connectivity/cycles, not shortest paths. Weighted graphs need Dijkstra (no negatives) or Bellman-Ford.","tag":"dsa"},{"q":"OOP pillars include?","options":["Looping, recursion","Encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, abstraction","Compiling, linking","Testing, debug"],"answer":1,"explain":"The four: Encapsulation (hide internals, e.g. premium calc inside Policy class), Abstraction (interfaces), Inheritance (HealthPolicy extends Policy), Polymorphism (overridden calculatePremium()). Insurance-domain example sticks because NICL code is OOP-shaped.","tag":"oop"},{"q":"Overloading vs overriding?","options":["Same thing","Compile-time signatures vs runtime inheritance","Both runtime","Both need virtual"],"answer":1,"explain":"OverLOADING = same name, different parameters, resolved at COMPILE time. OverRIDING = subclass redefines an inherited method, resolved at RUNTIME (virtual dispatch). Same spelling family, opposite mechanisms — exams love the pair.","tag":"oop"},{"q":"*p in C means?","options":["Address of p","Value p points to","Multiply p","Size of p"],"answer":1,"explain":"* DEREFERENCES: *p is the value stored at the address p holds. & does the reverse (&a = address of a). So int *p=&a; *p=7 writes 7 into a. sizeof(pointer) is fixed (4/8 bytes) regardless of type.","tag":"c"},{"q":"Java is platform-independent via?","options":["Pointers","JVM bytecode","Multiple inheritance","Macros"],"answer":1,"explain":"Java compiles to BYTECODE, and each platform's JVM executes it — write once, run anywhere. C++ compiles to native machine code per platform (plus pointers and multiple class inheritance, which Java drops in favour of interfaces).","tag":"java"},{"q":"Python tuple vs list?","options":["Both mutable","Tuple immutable","Tuple is key-value","List is unordered"],"answer":1,"explain":"List [] = ordered + MUTABLE (append/pop). Tuple () = ordered + IMMUTABLE (hashable → dict keys). Dict {k:v} = key-value; set {} = unique unordered. Mutability decides where each fits.","tag":"python"},{"q":"Prevent SQL injection with?","options":["String concat","Prepared/parameterized queries","Hiding buttons","Client validation only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Concatenating user input into SQL lets attackers smuggle queries (' OR '1'='1). Prepared statements send code and data separately, so input can never execute. Add least-privilege DB users + server-side validation. Client-side checks alone are cosmetic.","tag":"security"},{"q":"Waterfall vs Agile?","options":["Same","Sequential phases vs iterative sprints","Agile has no testing","Waterfall is faster"],"answer":1,"explain":"Waterfall = sequential requirements→design→code→test→deploy (rigid, big-bang). Agile/Scrum = 2–4 week sprints with backlog + standups, shipping working increments (adaptive). V-model tests each dev stage in parallel. Match methodology to requirement stability.","tag":"se"},{"q":"UAT is?","options":["Unit test by dev","Acceptance by business users","Compiler check","Load test"],"answer":1,"explain":"Test ladder: Unit (single function, e.g. JUnit) → Integration (modules together) → System (end-to-end) → UAT (BUSINESS users accept/reject). Black-box tests behaviour without code; white-box uses internals.","tag":"se"},{"q":"First compiler phase?","options":["Code generation","Lexical analysis (tokens)","Optimization","Linking"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pipeline: LEXICAL (source → tokens) → syntax/parsing (tokens → tree) → semantic (type-check) → intermediate code → optimization → code generation; linker+loader run after. Lexical errors are the 'unknown character' class.","tag":"compiler"},{"q":"Fastest memory?","options":["HDD","RAM","L1 cache","SSD"],"answer":2,"explain":"Speed ladder: registers > L1 > L2 > L3 > RAM > SSD > HDD/optical. Caches exploit locality (recent/repeated data). Bigger is slower and cheaper — that's the whole memory hierarchy trade.","tag":"arch"},{"q":"CIA triad?","options":["Central Intel Agency","Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability","Compile, Install, Assemble","None"],"answer":1,"explain":"Security's three goals: keep secrets SECRET (confidentiality), data UNALTERED (integrity, via hashes/signatures), systems REACHABLE (availability, anti-DDoS). Phishing hits confidentiality; ransomware hits availability; 2FA/MFA defends both.","tag":"security"},{"q":"SaaS example?","options":["EC2 VMs","Gmail","Docker","Linux kernel"],"answer":1,"explain":"SaaS = finished app over the web (Gmail, policy portal). IaaS = rented infrastructure (EC2). PaaS = rented platform (RDS, App Engine). Docker = containers (packaging), kernel = OS core — neither is a service model.","tag":"cloud"},{"q":"Supervised vs unsupervised ML?","options":["Same","Labeled fraud yes/no vs clustering","Only neural nets","No data needed"],"answer":1,"explain":"Supervised learns from LABELLED examples (fraud yes/no classification, premium regression). Unsupervised finds structure in UNLABELLED data (customer clustering/segmentation). NLP uses both: parsers today are mostly supervised.","tag":"ai"},{"q":"Process vs thread?","options":["Same","Process isolated program; thread lightweight unit sharing memory","Thread bigger","Both hardware"],"answer":1,"explain":"Process = own address space (Chrome tabs). Threads = share process memory, cheap context switch (a server handling 1000 clients). Share-vs-isolate is the split.","tag":"os"},{"q":"Semaphore vs mutex?","options":["Same","Mutex single-owner lock; semaphore counted permits","Both files","Both printers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Mutex = one holder (binary ownership). Semaphore = N permits (pool of 3 printers). Counting vs ownership.","tag":"os"},{"q":"Banker's algorithm prevents?","options":["Viruses","Deadlock via safe-state check","Fragmentation","Overheating"],"answer":1,"explain":"Simulates allocation: grants only if a SAFE sequence exists. Avoidance (not detection/recovery). Dijkstra's classic.","tag":"os"},{"q":"Demand paging loads pages?","options":["All at start","On page fault, when needed","Never","Only on boot"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lazy loading: first access faults, OS fetches page. Faster startup, less RAM — thrashing is its failure mode.","tag":"os"},{"q":"System call example?","options":["printf()","fork()/read()/open()","main()","x++"],"answer":1,"explain":"Syscalls cross into kernel (open/read/write/fork/exec). printf is a LIBRARY call wrapping write(). Boundary defines it.","tag":"os"},{"q":"Deadlock detection vs prevention?","options":["Same","Detection finds cycles after; prevention designs them out","Both need reboot","Neither exists"],"answer":1,"explain":"Prevention/avoidance (ordering, Banker) stop deadlocks; detection (wait-for graphs) + recovery (kill/preempt) cure them. Strategy families.","tag":"os"},{"q":"Context switch cost comes from?","options":["Saving/loading registers, caches, TLB","Printing","Disk size","Monitor"],"answer":0,"explain":"Swapping processes saves registers/PC/stack + flushes TLB/cache → overhead. Threads switch cheaper (shared memory). Why excess switching hurts.","tag":"os"},{"q":"ER: rectangle vs diamond vs oval?","options":["All entities","Entity / relationship / attribute","All keys","All tables"],"answer":1,"explain":"Chen notation: rectangle=entity, diamond=relationship, oval=attribute, underline=key. Diagram-vocabulary Q.","tag":"dbms"},{"q":"INNER vs LEFT vs FULL JOIN?","options":["Same rows","Only matches / all-left+matches / everything both sides","All identical","Only counts"],"answer":1,"explain":"INNER: matches only. LEFT: all left + matches (NULLs fill). FULL: union both sides. Venn diagrams map each.","tag":"sql"},{"q":"Subquery vs JOIN?","options":["Same always","Subquery nests SELECT; JOIN merges tables — often interchangeable","Subqueries illegal","JOINs slower always"],"answer":1,"explain":"Subquery = query-inside-query (WHERE x IN (SELECT...)); JOIN = side-by-side merge. Same results often; optimizer/performance differ. Know both forms.","tag":"sql"},{"q":"VIEW vs TABLE?","options":["Same storage","View = saved query (virtual); table stores rows","View is faster always","Tables can't index"],"answer":1,"explain":"Views store DEFINITION, compute on access (security/abstraction layer). Materialized views precompute. Virtual vs physical.","tag":"dbms"},{"q":"Stored procedure vs trigger?","options":["Same","Procedure called explicitly; trigger fires on events","Both manual","Both automatic"],"answer":1,"explain":"Procedure: CALL it (parameters, logic). Trigger: auto-fires on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE (audit trails). Explicit vs event-driven.","tag":"dbms"},{"q":"NoSQL document store example?","options":["MySQL","MongoDB (JSON docs)","Oracle","PostgreSQL"],"answer":1,"explain":"Document DBs (MongoDB, CouchDB) store JSON-like docs, flexible schema. Key-value (Redis), column (Cassandra), graph (Neo4j) are siblings.","tag":"dbms"},{"q":"DDL vs DML?","options":["Same","DDL defines structure (CREATE/ALTER/DROP); DML manipulates rows (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE)","Both query","Both admin"],"answer":1,"explain":"DDL = schema verbs (+TRUNCATE); DML = data verbs (+SELECT, strictly DQL). Command-family sorting.","tag":"sql"},{"q":"TCL COMMIT vs ROLLBACK?","options":["Same","Commit saves; rollback undoes to last commit","Both delete","Both lock"],"answer":1,"explain":"Transaction Control: COMMIT makes permanent, ROLLBACK reverts, SAVEPOINT marks spots. ACID in action.","tag":"sql"},{"q":"aggregate + GROUP BY + HAVING order?","options":["Any order","WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → ORDER BY","HAVING first","ORDER BY first"],"answer":1,"explain":"Logical order: filter rows (WHERE) → group → filter groups (HAVING) → sort. Aggregates (COUNT/SUM/AVG/MIN/MAX) feed HAVING, never WHERE.","tag":"sql"},{"q":"DISTINCT vs GROUP BY?","options":["Same output always","DISTINCT dedupes rows; GROUP BY aggregates per group","GROUP BY sorts","DISTINCT sums"],"answer":0,"explain":"Trick: SELECT DISTINCT col ≡ GROUP BY col with no aggregates — same rows. They diverge once aggregates appear. Equivalence insight.","tag":"sql"},{"q":"LIKE wildcards % vs _?","options":["Same","% any length; _ exactly one char","_ any length","Both exact"],"answer":1,"explain":"'A%' = starts with A; 'A_' = A + exactly one char; '%A%' = contains. Pattern-length control.","tag":"sql"},{"q":"Constraints: PK vs UNIQUE vs CHECK?","options":["Identical","PK unique+not-null single; UNIQUE allows nulls; CHECK validates values","All nullable","None enforced"],"answer":1,"explain":"PRIMARY KEY: unique, NOT NULL, one per table. UNIQUE: unique, nulls ok, many. CHECK: value rules (age>0). NOT NULL/DEFAULT/FOREIGN KEY complete the set.","tag":"sql"},{"q":"TCP 3-way handshake order?","options":["SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK","ACK, SYN, FIN","Hello, Hi, Bye","GET, POST, 200"],"answer":0,"explain":"SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK establishes sequence numbers both ways. FIN/4-way teardown closes. Connection setup ritual.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"UDP used for?","options":["File transfer","DNS, streaming, gaming (fast, lossy ok)","Banking","Email"],"answer":1,"explain":"Where speed beats reliability: DNS lookups, live media, games. TCP where data must survive. Use-case split.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"Class C private range?","options":["10.x.x.x","172.16.x.x","192.168.x.x","8.8.8.8"],"answer":2,"explain":"Private: 10/8 (A), 172.16/12 (B), 192.168/16 (C). Public 8.8.8.8 is Google DNS. NAT maps private↔public.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"/26 usable hosts?","options":["62","126","254","510"],"answer":0,"explain":"32−26=6 host bits → 2^6−2 = 64−2 = 62 usable. Same 2^h−2 formula as /24 (254): only h changes.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"NAT does?","options":["Encrypts","Maps private IPs to public","Assigns DNS","Speeds CPU"],"answer":1,"explain":"Network Address Translation lets whole LAN share one public IP (home routers). Solves IPv4 scarcity alongside private ranges.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"VLAN purpose?","options":["Faster cables","Logical LAN segments on shared switches","More ports","Wireless only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Virtual LANs split one physical switch into isolated broadcast domains (HR vs Guest). Segmentation without new hardware.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"OSI layers count vs TCP/IP?","options":["7 vs 4","4 vs 7","Same 5","9 vs 3"],"answer":0,"explain":"OSI 7 (P-D-N-T-S-P-A); TCP/IP 4 (Link, Internet, Transport, Application). Exams ask the pair together.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"CSMA/CD used in?","options":["Modern switched Ethernet","Classic shared Ethernet (hubs)","Wi-Fi","Fiber only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Carrier-sense + collision DETECT for shared coax/hub Ethernet. Wi-Fi uses CSMA/CA (avoidance). Switches made collisions rare.","tag":"networks"},{"q":"Array vs linked list?","options":["Same","Array contiguous+indexed; linked nodes+pointers","Linked is indexed","Array has no size"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arrays: O(1) index, fixed size, costly insert. Linked lists: O(n) traverse, cheap insert, extra pointer memory. Access-vs-mutation trade.","tag":"dsa"},{"q":"Inorder traversal of BST gives?","options":["Random order","Sorted ascending","Descending","Level order"],"answer":1,"explain":"Left-root-right on a BST yields sorted order — the classic BST property test. Preorder=root first, postorder=root last.","tag":"dsa"},{"q":"Hash collision handled by?","options":["Ignoring","Chaining / open addressing","Sorting","Rebooting"],"answer":1,"explain":"Chaining (bucket lists) or open addressing (probe next slot). Perfect hashing avoids them; load factor triggers rehash.","tag":"dsa"},{"q":"Recursion needs?","options":["Loops only","Base case + self-call","Goto","Global vars"],"answer":1,"explain":"Base case stops it; recursive case shrinks toward base. Missing base = stack overflow. Every recursive definition has both.","tag":"dsa"},{"q":"Big-O rank: log n vs n vs n log n?","options":["n < log n < n log n","log n < n < n log n","All equal","n log n smallest"],"answer":1,"explain":"1 < log n < n < n log n < n² < 2ⁿ. Binary search log, linear scan n, mergesort n log n.","tag":"dsa"},{"q":"Stable sort example?","options":["Quick","Merge","Heap","Selection"],"answer":1,"explain":"Stable = equal keys keep original order: Merge (and Bubble/Insertion). Quick/Heap/Selection are unstable. Stability matters for multi-key sorts.","tag":"dsa"},{"q":"Encapsulation example?","options":["Global vars","Private balance + deposit() methods","Copy-paste code","Long functions"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hide data (private fields), expose controlled methods (Account with private balance, public deposit/withdraw validating). Data+methods bundle = class.","tag":"oop"},{"q":"Abstraction via?","options":["Global state","Interfaces/abstract classes hiding implementation","More code","Comments"],"answer":1,"explain":"Show WHAT (drive()), hide HOW (engine internals) — interfaces/abstract classes. Encapsulation hides DATA; abstraction hides COMPLEXITY.","tag":"oop"},{"q":"Inheritance types include?","options":["Only single","Single, multilevel, multiple (C++), hierarchical","No types","Only private"],"answer":1,"explain":"Single (A→B), multilevel (A→B→C), multiple (C++: A,B→C; Java uses interfaces instead), hierarchical (one parent, many children).","tag":"oop"},{"q":"Polymorphism compile vs runtime?","options":["Same time","Overloading compile-time; overriding runtime","Both runtime","Neither exists"],"answer":1,"explain":"Same answer family as before from the other side: overload=signatures at compile; override=virtual dispatch at runtime. Bidirectional mastery.","tag":"oop"},{"q":"printf vs scanf?","options":["Same","printf outputs; scanf reads input","Both read","Both write files"],"answer":1,"explain":"printf = formatted OUTPUT; scanf = formatted INPUT (&vars needed). Format strings (%d %s %f) mirror each other.","tag":"c"},{"q":"C arrays start at?","options":["1","0","−1","Any"],"answer":1,"explain":"Zero-indexed: a[0] first, a[n−1] last. Off-by-one errors live here. Strings end with null terminator.","tag":"c"},{"q":"JVM vs JRE vs JDK?","options":["Same","JVM runs bytecode; JRE=JVM+libraries; JDK=JRE+compiler/tools","JDK smallest","JVM compiles"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nesting dolls: JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM. Need only RUN? JRE. DEVELOP? JDK. Clean layering Q.","tag":"java"},{"q":"Java access: private vs protected?","options":["Same","private class-only; protected package+subclasses","Both public","Both global"],"answer":1,"explain":"private < default(package) < protected < public in visibility. Encapsulation enforcement levels.","tag":"java"},{"q":"Python indentation does?","options":["Nothing","Defines blocks (no braces)","Only style","Slows code"],"answer":1,"explain":"Leading whitespace IS syntax: loops/functions/classes nest by indent. Forced readability; mixing tabs/spaces breaks.","tag":"python"},{"q":"List comprehension [x*2 for x in a] does?","options":["Filters only","Builds doubled list concisely","Sorts","Deletes"],"answer":1,"explain":"One-line map/filter: [expr for x in iter if cond]. Pythonic replacement for map()+filter().","tag":"python"},{"q":"None in Python is?","options":["Zero","Null/nothing singleton","Empty string","False only"],"answer":1,"explain":"None = absence of value (like null). Distinct from 0/''/False (though falsy). Default returns, missing dict.get().","tag":"python"},{"q":"pip does?","options":["Edits video","Installs Python packages","Compiles C","Runs servers"],"answer":1,"explain":"pip install numpy ← PyPI package manager. requirements.txt freezes versions. Tooling basics.","tag":"python"},{"q":"Scrum roles?","options":["None","Product Owner, Scrum Master, Dev Team","Manager only","Testers only"],"answer":1,"explain":"PO owns backlog/priorities, SM guards process, team builds. Ceremonies: planning, daily standup, review, retrospective.","tag":"se"},{"q":"Git commit vs push?","options":["Same","Commit snapshots locally; push uploads to remote","Both remote","Both delete"],"answer":1,"explain":"commit = local checkpoint; push = publish to origin; pull/fetch = download. Distributed VCS mental model.","tag":"se"},{"q":"Linker vs loader?","options":["Same","Linker combines objects+libraries; loader places in memory to run","Both edit source","Both run code"],"answer":1,"explain":"Compile→assemble→LINK (resolve symbols, one binary)→LOAD (memory layout, execute). Toolchain order.","tag":"compiler"},{"q":"Cache hit ratio high means?","options":["Slow","Most accesses served fast","Full disk","No RAM"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hit% = requests found in cache. High hits → near-cache speed (locality working). Miss penalty = fetch from slower level.","tag":"arch"},{"q":"Pipelining speeds CPU by?","options":["Higher clock only","Overlapping instruction stages","Bigger cache","More RAM"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fetch/decode/execute overlap like an assembly line (hazards stall it). Throughput, not latency, improves.","tag":"arch"},{"q":"8085 is?","options":["16-bit","8-bit microprocessor","64-bit","Not a CPU"],"answer":1,"explain":"Intel 8085: 8-bit data bus (16-bit address). 8086 = 16-bit successor. Bit-width history.","tag":"arch"},{"q":"Digital signature gives?","options":["Encryption only","Authenticity+integrity+non-repudiation","Speed","Compression"],"answer":1,"explain":"Private-key sign + public-key verify: proves WHO sent (auth), unaltered (integrity), can't deny (non-repudiation). Hash-then-sign construction.","tag":"crypto"},{"q":"Hash (SHA-256) used for?","options":["Encrypt files","Integrity fingerprints, password storage","Send email","Browse web"],"answer":1,"explain":"One-way digests: verify downloads, store salted password hashes (never plaintext). Not encryption (no decryption).","tag":"crypto"},{"q":"Social engineering attacks?","options":["Only malware","Human manipulation (pretexting, baiting, tailgating)","Hardware faults","Network configs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Exploits people, not code: fake IT calls (pretexting), infected USBs (baiting), following through doors (tailgating). Defense = verification culture.","tag":"security"},{"q":"XSS vs SQLi?","options":["Same","XSS injects scripts into pages; SQLi into queries","Both hardware","Both solved by firewall"],"answer":1,"explain":"XSS = malicious JS runs in VICTIMS' browsers (escape output, CSP). SQLi = malicious SQL runs on YOUR DB (parameterize). Output vs input side.","tag":"security"},{"q":"Public vs private vs hybrid cloud?","options":["Same","Shared multi-tenant / single-org / mixed","All offline","All free"],"answer":1,"explain":"Public (AWS shared), Private (dedicated, on/off-prem), Hybrid (mix + burst). Banks often hybrid for control + scale.","tag":"cloud"},{"q":"Cloud elasticity means?","options":["Fixed servers","Auto scale with demand, pay-per-use","Only storage","Manual reboot"],"answer":1,"explain":"Scale out/in automatically; metered billing. Opposite of provisioned data-center capacity planning. Measured service (NIST).","tag":"cloud"},{"q":"Train/test split purpose?","options":["More data","Honest evaluation on unseen data","Faster training","Less storage"],"answer":1,"explain":"Train on one slice, TEST on held-out slice — catches overfitting (memorized, not learned). Typical 80/20. No test set = no credibility.","tag":"ai"},{"q":"Overfitting signs?","options":["Both accuracies low","Train high, test low","Both high","Fast training"],"answer":1,"explain":"Memorized training noise: 99% train, 70% test. Cures: more data, regularization, simpler model, cross-validation. Underfitting = both low.","tag":"ai"},{"q":"Firewall default-deny means?","options":["Block everything incl. needed","Block all except explicitly allowed","Allow all","No rules"],"answer":1,"explain":"Default-deny (whitelist): only permitted traffic passes — fail-closed security. Default-allow is fail-open and dangerous. Posture decides breach impact.","tag":"security"}]}],"quizzes":[{"id":"prelim-mock-1","title":"Prelims Mock 1","desc":"25 questions • 25 min • −0.25","track":"prelims","questions":[{"q":"Yesterday I met my friend who is now a doctor and ___ musician. Find the error.","options":["Yesterday I met","my friend who","is now a doctor","and a musician"],"answer":3,"explain":"Rule: when two nouns describe the SAME person and are joined by 'and', the article comes only before the first noun. Here the friend is one person who is both doctor and musician, so the second 'a' is wrong. Correct sentence: '...is now a doctor and musician.' If they were two different people, both articles would stay.","tag":"article","deck":"english"},{"q":"Choose the correct sentence.","options":["Despite of several disputes, they stayed.","Despite several disputes, they stayed.","Despite of the fact they stayed.","Although of disputes they stayed."],"answer":1,"explain":"'Despite' is already a complete preposition and never takes 'of' — the error 'despite of' is a classic trap. Its cousin 'in spite' DOES need 'of' ('in spite of disputes'). 'Although' is a conjunction, so 'although of' is never correct.","tag":"preposition","deck":"english"},{"q":"...a number of emerging ___.","options":["economy","economies","economical","economize"],"answer":1,"explain":"'A number of' always takes a PLURAL noun — it means 'several'. Contrast with 'the number of', which takes a singular verb ('The number of cases IS rising'). Also, only the noun 'economies' fits; 'economical' is an adjective.","tag":"grammar","deck":"english"},{"q":"Antonym of CONSTANT?","options":["fitful","perpetual","prevalent","entrust"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Constant' = steady, unchanging. 'Fitful' = irregular, starting and stopping — the direct opposite. Watch out: 'perpetual' (never-ending) is a SYNONYM of constant, and 'prevalent' means widespread, which is unrelated.","tag":"vocab","deck":"english"},{"q":"Spot the misspelt word: plosible / explanation / government","options":["plosible","explanation","government","No error"],"answer":0,"explain":"The word is PLAUSIBLE (p-l-a-u-s-i-b-l-e), meaning reasonable or believable. Remember it with 'applause': plau-sible. 'Explanation' and 'government' (note: govern + ment, no extra n) are spelt correctly.","tag":"spelling","deck":"english"},{"q":"Women spend hours ___ water.","options":["fetching","captivating","abandoning","conveying"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Fetching' means going somewhere and bringing something back — exactly what collecting water involves. 'Captivating' means fascinating people, 'abandoning' means leaving behind, 'conveying' means communicating ideas. Only fetching fits daily water collection.","tag":"vocab","deck":"english"},{"q":"Neither of the answers ___ correct.","options":["are","is","were","have"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Neither of' is always singular — think of it as 'not one and not the other'. So it takes 'is', never 'are/were/have'. Same rule for 'either of' and 'each of': 'Each of the boys HAS a pen.'","tag":"sva","deck":"english"},{"q":"All pens are books. Some books are chairs. Valid conclusion?","options":["Some pens are chairs (definite)","Either 'some pens are chairs' or 'no pen is chair'","All pens are chairs","No conclusion possible"],"answer":1,"explain":"All + Some yields NO definite conclusion (the middle term 'books' isn't distributed to link pens to chairs). But 'Some pens are chairs' and 'No pen is a chair' are complementary (same subject-predicate, one Some one No) — one must be true. Hence either-or.","tag":"syllogism","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"'Only a few Perk is Kitkat' implies?","options":["Some are + some are not (both definite)","Only 'some are'","Only 'some are not'","Nothing definite"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Only a few' is special: it guarantees BOTH 'Some Perk are Kitkat' AND 'Some Perk are NOT Kitkat' as definite conclusions. It behaves like Some + Some-not combined — a favourite trap because test-takers treat it as plain 'some'.","tag":"syllogism","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"CAT → DBU. DOG → ?","options":["EPH","DPH","EOH","CNF"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each letter shifts +1: C→D, A→B, T→U. Apply to DOG: D→E, O→P, G→H = EPH. First check constant shifts (+1/+2) before trying complex patterns.","tag":"coding","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"'She is my grandfather's only son's daughter.' She is?","options":["Sister","Mother","Aunt","Cousin"],"answer":0,"explain":"Work inside-out: grandfather's only son = my father. Father's daughter = my sister (or self if the speaker is female — with 'she', it's sister). Inside-out decoding prevents the classic mistake of jumping to aunt.","tag":"blood","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"8 around a circle facing centre. R's right side is?","options":["Clockwise","Anti-clockwise","Towards centre","Cannot say"],"answer":1,"explain":"Facing the centre, your right hand points anti-clockwise around the circle. Verify: stand at the top facing down (centre) — your right is the left side of the page = anti-clockwise direction. Facing AWAY from centre flips it.","tag":"seating","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Govt bans plastic. Assumption 'plastic is harmful'?","options":["Implicit","Not implicit","Both","Neither"],"answer":0,"explain":"An assumption must be unstated yet NECESSARY for the statement. A ban only makes sense if plastic is considered harmful → implicit. 'People will obey' is NOT implicit (compliance isn't required for the decision to ban). Test: negate it — if negation kills the statement, it's implicit.","tag":"critical","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Facing south, turn left, then left again. Facing?","options":["South","East","North","West"],"answer":2,"explain":"Facing south, your left is east: South→East. Turn left again from east: left of east is north. So North. Two lefts (or two rights) = U-turn to the opposite direction.","tag":"direction","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"'He is the son of my grandmother's only son.' He is my?","options":["Father","Brother","Uncle","Cousin"],"answer":1,"explain":"Grandmother's only son = my father. Father's son = my brother. Note: if the speaker is male it's 'brother'; the phrasing points to a male relative of the same generation → brother.","tag":"blood","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"C top floor, A just below C, B just below A (5 floors). B's floor?","options":["2nd","3rd","4th","5th"],"answer":1,"explain":"Stack from top: C=5th, A=4th (just below C), B=3rd (just below A). 'Just below/above' means immediately adjacent — the strongest, easiest clue; always place it first.","tag":"puzzle","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Rs.4800 for 2y at 20% SI. Interest reinvested 2y at 10% CI. Interest from Y?","options":["Rs.384","Rs.403.2","Rs.472.4","Rs.503.8"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1 — SI from X: 4800 × 20% × 2 = Rs.1920. Step 2 — this 1920 becomes principal for Y: amount = 1920 × 1.1 × 1.1 = Rs.2323.2. Step 3 — CI interest = amount − principal = 2323.2 − 1920 = Rs.403.2. Trap: 384 is 20% of 1920 (one year's interest), not two years compounded.","tag":"si-ci","deck":"quant"},{"q":"Train A: pole 16s, 600m platform 36s. Speed?","options":["25 m/s","30 m/s","35 m/s","40 m/s"],"answer":1,"explain":"The extra 20 seconds (36−16) cover exactly the 600m platform, so speed = 600/20 = 30 m/s. Train length = 30×16 = 480m. Shortcut for these Qs: speed = platform length ÷ (platform time − pole time).","tag":"train","deck":"quant"},{"q":"Series: 3, 29, 127, 345, ? (odd cubes +2)","options":["511","731","999","1333"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each term is an odd cube plus 2: 1³+2=3, 3³+2=29, 5³+2=127, 7³+2=345, so 9³+2=729+2=731. Clue: numbers just above perfect cubes (127, 345) scream 'cube ± constant'.","tag":"series","deck":"quant"},{"q":"m²−24m+135=0. Roots?","options":["15, 9","12, 12","15, −9","27, 5"],"answer":0,"explain":"Need two numbers multiplying to +135 with sum +24 (signs: −24m means both negative factors... actually both numbers are positive 15 and 9 since −15×−9=+135 and −15−9=−24). So m=15 or 9. Rule: for x²−Sx+P, find factor pairs of P summing to S.","tag":"quadratic","deck":"quant"},{"q":"144 km/h train crosses a pole in 12.5s. Length?","options":["400m","450m","500m","550m"],"answer":2,"explain":"Convert km/h to m/s with ×5/18: 144×5/18 = 40 m/s. A pole means distance = train's own length = speed × time = 40×12.5 = 500m. Memorize ×5/18 (km/h→m/s) and ×18/5 (reverse).","tag":"train","deck":"quant"},{"q":"10 people, average weight 50 kg. Total?","options":["450","500","550","600"],"answer":1,"explain":"Total = average × count = 50×10 = 500 kg. Nearly every average Q is solved by converting to totals first, operating, then converting back.","tag":"average","deck":"quant"},{"q":"48% of 650 + 22% of 350 = ?","options":["389","399","379","409"],"answer":0,"explain":"Split for mental math: 48% of 650 = (50%−2%) of 650 = 325−13 = 312. 22% of 350 = 10%×2 (=70) + 2% (=7) = 77. Total 312+77 = 389.","tag":"simplification","deck":"quant"},{"q":"Series: 5, 11, 23, 47, ?","options":["71","83","95","119"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each term doubles and adds 1: 5×2+1=11, 11×2+1=23, 23×2+1=47, so 47×2+1=95. Pattern family: ×2±1, ×3±2 — test these before anything exotic.","tag":"series","deck":"quant"},{"q":"x²−11x+30=0. Roots?","options":["5, 6","3, 10","−5, −6","15, 2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Need factors of 30 summing to 11: 5 and 6 (5×6=30, 5+6=11), both positive because the middle sign is negative... check: (x−5)(x−6)=x²−11x+30 ✓. So x=5 or 6. Option (b) multiplies to 30 but sums to 13 — always verify the sum.","tag":"quadratic","deck":"quant"}]},{"id":"prelim-mock-2","title":"Prelims Mock 2","desc":"25 questions • 25 min • −0.25","track":"prelims","questions":[{"q":"She ___ in hilly areas since her childhood.","options":["lives","has been living","lived","will live"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Since + a starting point in the past' (her childhood) with an action continuing to now always needs the present perfect continuous: has/have + been + verb-ing. So 'has been living'. Simple present 'lives' only suits habits with no 'since' clause, e.g. 'She lives in Shimla.'","tag":"tense","deck":"english"},{"q":"The university will conduct exams after COVID-19 cases ___.","options":["will decline","decline","declined","declining"],"answer":1,"explain":"After time conjunctions (after, when, until, if, as soon as), use the PRESENT tense even when the main clause is future. The main clause already carries the future ('will conduct'), so 'will decline' repeats it wrongly. Correct: '...after cases decline.'","tag":"tense","deck":"english"},{"q":"Synonym of SUSTAINED (losses sustained)?","options":["persisted","affiliated","rendered","intermittent"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Sustained' here means suffered/endured over time. 'Persisted' (continued to exist) is the closest match. 'Affiliated' means attached to an organisation, 'rendered' means provided/gave, and 'intermittent' (on-and-off) is nearly an antonym.","tag":"vocab","deck":"english"},{"q":"Spot the misspelt word: observatory / residents / strom / alerted","options":["observatory","residents","strom","alerted"],"answer":2,"explain":"'strom' is missing its 'o' — the word is STORM (s-t-o-r-m). The others are correct: observatory (a building for watching the sky), residents (people living somewhere), alerted (warned).","tag":"spelling","deck":"english"},{"q":"Power plants with zero ___ stock.","options":["buffer","reserve","preserve","conserve"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Reserve stock' is the standard collocation — a reserve is something stored for emergencies. 'Buffer stock' exists too (for smoothing supply), but in the phrase 'zero ___ stock' for emergencies, 'reserve' is the expected answer. Preserve/conserve are verbs (protect/save), not nouns for stored goods.","tag":"collocation","deck":"english"},{"q":"Need a ___ policy framework.","options":["coherent","ludicrous","spurious","absurd"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Coherent' means logical, consistent and well-organised — what a policy framework must be. The other three all mean illogical in different ways: ludicrous (laughably absurd), spurious (false though seeming true), absurd (wildly unreasonable).","tag":"vocab","deck":"english"},{"q":"He was accused ___ theft.","options":["of","for","with","about"],"answer":0,"explain":"The fixed preposition with 'accused' is OF when naming the crime: 'accused of theft'. ('Accused for' or 'with' are common Indian-English errors.) Note the related pattern: 'charged WITH a crime' — accused→of, charged→with.","tag":"preposition","deck":"english"},{"q":"'Only pens are copies' means?","options":["All pens are copies","All copies are pens","No pen is a copy","Some pens are not copies"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Only A are B' REVERSES the All-statement: it means All B are A (every copy is a pen, but non-pens can never be copies). So 'all pens are copies' is false — pens need not be copies. Draw it: Copies circle fully inside Pens circle.","tag":"syllogism","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"A ≥ B > C = D. Is A > D?","options":["Yes","No","Cannot say","Only if E is large"],"answer":0,"explain":"Collapse the chain from A to D: A ≥ B (at least as big), B > C (strictly bigger), C = D (equal). ≥ combined with > gives strict >: A > D definitely. Rule: one strict link anywhere makes the whole chain strict; '=' links are neutral.","tag":"inequality","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Opposite-letter code (A↔Z). Opposite of M?","options":["L","M","N","O"],"answer":2,"explain":"Opposite pairs sum to 27 (A=1+Z=26, B=2+Y=25...). M=13 needs 14=N. Shortcut: opposites are mirror positions — M (13th from start) ↔ N (13th from end).","tag":"coding","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Facing north, turn 90° right then 180° left. Facing?","options":["East","West","North","South"],"answer":1,"explain":"Track step by step: North →(90° right)→ East →(180° left)→ West. A 180° turn always lands exactly opposite, so from East you face West. Draw a small compass for multi-turn Qs.","tag":"direction","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"A, C, F, J, O, ? Next?","options":["S","T","U","V"],"answer":2,"explain":"Gaps between letters grow: A→C (+2), C→F (+3), F→J (+4), J→O (+5), so next is +6: O+6=U. Arithmetic-gap series are the commonest alpha patterns — always compute gaps first.","tag":"series","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"40 students. Ravi 12th from left. From right?","options":["27th","28th","29th","Cannot say"],"answer":2,"explain":"Formula: rank from other side = total − given rank + 1 = 40−12+1 = 29. The +1 corrects double-counting Ravi himself. Check: 12+29−1=40 ✓.","tag":"ranking","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Walk 4m east, 3m north. Distance from start?","options":["5m","6m","7m","12m"],"answer":0,"explain":"East and north legs form a right angle: distance = √(4²+3²) = √25 = 5m (the 3-4-5 triple). Never add legs (7m) — displacement is the straight line, computed by Pythagoras.","tag":"direction","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"A is B's brother, B is C's mother. A is C's?","options":["Father","Uncle","Brother","Son"],"answer":1,"explain":"B is C's mother, and A is B's brother → A is the mother's brother = maternal uncle. Chain method: replace B with 'C's mother' → 'A is (C's mother)'s brother' → uncle.","tag":"blood","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Boxes X,Y,Z stacked. X above Y, Z below Y. Top?","options":["X","Y","Z","Cannot say"],"answer":0,"explain":"Y is the middle reference: X is above it, Z below it → top-to-bottom order X, Y, Z. Top = X. Pick one item as anchor and hang every clue off it.","tag":"puzzle","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Still:stream=6:1, upstream 20 km/h. Time for 126 km downstream?","options":["4.2h","4.5h","5h","5.5h"],"answer":1,"explain":"Let still=6k, stream=k. Upstream = still − stream = 5k = 20 → k=4. So still=24, stream=4, downstream = 24+4 = 28 km/h. Time = 126/28 = 4.5 hours. Remember: upstream subtracts, downstream adds.","tag":"boat","deck":"quant"},{"q":"Series: 2, 96, 16, 768, 128, ?","options":["1536","3072","6144","12288"],"answer":2,"explain":"Alternate operations: ×48, then ÷6, repeating. 2×48=96, 96÷6=16, 16×48=768, 768÷6=128, so 128×48=6144. When a series jumps up-down-up, always test alternating patterns.","tag":"series","deck":"quant"},{"q":"?×72/100 = 118 − 80% of 125. Find ?","options":["18","25","32","36"],"answer":1,"explain":"Simplify the right side first: 80% of 125 = 100, so RHS = 118−100 = 18. Then ? × 0.72 = 18 → ? = 18/0.72 = 25. Doing RHS first avoids messy decimals.","tag":"percent","deck":"quant"},{"q":"(m−16) = 32/(m−12). m?","options":["20, 8","16, 12","18, 10","24, 4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cross-multiply first: (m−16)(m−12)=32 → m²−28m+192−32=0 → m²−28m+160=0. Factor: need pair multiplying to 160, summing to 28 → 20 and 8. Always clear fractions before factoring.","tag":"quadratic","deck":"quant"},{"q":"Square perimeter 124m. Area?","options":["900","961","1024","1089"],"answer":1,"explain":"Side = perimeter/4 = 31. Area = 31² = 961. Memorize squares around 30: 29²=841, 30²=900, 31²=961, 32²=1024 — they recur constantly.","tag":"mensuration","deck":"quant"},{"q":"8% rent + 12% groceries, then 25% of the rest on transport. Total spent?","options":["32%","36%","40%","45%"],"answer":2,"explain":"After rent+groceries, 80% remains. Transport = 25% OF that remainder = 0.25×80 = 20% of salary (not 25%!). Total = 8+12+20 = 40%. Trap: applying the second % to salary instead of the remainder.","tag":"percent","deck":"quant"},{"q":"31% of 1001 ≈ ?","options":["300","310","330","341"],"answer":1,"explain":"Round to friendly numbers: ≈30% of 1000 = 300, plus ~1% of 1000 (=10) → 310. Approximation Qs reward rounding first, not exact computation.","tag":"approx","deck":"quant"},{"q":"Series: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30, ?","options":["36","40","42","44"],"answer":2,"explain":"First differences: +4, +6, +8, +10 — rising by 2 each time, so next difference is +12: 30+12=42. These are pronic numbers n(n+1): 1×2, 2×3, 3×4… 6×7=42.","tag":"series","deck":"quant"},{"q":"Rs.10000 at 10% CI for 2 years. Amount?","options":["11000","12000","12100","12150"],"answer":2,"explain":"Amount = P(1+r)ⁿ = 10000×1.1² = 10000×1.21 = 12100. (Interest alone would be 2100 — read whether the Q asks amount or interest; here it's amount.)","tag":"si-ci","deck":"quant"}]},{"id":"mains-mock-1","title":"Mains Mock 1","desc":"GA + Computer Knowledge weighted","track":"mains","questions":[{"q":"New India Assurance HQ + founded?","options":["Mumbai, 1919","Delhi, 1956","Kolkata, 1972","Chennai, 1938"],"answer":0,"explain":"New India Assurance — Mumbai HQ, founded 1919 by Dorabji Tata; India's largest public-sector general insurer. Contrast: LIC (life, 1956, Mumbai), GIC (reinsurance, 1972).","tag":"static","deck":"ga"},{"q":"PMFBY farmer premium — Kharif?","options":["1%","1.5%","2%","5%"],"answer":2,"explain":"PM Fasal Bima: Kharif 2%, Rabi 1.5%, commercial/horticultural 5% — government pays the balance. Mnemonic: 2-1.5-5 (Kharif-Rabi-commercial).","tag":"scheme","deck":"ga"},{"q":"Ayushman PM-JAY cover?","options":["Rs.1L","Rs.2L","Rs.5L per family/year","Rs.10L"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rs.5 lakh per family per year, cashless, for secondary + tertiary hospitalization — the world's largest health cover scheme, run by the National Health Authority. Launched 2018 from Ranchi.","tag":"scheme","deck":"ga"},{"q":"India's sole reinsurer?","options":["LIC","GIC Re","AIC","ECGC"],"answer":1,"explain":"GIC Re (General Insurance Corp, Mumbai) is the designated Indian reinsurer — it insures the insurers. LIC does life; AIC does crop (primary, not reinsurance); ECGC covers export credit.","tag":"insurance","deck":"ga"},{"q":"Insurance FDI limit (since 2021)?","options":["26%","49%","74%","100%"],"answer":2,"explain":"Raised from 49% to 74% in 2021 to pull foreign capital into insurers. Timeline: 26 (2000) → 49 (2015) → 74 (2021).","tag":"current","deck":"ga"},{"q":"MUDRA Shishu limit?","options":["Rs.50,000","Rs.5L","Rs.10L","Rs.20L"],"answer":0,"explain":"Shishu ≤50k (infant enterprise), Kishore 50k–5L, Tarun 5L–10L (plus Tarun Plus to 20L now). Shishu=smallest, matching the name (shishu=baby).","tag":"scheme","deck":"ga"},{"q":"LIC established in?","options":["1938","1956","1972","1991"],"answer":1,"explain":"Life Insurance Corporation of India came up in 1956 (Mumbai HQ) by nationalizing 245 insurers. Contrast GIC (general/reinsurance, 1972) to avoid the swap trap. 1938 is the old Insurance Act year - a deliberate distractor.","tag":"static","deck":"ga"},{"q":"First-generation computers used?","options":["Transistors","Vacuum tubes","ICs","VLSI chips"],"answer":1,"explain":"Gen 1 (1940s-50s, e.g. ENIAC/UNIVAC) ran on bulky, hot vacuum tubes. Then came transistors (G2), integrated circuits (G3), VLSI microprocessors (G4), and AI/parallel computing (G5). Order by shrinking size: tube → transistor → IC → chip.","tag":"history","deck":"ck"},{"q":"Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y do?","options":["Cut/Paste","Undo/Redo","Find/Replace","Save/Print"],"answer":1,"explain":"Z = undo last action, Y (or Ctrl+Shift+Z) = redo it. Neighbourhood: X/C/V cut-copy-paste, S save, P print, F find, H replace, F7 spelling. These six shortcuts cover most CK shortcut Qs.","tag":"shortcuts","deck":"ck"},{"q":"F5 in PowerPoint?","options":["Save","Slideshow from start","Spellcheck","New slide"],"answer":1,"explain":"F5 starts the slideshow from slide 1; Shift+F5 starts from the CURRENT slide (handy while editing). F7 is spelling, F12 is Save As. Don't confuse with browser F5 (refresh).","tag":"ppt","deck":"ck"},{"q":"HTTPS default port?","options":["80","443","53","25"],"answer":1,"explain":"HTTP=80, HTTPS=443, DNS=53, SMTP=25, FTP=21. Secure variants just add a port: the S in HTTPS buys TLS encryption and the padlock. Memorize this five-row table cold.","tag":"ports","deck":"ck"},{"q":"ALU + CU = ?","options":["RAM","CPU","ROM","Cache"],"answer":1,"explain":"The CPU is exactly Arithmetic-Logic Unit (does the math) + Control Unit (directs traffic) plus registers. RAM/ROM/cache are MEMORY — a different subsystem. ALU+CU=CPU is the single most-asked architecture one-liner.","tag":"hardware","deck":"ck"},{"q":"MICR is used on?","options":["Wi-Fi routers","Bank cheques","Fingerprints","Barcodes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Magnetic Ink Character Recognition reads the special magnetic numbers at a cheque's bottom for fast clearing (the 9-digit MICR code). Barcodes are optical (supermarkets); fingerprints are biometric.","tag":"io","deck":"ck"},{"q":"RAM vs ROM?","options":["Both volatile","Volatile vs non-volatile","Both permanent","Same speed"],"answer":1,"explain":"RAM is working memory — fast but VOLATILE (wiped on power-off). ROM is NON-volatile firmware (holds BIOS/boot code). That's why unsaved work dies in a power cut but the PC still boots.","tag":"memory","deck":"ck"},{"q":"Yesterday I met my friend who is now a doctor and ___ musician. Find the error.","options":["Yesterday I met","my friend who","is now a doctor","and a musician"],"answer":3,"explain":"Rule: when two nouns describe the SAME person and are joined by 'and', the article comes only before the first noun. Here the friend is one person who is both doctor and musician, so the second 'a' is wrong. Correct sentence: '...is now a doctor and musician.' If they were two different people, both articles would stay.","tag":"article","deck":"english"},{"q":"Choose the correct sentence.","options":["Despite of several disputes, they stayed.","Despite several disputes, they stayed.","Despite of the fact they stayed.","Although of disputes they stayed."],"answer":1,"explain":"'Despite' is already a complete preposition and never takes 'of' — the error 'despite of' is a classic trap. Its cousin 'in spite' DOES need 'of' ('in spite of disputes'). 'Although' is a conjunction, so 'although of' is never correct.","tag":"preposition","deck":"english"},{"q":"...a number of emerging ___.","options":["economy","economies","economical","economize"],"answer":1,"explain":"'A number of' always takes a PLURAL noun — it means 'several'. Contrast with 'the number of', which takes a singular verb ('The number of cases IS rising'). Also, only the noun 'economies' fits; 'economical' is an adjective.","tag":"grammar","deck":"english"},{"q":"Antonym of CONSTANT?","options":["fitful","perpetual","prevalent","entrust"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Constant' = steady, unchanging. 'Fitful' = irregular, starting and stopping — the direct opposite. Watch out: 'perpetual' (never-ending) is a SYNONYM of constant, and 'prevalent' means widespread, which is unrelated.","tag":"vocab","deck":"english"},{"q":"All pens are books. Some books are chairs. Valid conclusion?","options":["Some pens are chairs (definite)","Either 'some pens are chairs' or 'no pen is chair'","All pens are chairs","No conclusion possible"],"answer":1,"explain":"All + Some yields NO definite conclusion (the middle term 'books' isn't distributed to link pens to chairs). But 'Some pens are chairs' and 'No pen is a chair' are complementary (same subject-predicate, one Some one No) — one must be true. Hence either-or.","tag":"syllogism","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"'Only a few Perk is Kitkat' implies?","options":["Some are + some are not (both definite)","Only 'some are'","Only 'some are not'","Nothing definite"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Only a few' is special: it guarantees BOTH 'Some Perk are Kitkat' AND 'Some Perk are NOT Kitkat' as definite conclusions. It behaves like Some + Some-not combined — a favourite trap because test-takers treat it as plain 'some'.","tag":"syllogism","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"CAT → DBU. DOG → ?","options":["EPH","DPH","EOH","CNF"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each letter shifts +1: C→D, A→B, T→U. Apply to DOG: D→E, O→P, G→H = EPH. First check constant shifts (+1/+2) before trying complex patterns.","tag":"coding","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"'She is my grandfather's only son's daughter.' She is?","options":["Sister","Mother","Aunt","Cousin"],"answer":0,"explain":"Work inside-out: grandfather's only son = my father. Father's daughter = my sister (or self if the speaker is female — with 'she', it's sister). Inside-out decoding prevents the classic mistake of jumping to aunt.","tag":"blood","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Rs.4800 for 2y at 20% SI. Interest reinvested 2y at 10% CI. Interest from Y?","options":["Rs.384","Rs.403.2","Rs.472.4","Rs.503.8"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1 — SI from X: 4800 × 20% × 2 = Rs.1920. Step 2 — this 1920 becomes principal for Y: amount = 1920 × 1.1 × 1.1 = Rs.2323.2. Step 3 — CI interest = amount − principal = 2323.2 − 1920 = Rs.403.2. Trap: 384 is 20% of 1920 (one year's interest), not two years compounded.","tag":"si-ci","deck":"quant"},{"q":"Train A: pole 16s, 600m platform 36s. Speed?","options":["25 m/s","30 m/s","35 m/s","40 m/s"],"answer":1,"explain":"The extra 20 seconds (36−16) cover exactly the 600m platform, so speed = 600/20 = 30 m/s. Train length = 30×16 = 480m. Shortcut for these Qs: speed = platform length ÷ (platform time − pole time).","tag":"train","deck":"quant"},{"q":"Series: 3, 29, 127, 345, ? (odd cubes +2)","options":["511","731","999","1333"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each term is an odd cube plus 2: 1³+2=3, 3³+2=29, 5³+2=127, 7³+2=345, so 9³+2=729+2=731. Clue: numbers just above perfect cubes (127, 345) scream 'cube ± constant'.","tag":"series","deck":"quant"}]},{"id":"mains-mock-2","title":"Mains Mock 2","desc":"GA + CK + IT flavour","track":"mains","questions":[{"q":"IRDAI headquarters?","options":["Mumbai","Delhi","Hyderabad","Chennai"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India sits in Hyderabad (set up 1999). Trap options: RBI and SEBI are in Mumbai; PFRDA in Delhi. Regulator-HQ pairs are favourite static Qs.","tag":"regulator","deck":"ga"},{"q":"PMSBY cover + premium?","options":["Rs.2L, ~Rs.20/yr","Rs.2L, ~Rs.436/yr","Rs.5L, free","Rs.1L, Rs.100/yr"],"answer":0,"explain":"Suraksha = ACCIDENT cover Rs.2L at ~Rs.20/year, age 18–70. Don't confuse with PMJJBY (LIFE cover Rs.2L at ~Rs.436, age 18–50). Pair them: Suraksha-20-accident vs Jeevan-436-life.","tag":"scheme","deck":"ga"},{"q":"DICGC cover per depositor per bank?","options":["Rs.1L","Rs.3L","Rs.5L","Rs.10L"],"answer":2,"explain":"Deposit Insurance (RBI subsidiary) covers Rs.5 lakh per depositor per bank — raised from Rs.1L in 2020 after the PMC crisis. Covers all commercial + cooperative banks.","tag":"banking","deck":"ga"},{"q":"Third-party motor insurance is?","options":["Optional","Mandatory under MV Act","Only for trucks","Being phased out"],"answer":1,"explain":"Compulsory under the Motor Vehicles Act: every vehicle must cover third-party injury/damage. It never covers your OWN damage — that needs a comprehensive/own-damage add-on.","tag":"motor","deck":"ga"},{"q":"Bima Sugam is?","options":["Rural bundled product","Online insurance marketplace","Agent training force","Claim form"],"answer":1,"explain":"IRDAI's trio: Bima SUGAM = online marketplace (compare/buy like Amazon), Bima VISTAR = rural bundled product, Bima VAHAK = women-led distribution force. Part of 'Insurance for all by 2047'.","tag":"current","deck":"ga"},{"q":"Census 2011 literacy?","options":["65%","70%","74.04%","80%"],"answer":2,"explain":"74.04% literacy, sex ratio 943, density 382, population 121 crore. Cluster to memorize: 121cr–74%–943–382. (Next census delayed — check current affairs.)","tag":"static","deck":"ga"},{"q":"(1011)₂ in decimal?","options":["9","11","13","15"],"answer":1,"explain":"Weight each bit right-to-left as 1,2,4,8: 1×8 + 0×4 + 1×2 + 1×1 = 8+0+2+1 = 11. Always expand positional values instead of guessing.","tag":"binary","deck":"ck"},{"q":"$A$1 in Excel is?","options":["Fully relative","Absolute reference","Row-fixed only","Invalid"],"answer":1,"explain":"$ locks: $A$1 locks both (absolute — never shifts when copied), A$1 locks only the row, $A1 only the column, A1 fully relative. Test: dragging $A$1 down keeps pointing at A1.","tag":"excel","deck":"ck"},{"q":"SMTP vs POP3?","options":["Both receive mail","Send vs receive","Both send mail","Chat protocols"],"answer":1,"explain":"SMTP (port 587/25) SENDS mail out; POP3 (110) or IMAP (143) RECEIVE it. Memory hook: S=Send. IMAP syncs across devices; POP3 downloads then usually deletes from server.","tag":"email","deck":"ck"},{"q":"Virus vs worm?","options":["Same thing","Virus needs host+action; worm self-spreads","Worm needs a host","Neither spreads"],"answer":1,"explain":"A virus attaches to a host file and needs you to run it; a worm is standalone and crawls networks by itself (e.g. WannaCry). Trojan = disguised as legit software; ransomware encrypts for money; spyware steals silently.","tag":"security","deck":"ck"},{"q":"1 GB equals?","options":["1000 MB","1024 MB","1024 KB","100 MB"],"answer":1,"explain":"Memory uses binary: 1 KB=1024 B, 1 MB=1024 KB, 1 GB=1024 MB (2^10 each step). Telecom/storage marketing sometimes uses 1000, but exams expect 1024. 1024 KB would be just 1 MB.","tag":"memory","deck":"ck"},{"q":"UPI vs IFSC?","options":["Both branch codes","Instant payments vs branch ID","Card types","Loan schemes"],"answer":1,"explain":"UPI (by NPCI) = instant mobile payments via VPA. IFSC = 11-character branch identifier (4 bank code + 0 + 6 branch) used in NEFT/RTGS/IMPS transfers. One moves money, one addresses the branch.","tag":"banking-tech","deck":"ck"},{"q":"She ___ in hilly areas since her childhood.","options":["lives","has been living","lived","will live"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Since + a starting point in the past' (her childhood) with an action continuing to now always needs the present perfect continuous: has/have + been + verb-ing. So 'has been living'. Simple present 'lives' only suits habits with no 'since' clause, e.g. 'She lives in Shimla.'","tag":"tense","deck":"english"},{"q":"The university will conduct exams after COVID-19 cases ___.","options":["will decline","decline","declined","declining"],"answer":1,"explain":"After time conjunctions (after, when, until, if, as soon as), use the PRESENT tense even when the main clause is future. The main clause already carries the future ('will conduct'), so 'will decline' repeats it wrongly. Correct: '...after cases decline.'","tag":"tense","deck":"english"},{"q":"Synonym of SUSTAINED (losses sustained)?","options":["persisted","affiliated","rendered","intermittent"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Sustained' here means suffered/endured over time. 'Persisted' (continued to exist) is the closest match. 'Affiliated' means attached to an organisation, 'rendered' means provided/gave, and 'intermittent' (on-and-off) is nearly an antonym.","tag":"vocab","deck":"english"},{"q":"'Only pens are copies' means?","options":["All pens are copies","All copies are pens","No pen is a copy","Some pens are not copies"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Only A are B' REVERSES the All-statement: it means All B are A (every copy is a pen, but non-pens can never be copies). So 'all pens are copies' is false — pens need not be copies. Draw it: Copies circle fully inside Pens circle.","tag":"syllogism","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"A ≥ B > C = D. Is A > D?","options":["Yes","No","Cannot say","Only if E is large"],"answer":0,"explain":"Collapse the chain from A to D: A ≥ B (at least as big), B > C (strictly bigger), C = D (equal). ≥ combined with > gives strict >: A > D definitely. Rule: one strict link anywhere makes the whole chain strict; '=' links are neutral.","tag":"inequality","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Opposite-letter code (A↔Z). Opposite of M?","options":["L","M","N","O"],"answer":2,"explain":"Opposite pairs sum to 27 (A=1+Z=26, B=2+Y=25...). M=13 needs 14=N. Shortcut: opposites are mirror positions — M (13th from start) ↔ N (13th from end).","tag":"coding","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Facing north, turn 90° right then 180° left. Facing?","options":["East","West","North","South"],"answer":1,"explain":"Track step by step: North →(90° right)→ East →(180° left)→ West. A 180° turn always lands exactly opposite, so from East you face West. Draw a small compass for multi-turn Qs.","tag":"direction","deck":"reasoning"},{"q":"Which is NOT an OS function?","options":["Memory management","Scheduling","Word processing","File management"],"answer":2,"explain":"The OS manages hardware resources: processes (scheduling), memory, files, devices, security. Word processing is an APPLICATION running on top (like MS Word on Windows). Test: if users install it separately, it's not the OS.","tag":"os","deck":"it"},{"q":"Minimum average waiting-time scheduling?","options":["FCFS","SJF","Round Robin","LIFO"],"answer":1,"explain":"Shortest Job First always picks the quickest pending job, minimizing average wait (provably optimal for known burst times). FCFS suffers the convoy effect (long job blocks all); Round Robin is fair but slower on average. Preemptive SJF = SRTF.","tag":"os","deck":"it"},{"q":"BCNF vs 3NF?","options":["BCNF weaker","BCNF implies 3NF","Unrelated","3NF implies BCNF"],"answer":1,"explain":"Normal forms nest strictly: 1NF ⊂ 2NF ⊂ 3NF ⊂ BCNF. Every BCNF table is automatically in 3NF (every determinant is a superkey), but a 3NF table can still violate BCNF. Stronger form ⇒ all weaker ones hold.","tag":"dbms","deck":"it"},{"q":"WHERE vs HAVING?","options":["Same thing","Rows-before vs groups-after grouping","Only HAVING filters","Neither uses aggregates"],"answer":1,"explain":"WHERE filters individual ROWS before grouping (can't use aggregates); HAVING filters GROUPS after GROUP BY (works with COUNT/SUM/AVG). Example: WHERE year=2024 ... GROUP BY branch HAVING COUNT(*)>5.","tag":"sql","deck":"it"},{"q":"Reliable claim upload: TCP or UDP?","options":["UDP","TCP","ICMP","ARP"],"answer":1,"explain":"TCP is connection-oriented: sequence numbers, ACKs, retransmission and ordering guarantee every byte arrives — exactly what document uploads need. UDP skips all that for speed (live video, DNS, gaming) and may lose data. Reliability ⇒ TCP.","tag":"networks","deck":"it"},{"q":"Bulk-data encryption: AES or RSA?","options":["RSA","AES","SHA-256","MD5"],"answer":1,"explain":"AES (symmetric, one shared key) is ~1000× faster — used for the actual data. RSA (asymmetric, key pair) only exchanges keys and signs. SHA-256/MD5 are HASHES (one-way fingerprints), not encryption at all. Real systems are hybrid: RSA handshake, AES session (that's HTTPS).","tag":"crypto","deck":"it"}]}],"videos":[{"label":"Quant","query":"NICL AO quant simplification DI"},{"label":"Reasoning","query":"NICL AO reasoning puzzles seating"},{"label":"English","query":"NICL AO English comprehension error detection"},{"label":"Insurance GA","query":"IRDAI insurance awareness government schemes"},{"label":"Computers","query":"computer fundamentals MS Office IBPS"},{"label":"IT","query":"DBMS computer networks operating system gate"}],"guides":[{"id":"quant-tricks","title":"Quant tricks & number bank","track":"prelims","body":"# Quant Tricks — fast methods for every repeating template\n\n> Companion to `score-80-playbook.md`. Each trick: when to spot it → the move → 15-second example. Practice refs point into the app bank (Quant deck) and `quiz-1/2.md`.\n\n## 0. Number bank (memorize once, earn daily)\n\n- Squares 11–30: 121, 144, 169, 196, 225, 256, 289, 324, 361, 400, 441, 484, 529, 576, 625, 676, 729, 784, 841, 900\n- Cubes 2–12: 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729, 1000, 1331, 1728\n- Powers of 2 to 2^10: 2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256,512,1024\n- % ↔ fraction: 50=1/2, 25=1/4, 20=1/5, 12.5=1/8, 10=1/10, 16.66=1/6, 33.33=1/3, 14.28=1/7, 11.11=1/9, 9.09=1/11, 8.33=1/12, 6.25=1/16, 6.66=1/15\n- km/h→m/s ×5/18; m/s→km/h ×18/5\n\n## 1. Simplification / approximation\n\n- **Split %**: 48% of 650 = (50%−2%) = 325−13 = 312. Never multiply 0.48 directly.\n- **Round first**: 31% of 1001 ≈ 30% of 1000 + 1% ≈ 310. Approximation Qs punish exactness.\n- **BODMAS with division last**: 15×12÷4+8 → multiply first (180), divide (45), add (8) = 53.\n- **Conjugates**: 19×21 = (20−1)(20+1) = 399. a²−b² hunting.\n- **a³−b³ identity**: (0.7³−0.6³)÷(0.7²+0.6²+0.7×0.6) = 0.7−0.6 = 0.1. Bracket shape = cancel, don't expand.\n\n## 2. Number series (spot in 10s)\n\n1. Constant difference? → extend. 2. Constant ratio? → multiply. 3. ×n±k (×2+1)? 4. Squares/cubes ±k (numbers near 122, 344 scream cubes)? 5. n(n+1) pronic (2,6,12,20,30…)? 6. Alternating two patterns? 7. Differences of differences (second-order)?\n- Wrong-term: rebuild rule, the misfit pops out (…,32,63 → should be 64).\n\n## 3. Quadratics & equations\n\n- Factor hunt: x²−Sx+P → two numbers with product P, sum S (x²−24m+135: −15×−9=+135 and −15−9=−24 → roots 15,9).\n- Clear fractions FIRST: (m−16)=32/(m−12) → cross-multiply, then factor.\n- Compare roots, don't fully solve when asked \"relation\": sign of roots often suffices.\n- Linear pair (age/mixture style): one equation in x, e.g. 3x+10=2(x+10) → x=10.\n\n## 4. SI / CI\n\n- SI = linear: P·r·t. Doubling in n years → rate = 100/n (double in 5y = 20%).\n- CI amount = P(1+r)ⁿ. 2-yr effective at 10% = 21%; at 20% = 44%.\n- **CI−SI (2y) = P(r/100)²** — one step (4000,10% → 40).\n- Read the ask: amount vs interest (12100 vs 2100 trap).\n\n## 5. Time–Work–Pipes\n\n- Add rates: 1/a+1/b; together = ab/(a+b). Three workers: sum three fractions.\n- Remaining worker: 1/A = combined − other (1/6−1/10=1/15).\n- Man-days constant: men×days fixed (15×10 = 10×15).\n- Pair-sum trick: (P+Q)+(Q+R)+(R+P) rates = 2×all → halve, subtract to isolate.\n- Pipes: inlet +, outlet −. Negative net = empties.\n\n## 6. Speed–Time–Distance–Trains–Boats\n\n- Platform/pole: speed = platform ÷ (platform-time − pole-time) = 600/20 = 30.\n- Two bodies toward each other: ADD speeds; same direction: SUBTRACT.\n- Boat: downstream = still+stream; upstream = still−stream; still = avg of the two; stream = half their difference.\n-  advance-planning: convert units before anything (72 km/h → 20 m/s).\n\n## 7. Percentages / Profit / Discount / Ratio / Average / Age / Mixture\n\n- Reverse %: divide (20% of X=60 → X=300).\n- Successive +a% then −b%: net = a−b−ab/100 (+20/−20 → −4%).\n- Price↑r% hold spend: cut r/(100+r) (25% → 20%).\n- P&L on CP; discount on MP. CP-15=SP-12 → gain (15−12)/12=25%.\n- Join ratios via LCM of shared term (2:3 + 4:5 → 8:12:15).\n- Averages: totals first (5×27=135, minus 4×25=100 → excluded 35).\n- Ages: total now vs then differs by headcount×years (3 people, 5y → +15).\n- Partnership: profit ∝ capital×time (2×6 : 3×4 = 1:1).\n- Mixture: track the UNCHANGED component (milk 30L stays; total 40→50; 60%).\n- HCF: min exponents; LCM: max exponents; N = HCF×LCM/known.\n- Probability: count favourable ÷ total (die even = 3/6; two-dice sum-9 = 4/36).\n\n## 8. Mensuration 30-second kit\n\n- Square a²/4a · Rectangle lb/2(l+b) · Triangle ½bh · Circle πr²/2πr · Cube a³/6a² · Cuboid lbh/2(lb+bh+hl) · Cylinder πr²h/2πr(h+r)\n- Cuboid shortcut: V = √(product of 3 face areas) = √3600 = 60.\n- 7-14-22 triple with π=22/7 (r=7 → 154).\n\n## 9. DI set routine (15 marks, same 5 moves)\n\nFor each set, in order: ① totals ② differences ③ % vs 'than'-base ④ averages/ratios ⑤ price×qty. Never read options before computing the column totals — parts chain.\n\n## 10. Exam-hall checks (30 seconds, saves 2 marks)\n\n- Units match? (min vs hours, m vs km)\n- % base = 'than'-party? Amount vs interest? Length vs length+platform?\n- Option trap scan: round-number and off-by-one options are usually wrong.\n- Mark-and-move at 90 seconds; no sunk cost.\n"},{"id":"score-80","title":"Score-80 playbook (cutoff math + attempt plan)","track":"prelims","body":"# Score-80 Playbook — NICL AO Prelims (100 marks, −0.25)\n\n> Built from YOUR papers: `papers/NICL-PYQ-2024-Prelims.md` (100Q), `papers/NICL-PYQ-2017-English.md` (100Q), `papers/NIACL-PYQ-2013-English.md` (100Q mixed), `papers/archive/*` (NIACL 2016–2023). Counts below are real, not generic advice.\n\n## 1. The math of 80\n\nScore = Correct − 0.25 × Wrong. Two viable routes:\n\n| Route | Attempt | Right | Wrong | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A — sniper | 85 | 82 | 3 | **81.25** |\n| B — volume | 92 | 86 | 6 | **84.5** |\n\nRoute A needs ~96% accuracy; Route B ~93%. **Take A**: 15 skips is plenty, and every blind guess costs 0.25. Golden rule: **attempt only when ≥70% sure; skip everything else on first pass, return if time remains.**\n\nSectional floors are qualifying (not merit) but mandatory — never sacrifice a section. Minimum safety line per section: **Eng 18/30, Reas 20/35, Quant 20/35**, then push total to 80.\n\n## 2. Topic weightage (NICL 2024 Prelims — your main paper)\n\n**English 30** — RC fasting 10 (Q1–10) · Para-jumble 5 (Q11–15) · Error detection 5 (Q16–20) · Sentence improvement 4 (Q22–25) · Fillers/blank 5 (Q26–30)\n**Reasoning 35** — Box puzzle 5 (Q31–35) · Blood + word-formation 4 (Q36–39) · Fruit seating 5 (Q40–44) · Coding 5 (Q46–50) · Floor-flat 5 (Q51–55) · Syllogism + odd-one 5 (Q56–60) · Circle seating 5 (Q61–65)\n**Quant 35** — Quadratics 5 (Q66–70) · DI Atlas/Dictionary 5 (Q71–75) · Simplification 5 (Q76–80) · DI items 5 (Q81–85) · DI class boys/girls 5 (Q86–90) · Arithmetic 10 (Q91–100: train, work, square, SI, partnership, average, age, mixture, percent, spend)\n\nCross-paper check: 2017 English = RC + cloze + vocab (same English DNA). 2013 = error/improvement/narration + coding/analogy + arithmetic. 2012 Reasoning = series/coding/puzzles/ranking. **Puzzles+seating ≈ 20/35 Reasoning every cycle. DI ≈ 15/35 Quant. Grammar+vocab ≈ 20/30 English.**\n\n## 3. Repeating patterns (drill these first — they repeat verbatim in style)\n\n1. **Syllogism 'Only / Only a few'** (2024 Q56–58; bank: reasoning 30 cards) — Only A are B reverses; only-a-few = some + some-not. ~5 free marks.\n2. **Coding by common-word isolation** (2024 Q46–50; 2013 Q44 pattern) — overlap sentences, cancel common words. ~5 marks, zero theory.\n3. **Quadratics factor-first** (2024 Q66–70) — cross-multiply, factor, compare. 5 marks in ~6 min.\n4. **Simplification/approximation** (2024 Q78–80; 2013 Q88 identity) — BODMAS + rounding + a³−b³ identity. 5 marks, fastest in paper.\n5. **Error-spotting fixed pairs** (2024 Q16–25; 2013 Q5/Q14) — pronoun case, has/have, to+base verb, Despite-without-of. Same 6 rules yearly.\n6. **Fillers = collocations** (2024 Q26–30; 2017 cloze blanks 2,3,5,6,8) — reserve stock, in a bid to, plenary, prohibit, endeavour. Read them aloud; ear decides.\n7. **DI four operations** (2024 Q71–90) — difference, % vs 'than'-base, average, ratio, price×qty. Same 5 moves × 3 sets = 15 marks.\n8. **Seating with a twist** (2024 Q40–44 T–K swap; floor-flat interchange) — redraw, never overwrite. Second question in a set is always the twist.\n9. **Blood-relation inside-out** (2024 Q36–38; 2013 Q35 cousin; 2012 Q6 mother) — decode innermost phrase first.\n10. **Arithmetic greatest-hits** (2024 Q91–100) — train/(platform), work-share, SI-rate-change, average-via-total, mixture-track-milk, %−of−remainder. Same 10 templates as 2013 Q80–99.\n\n## 4. The 80-mark attempt plan (60 min, sectional 20′ each)\n\n**English (20′) — target 26/30.** Order: fillers 5 (4′) → error/improvement 9 (7′) → RC 10 (8′) → para-jumble last (1′, attempt only sure ones). Skip jumbles freely — worst ROI.\n**Reasoning (20′) — target 26/35.** Order: syllogism+odd-one 5 (4′) → coding 5 (4′) → blood/word/rank 4 (3′) → ONE full puzzle set, your best type (8′) → second set only if ahead. Never start a third puzzle.\n**Quant (20′) — target 28/35.** Order: simplification 5 (3′) → quadratics 5 (4′) → easiest DI set 5 (5′) → arithmetic pick-5, easiest first (6′) → remaining DI with leftover time. Skip mensuration outliers on sight.\n\n26 + 26 + 28 = 80. Attempt ≈ 86, accuracy bar ≈ 95%.\n\n## 5. What to skip (buying accuracy)\n\n- 3rd puzzle set in Reasoning; number-pair oddities; dice-figure Qs without diagrams.\n- Para-jumbles beyond 2 attempts; RC inference options with 'always/never' absolutes.\n- Arithmetic with 3+ unknowns; probability beyond single-die; cube-painting counts.\n- Any Q unreadable in 20 seconds — mark, move, return.\n\n## 6. 7-day drill (fits your 2h/day)\n\n- **D1:** Syllogism 30Q + coding 20Q (bank: reasoning). Target 95%.\n- **D2:** Quadratics 25Q + simplification 25Q (bank: quant). Target 100% on simpl.\n- **D3:** One 2024 DI set ×3 timed + error-detection 20Q.\n- **D4:** Full 2024 Prelims timed (60′). Log every miss by pattern # above.\n- **D5:** Fix worst 2 patterns (40Q) + fillers/cloze 30Q.\n- **D6:** Full 2017 English timed (English stamina) + 1 puzzle set.\n- **D7:** Rest morning. Formula + collocation + port/code sheet review only.\n- Daily: 20 app questions (streak alive) + 10 flashcards.\n\n## 7. Mock scoreboard (track this, not just marks)\n\nAfter each mock record: Attempted / Right / Wrong / Score / Time left per section / Pattern of each miss (#1–10). You are ready when two consecutive mocks hit **78+ with ≤4 wrong** — exam-day adrenaline covers the rest.\n"}],"plan":"# NICL AO 2026 — Study Plan (2h weekday + 5h weekend)\n\nPrelims 17 Nov (Eng 30 + Reas 35 + Quant 35, 60 min, −0.25, sectional cutoffs).\nMains 22 Dec (250 marks + Descriptive Essay/Précis/Comprehension 30).\n\n## Weekly rhythm\n- **Mon**: Quant drill 70′ + English 40′ + formulas 10′\n- **Tue**: Reasoning 70′ + GA/IT 40′ + headlines 10′\n- **Wed**: Quant 70′ + Computer Knowledge 40′ + shortcuts 10′\n- **Thu**: Reasoning 70′ + English 40′ + vocab 10′\n- **Fri**: Weakest topic 60′ + GA/CK or Technical 40′ + flashcards 20′\n- **Sat**: Full mock 60′ + 2h analysis (log errors by topic) + PYQ paper\n- **Sun**: New concept 2h + sectional test 2h + flashcards 1h\n\n## Phase A — Prelims (5 weeks)\n1. Basics: simplification + series, inequality + syllogism, error detection.\n2. Arithmetic (SI-CI, boat, train) + seating + DBMS/SQL (IT).\n3. DI sets + 1 puzzle/day (8-min cap) + Networks.\n4. Speed only: 20-min sectional tests, no new theory.\n5. Taper: one sectional/day + flashcards. Sleep before the exam.\n\nExam order: English easy → Reasoning easy → Quant simplification → puzzles/DI.\nTarget: 65–75 attempts at 85% accuracy.\n\n## Phase B — Mains\n- Mon Quant-Adv DI • Tue high puzzles • Wed GA capsules • Thu English • Fri Technical/CK.\n- Sun: typed descriptive (alternate Essay / Précis+Comprehension).\n- 4 full Mains mocks. Order: GA → English → CK → Quant → Reasoning → Technical → Descriptive.\n","resources":"# NICL AO 2026 — Resources\n\n## In this repo (`papers/`)\n- NICL-AO-2024-Prelims.pdf — only full memory-based paper (4 Mar 2024). Solve first.\n- NICL-AO-2026-Notification.pdf — official pattern, syllabus, centres.\n\n## Papers (free account needed)\n- Testbook PYP section — NICL 2017 Prelims ×3, 2013, 2012: testbook.com/nicl-ao/previous-year-papers\n- Adda247 free practice pack + IT Officer mocks.\n\n## Free mocks\n- ixambee.com/free-mock-tests/nicl-ao\n- Adda247 NICL AO test series (free pack).\n\n## Official handouts\n- nationalinsurance.nic.co.in → Recruitment at admit-card time (Phase-I/II sample Qs).\n\n## Proxies (same Prelims skeleton)\n- UIIC AO papers, LIC AAO Prelims, IBPS PO Prelims.\n- IT Technical proxy: IBPS SO IT Professional Knowledge PYQs.\n\n## Current affairs (Mains GA)\n- Daily: BankersAdda / Adda247 GK updates. Monthly capsules Jun–Nov 2026.\n- Budget + Economic Survey summary. IRDAI: Bima Sugam / Vistar / Vahak.\n"}]};
